WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.280
- Welcome to the Huberman Lab Podcast,

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:04.029
where we discuss science

00:00:04.029 --> 00:00:06.129
and science-based tools for everyday life.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:10.320
I'm Andrew Huberman,

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.380
and I'm a professor of
neurobiology and ophthalmology

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.240
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.580
Today my guest is Dr. Nolan Williams.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.620
Dr. Williams is a medical doctor

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.290
and professor of psychiatry
and behavioral sciences

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:24.690
at Stanford University School of Medicine.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:27.000
His laboratory and clinic
focus on depression

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.890
and other mood disorders.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:30.600
They focus specifically on the use

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.910
of transcranial magnetic stimulation,

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.100
which is a brain stimulation technique

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.670
that can either activate or
quiet specific brain circuits,

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.560
as well as circuits within the body,

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.950
in order to treat depression
and other mood disorders.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.590
Other laboratories and clinics use TMS.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:49.590
What sets apart the work of
Nolan Williams and colleagues is

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:52.440
that they combine TMS
with other treatments,

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.600
and some of those treatments
are among the more cutting edge

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.220
that you've probably
heard about these days,

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:01.220
including ibogaine, psilocybin,
MDMA, cannabis, DMT,

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.580
and other drugs that at this
point in time are experimental

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.260
in terms of clinical trials,

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.480
but that at least the
preliminary data show

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.790
hold great promise for the
treatment of depression

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:13.260
and other mood disorders.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.450
In the course of my
discussion with Dr. Williams,

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:17.670
we covered things such as the history

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:20.130
of each of these drugs,
how they came to be,

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:22.710
and their current status in
terms of their clinical use

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:23.880
and legality.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:25.800
We also talk about their safety profiles

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.110
both in children and in adults,

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.750
and we talk about what the
future of psychedelic research

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:33.420
and clinical use really looks like.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.510
For instance, we discuss
how a number of laboratories

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.760
and clinics are modifying psychedelics

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.370
to remove some of their
hallucinogenic properties

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:43.440
while maintaining some
of their antidepressant

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.210
or anti trauma properties.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.040
You'll also learn about
some fascinating research

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.370
in Dr. Williams' laboratory
focused on ketamine,

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.740
which is a drug that is
increasingly being used

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:53.880
to treat depression.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.680
And contrary to common belief,

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.800
the effects of ketamine in
terms of relieving depression

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.250
may not actually arise from
its dissociative effects.

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:03.690
One thing that you'll find extraordinary

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.240
about Dr. Williams is
that not only does he have

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.090
vast knowledge of the various
treatments for depression,

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.940
but that he and his laboratory
are really combining

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:13.590
these treatments in the most potent way.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.390
That is, combining psychedelic treatments

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:16.770
with brain-machine interface,

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:18.240
or combining brain-machine interface

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.100
with particular learning protocols.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.290
That is, neuroplasticity protocols,

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:25.050
which can directly change
the brain in specific ways.

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.690
So today you're going to
learn a tremendous amount

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:29.910
about the neural circuitry
underlying depression,

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:31.350
as well as positive moods.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:33.090
You'll also learn about
all the various drugs

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:34.110
that I described,

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:36.540
and you're really going to
learn about the current status

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.090
and future of the treatment
of mood disorders.

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:41.580
Today, you'll also learn about
a number of ongoing studies

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:43.440
in Dr. Williams' laboratory.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.420
I should mention that they
are recruiting subjects

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:46.650
for these studies.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:48.000
If you go to BSL,

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.830
which stands for Brain
Stimulation Laboratory,

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.320
so that's bsl.stanford.edu,

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.150
you have the opportunity to apply

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:55.440
for one of these clinical trials

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.230
for the treatment of depression
and other mood disorders.

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:00.600
I confess that the conversation
with Dr. Williams was,

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.670
for me, one of the more stimulating

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:05.250
and informative
conversations I've ever had

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:06.540
about psychedelics,

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:08.910
which is simply to say that his breadth

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:12.420
and depth of knowledge on
that topic is incredible,

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:13.950
and his breadth and depth of knowledge

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:15.690
in terms of the underlying brain science

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:17.190
and how it can all be combined

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:20.550
with clinical applications
is also extraordinary.

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:22.230
I'm sure that by the
end of today's episode,

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:23.063
you're going to come away

00:03:23.063 --> 00:03:25.410
with a tremendous amount of
knowledge about the clinical

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:27.630
and non-clinical uses of those substances,

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:29.700
and you're going to understand a lot more

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.190
about how the healthy
and diseased brain work.

