conditions like cancer,

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:57.300
we actually have a fairly
limited number of tools

00:09:57.300 --> 00:09:58.593
to approach depression.

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:00.960
And yet number of tools

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:03.240
and the potency of those tools is growing.

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:05.190
So if you could educate us on depression,

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:06.360
I would really appreciate it.

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:07.193
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:10:07.193 --> 00:10:09.693
So depression is a condition that,

00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:14.280
it has a lot of manifestations, you know.

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:16.650
So you can have kind of a depression

00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:18.540
that's primarily loss of interest.

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:21.360
You can have folks who feel very anxious

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:22.650
and they're kind of overactive.

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:25.290
You can have people who don't
have any anxiety at all,

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:26.460
and they're very underactive

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:29.154
and they have low
motivation to do anything.

00:10:29.154 --> 00:10:30.960
You know, so you have this
huge range of symptoms

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:33.510
that are in that umbrella of depression.

00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:35.734
And some of our work is to actually work

00:10:35.734 --> 00:10:37.950
with folks like Conor Liston and Cornell,

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:42.330
and try to actually get biotypes
based off of neuroimaging

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:45.600
to see if we can kind of parse
out the different depression

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:49.200
kind of presentations,
and see that clinically,

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:51.240
and also see that in the brain.

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:55.440
Depression is the most
disabling condition worldwide.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:56.970
What's interesting about depression is

00:10:56.970 --> 00:11:00.960
it's both a risk factor
for other illnesses,

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:04.380
and it makes other medical and
psychiatric illnesses worse,

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:05.476
right?

00:11:05.476 --> 00:11:09.030
So recently the American Heart
Association added depression

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:11.430
as the fourth major risk factor

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:13.200
for coronary artery disease, right?

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:16.620
So alongside the risk
factors that we know,

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:19.560
hypertension, high blood
pressure, hyperlipidemia,

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:24.150
high cholesterol, and diabetes,
you know, high blood sugar,

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:26.850
those three have been on
the list for a long time

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:29.250
and depression ended up
being added to the list

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:30.600
as the fourth one.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:33.090
And you know, really interesting, right?

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:35.670
So in addition to taking medications

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:37.710
to address those other three risk factors,

00:11:37.710 --> 00:11:38.820
we really have to be thinking

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:42.030
about how do you treat
folks with depression

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:45.780
to reduce the risk of having
a heart attack in the future?

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:48.750
And, you know, there's some
of that's being worked on now,

00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:50.520
but we don't have a complete solution

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:52.920
to thinking about that at this time.

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:55.170
And then the other thing
that's interesting is

00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:57.515
once you have a heart attack,

00:11:57.515 --> 00:12:00.690
in the individuals that end
up having a heart attack,

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:04.230
the risk of having depression
after the heart attack is

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:07.110
higher than the normal population, right?

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:10.770
And so a lot of what we're
doing in the lab actually is

00:12:10.770 --> 00:12:13.350
measuring kind of brain heart connections.

00:12:13.350 --> 00:12:16.230
And we can actually, with
transcranial magnetic stimulation,

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:17.580
a form of brain stimulation,

00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:19.350
we can actually decelerate the heart rate

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:22.350
and capture that heart rate deceleration

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:24.060
over the mood regulatory regions.

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:27.120
And so actually a direct
probe of that connection.

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:28.410
So it's interesting.

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:32.160
And so, you know, as you
said a second ago, you know,

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:33.660
it's a very disabling condition.

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:36.590
Moderate depression's about as disabling

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:39.480
as having a heart attack,
acutely having a heart attack.

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:41.550
Severe depression is disabling,

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:44.490
is having cancer without
treatment, you know,

00:12:44.490 --> 00:12:47.040
and dying from a cancer without treatment.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:51.240
And so, you know, it's
kind of underappreciated

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:54.180
just how disabling
depression is in that way.

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:55.980
And I think important

00:12:55.980 --> 00:12:59.790
as stigma is consistently
kind of being reduced

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:02.820
over the years for mental illnesses,

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:06.090
then the idea that we can start
really putting more funding

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:08.580
and putting more focus at
the federal level, you know,

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:10.680
private foundation level, whatever it is,

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:11.730
at a given university

00:13:11.730 --> 00:13:15.420
to thinking about developing treatments.

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:16.950
We've been very interested

00:13:16.950 --> 00:13:20.910
in a very particular
clinical set of problems

00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:25.910
around the most severe and
the most high acuity settings

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:29.430
that folks with depression
end up being in.

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:32.310
And that's in, you
know, emergency settings

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:34.290
where they go into inpatient units.

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:37.200
And you know, in the rest of medicine,

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:38.820
if it's talking about heart attacks,

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:42.090
if I start having chest pain right now

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:44.430
and you bring me to a
primary care doctor's office,

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:46.140
they're going to have a
certain number of tests

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:47.250
and treatments, right?

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:50.550
But very limited cuz it's
an outpatient facility.

00:13:50.550 --> 00:13:51.997
If you bring me to the
emergency room after that,

00:13:51.997 --> 00:13:54.360
there are more tests and more treatments.

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:57.720
If you put me in the
ICU or in the cath lab

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:00.840
where they do invasive
procedures to the heart,

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:02.850
there are more tests and more treatments.

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:07.850
In psychiatry, as we elevate
the acuity of an individual,

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:10.200
you go from being just
depressed to being depressed

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:12.120
and now thinking about ending your life,

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:16.500
the number of treatments
actually go down on average.

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:17.940
I mean, in some scenarios, they go up,

00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:21.090
but on average they go down
and there are no tests, right?

00:14:21.090 --> 00:14:24.510
And so we've been very focused
on that particular problem.

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:27.390
Somebody that maybe was doing, you know,

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:30.060
fairly okay with a pretty
moderate depression

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:32.010
and then their depression gets worse

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:35.040
and then they end up in
an emergency setting.

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:38.310
And the field really
hasn't developed a way

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:42.030
of consistently being
able to treat that problem

00:14:42.030 --> 00:14:42.900
and folks end up getting

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:45.000
the same standard oral antidepressants

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:47.640
that they've been getting outpatient.

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:51.180
And I came to this because
I, you know, dual trained

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:52.680
as a neurologist and psychiatrist,

00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:55.650
went back and forth between
neurology and psychiatry,

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:58.050
saw that in neurology we
have all of these ways

00:14:58.050 --> 00:15:01.080
of treating acute brain based problems

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:04.050
and really wanted to
emulate that in psychiatry

00:15:04.050 --> 00:15:05.340
and find ways to develop

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:08.823
and engineer new, you know,
brain based solutions.

00:15:09.780 --> 00:15:11.070
- There's a lot to unpack there.

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:13.470
One thing that you said is,

00:15:13.470 --> 00:15:16.800
I'd like to focus on a bit
more because I think we hear

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:19.110
that the brain and the
heart are connected,

00:15:19.110 --> 00:15:23.340
but you described, I believe,
a direct relationship

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:26.310
between areas of the brain
associated with emotion

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:28.170
and heart rate.

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:29.312
- [Nolan] Yes.

00:15:29.312 --> 00:15:32.070
- And that makes perfect
logical sense to me.

00:15:32.070 --> 00:15:35.130
But I think at the same time,

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:37.830
many people out there probably
think of the relationship

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:41.460
between the heart and
the mind as kind of woo

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:43.440
or kind of a soft biology.

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:46.020
But here you're talking about
an actual physical connection.

00:15:46.020 --> 00:15:46.853
- [Nolan] Yep.

00:15:46.853 --> 00:15:49.360
- Between, what area of the brain is it?

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:52.950
- The first place where the
stimulation goes is called

00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:54.513
the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex.

00:15:54.513 --> 00:15:57.060
It's kind of the sense of control,

00:15:57.060 --> 00:15:58.380
kind of governor of the brain.

00:15:58.380 --> 00:16:02.610
And then what we know is
that when you use a magnet,

00:16:02.610 --> 00:16:04.530
use kind of what we call Faraday's Law,

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:07.860
this idea of using a magnetic pulse

00:16:07.860 --> 00:16:10.590
to induce an electrical current

00:16:10.590 --> 00:16:12.900
and electrically conducting substances.

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:14.700
So in this case, brain tissue,

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:17.970
but not skull or scalp
or any of that, or hair.

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:20.220
You avoid all that, just the brain tissue.

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:24.750
Then you have a direct
depolarization of cortical neurons,

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:27.390
you know, the surface
of the brain's neurons,

00:16:27.390 --> 00:16:29.760
in this dorsolateral prefrontal.

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:33.420
And if you do that in the
actual scanner, which we can do,

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:35.910
you can see that that distributes down

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:39.930
into the anterior cingulate and
the insula and the amygdala.

00:16:39.930 --> 00:16:42.960
And ultimately the tract
goes into something called

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:45.150
the nucleus tractus solitarius

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:47.820
and ultimately into the
vagus nerve into the heart.

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:52.820
So the heart very consistently
seems to be the end organ

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:56.010
of the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex.

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:58.800
If you measure heart rate in standard ways

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:00.597
that cardiologists measure heart rate

00:17:00.597 --> 00:17:03.420
and you stimulate over
this left dorsolateral,

00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:05.220
you get a deceleration of the heart rate

00:17:05.220 --> 00:17:08.430
and it's very time locked
to the stimulation.

00:17:08.430 --> 00:17:10.830
So it's a two second train of stimulation.

00:17:10.830 --> 00:17:12.960
At one second, you see the deceleration,

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:15.060
it goes down about 10 beats per minute,

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:17.070
and then it'll drift back
up and there's a break

00:17:17.070 --> 00:17:18.690
for eight seconds on the stimulation.

00:17:18.690 --> 00:17:21.390
Drifts back up and the
stimulation goes back in

00:17:21.390 --> 00:17:22.920
and then the heart rate goes back down.

00:17:22.920 --> 00:17:25.350
And so you see the
heart rate just do this,

00:17:25.350 --> 00:17:27.510
10 beats per minute every train.

00:17:27.510 --> 00:17:30.810
And so we know, and if you
do that over visual cortex,

00:17:30.810 --> 00:17:31.643
you don't get that,

00:17:31.643 --> 00:17:33.780
or motor cortex, you don't
get any of those findings.

00:17:33.780 --> 00:17:38.070
It's really specific to
this kind of control region

00:17:38.070 --> 00:17:38.903
of the brain.

00:17:38.903 --> 00:17:41.610
And so, yeah, it seems to, you know,

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:43.680
it's our work, other folks' work.

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:46.830
Martin Arens in Europe, the Netherlands,

00:17:46.830 --> 00:17:48.990
work showing the same connections.

00:17:48.990 --> 00:17:51.840
I think it's been replicated
like four or five times.

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:56.220
- So you mentioned left
dorsolateral prefrontal cortex.

00:17:56.220 --> 00:17:58.290
Anytime I hear about
lateralization of function,

00:17:58.290 --> 00:18:01.410
I get particularly curious

00:18:01.410 --> 00:18:06.360
because obviously we have
two mirror symmetric sides

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:07.320
of the brain.

00:18:07.320 --> 00:18:09.630
There are, you know,
rare exceptions to this,

00:18:09.630 --> 00:18:12.120
like the pineal and things of that sort

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:15.933
that there is only one pineal.

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:17.670
What is special

00:18:17.670 --> 00:18:20.040
about the left dorsolateral
prefrontal cortex?

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:21.780
Does this have anything
to do with handedness,

00:18:21.780 --> 00:18:22.980
right hand or left hand?

00:18:22.980 --> 00:18:24.900
Because we know right-hand
and left-handedness has a lot

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:27.450
to do with lateralization
of function for language,

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:30.540
a topic for another time.

00:18:30.540 --> 00:18:32.460
But why do you think

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:35.550
that left dorsolateral prefrontal
cortex would be connected

00:18:35.550 --> 00:18:37.950
to the heart in this way?

00:18:37.950 --> 00:18:38.783
- Yeah.

00:18:38.783 --> 00:18:43.560
Yeah, I think, so left
dorsolateral is thought

00:18:43.560 --> 00:18:46.350
to be the side that when you excite it,

00:18:46.350 --> 00:18:49.800
when you kind of do
excitatory stimulation,

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:51.390
potentiating sort of stimulation,

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:54.810
that you can reduce depressive symptoms.

00:18:54.810 --> 00:18:57.690
And a guy by the name of Mike
Fox at Harvard demonstrated

00:18:57.690 --> 00:19:02.217
that if you have strokes in
the brain that cause depression

00:19:02.217 --> 00:19:04.590
and you put them on the human connectome,

00:19:04.590 --> 00:19:06.960
a hundred, you know, thousand patient map,

00:19:06.960 --> 00:19:07.920
and you ask the question

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:10.200
what they're all
functionally connected to?

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:11.940
Left dorsolateral.

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:15.720
If you take lesions that
cause mania in individuals

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:17.820
and you put those all on
the human connectome map

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:21.840
and ask what the one common
area they're all connected to,

00:19:21.840 --> 00:19:24.150
it's the right dorsolateral.

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:28.920
And so there seems to be
a hemispheric, you know,

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:31.890
balancing of mood between
these two brain regions.

00:19:31.890 --> 00:19:34.650
And we know this from an
experimental standpoint too,

00:19:34.650 --> 00:19:37.200
because you can take
individuals with depression

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:41.007
and you can excite the left
or you can inhibit the right

00:19:41.007 --> 00:19:43.500
and they're both antidepressant.

00:19:43.500 --> 00:19:45.900
You can excite the right

00:19:45.900 --> 00:19:48.690
and that's anti-manic in some studies.

00:19:48.690 --> 00:19:52.350
And so this idea that there
is this hemispheric balancing

00:19:52.350 --> 00:19:54.630
of mood is quite interesting, right?

00:19:54.630 --> 00:19:56.550
- It's incredibly interesting.

00:19:56.550 --> 00:19:58.230
And just so people know,

00:19:58.230 --> 00:20:01.230
if you're curious what the connectome is,

00:20:01.230 --> 00:20:03.510
connectome is a term that
was built out of this notion

00:20:03.510 --> 00:20:07.080
of genomes being large collections

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:09.000
of sequencing and mapping of genes.

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:11.130
They're proteomes of proteins,

00:20:11.130 --> 00:20:13.980
of connectomes is so-called connectomics,

00:20:13.980 --> 00:20:15.420
of connections between neurons.

00:20:15.420 --> 00:20:18.598
So the Human Connectome Project is ongoing

00:20:18.598 --> 00:20:21.210
and I find that incredible

00:20:21.210 --> 00:20:22.590
that within the Connectome Project,

00:20:22.590 --> 00:20:24.270
they can identify these regularities

00:20:24.270 --> 00:20:27.420
of right versus left
dorsolateral prefrontal cortex.

00:20:27.420 --> 00:20:32.420
Especially since I've looked
at a fair number of brains

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:35.433
from humans, certainly
not as many as you have.

00:20:36.270 --> 00:20:39.840
And if you look at the
architecture, the layers,

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:42.660
the cell types, and
even the neurochemicals

00:20:42.660 --> 00:20:45.600
of which cells are expressing,
say, dopamine or serotonin

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:46.980
or receiving input from areas

00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:48.810
that make dopamine or serotonin,

00:20:48.810 --> 00:20:51.420
they don't look that different
on the right and left side.

00:20:51.420 --> 00:20:54.840
And yet here we're talking
about a kind of an accelerator

00:20:54.840 --> 00:20:59.160
and a brake, if you will,
on depression and mania

00:20:59.160 --> 00:21:01.920
using what, at least by my eye

00:21:01.920 --> 00:21:04.230
and I think other people's
eye look to be basically

00:21:04.230 --> 00:21:06.300
the same set of of bits.

00:21:06.300 --> 00:21:08.370
The same parts list, more or less.

00:21:08.370 --> 00:21:11.730
So what gives these properties

00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:14.130
to the right and left
dorsolateral prefrontal cortex?

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:16.110
Is it the inputs they receive?

00:21:16.110 --> 00:21:17.850
Is this something that we
learn during development

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:19.680
or do you think that
we come into the world

00:21:19.680 --> 00:21:22.260
with these hemispheric biases?

00:21:22.260 --> 00:21:24.000
- Yeah, it's a great question.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:26.010
And you know, it hasn't been worked out,

00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:28.380
which your original question was around,

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:31.800
in a left handed individual,
which as you know,

00:21:31.800 --> 00:21:35.550
25% of those folks end up
having a right brain dominance

00:21:35.550 --> 00:21:38.910
or 1% of right-handed people
have a right brain dominance

00:21:38.910 --> 00:21:40.140
if it's flipped, right?

00:21:40.140 --> 00:21:44.070
And unfortunately that
study still hasn't been done

00:21:44.070 --> 00:21:46.410
at the level, 'cause that would
be probably pretty helpful

00:21:46.410 --> 00:21:48.330
for teasing some of this out.

00:21:48.330 --> 00:21:52.590
But, you know, it's still
being sorted out, right?

00:21:52.590 --> 00:21:56.970
We know enough to know
this phenomenon exists

00:21:56.970 --> 00:22:00.540
because we can use TMS as a probe

00:22:00.540 --> 00:22:03.240
and do these sorts of manipulations.

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:05.610
But to my knowledge,

00:22:05.610 --> 00:22:08.070
there hasn't been anybody that's
gotten so interested in it

00:22:08.070 --> 00:22:12.360
that they've been able to get
a mechanism of why that is.

00:22:12.360 --> 00:22:16.320
But, you know, it's
kind of empirically true

00:22:16.320 --> 00:22:19.170
in the sense that you can push
and pull on those systems,

00:22:19.170 --> 00:22:22.830
or in the case of strokes that folks have,

00:22:22.830 --> 00:22:25.560
and then you kind of get their
brains and their brain images

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:28.020
and look at where the strokes landed,

00:22:28.020 --> 00:22:30.420
those kind of causal
bits of information point

00:22:30.420 --> 00:22:33.390
to this asymmetry.

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:34.223
- Interesting.

00:22:34.223 --> 00:22:37.350
Well, in that case, going
with what we do know,

00:22:37.350 --> 00:22:39.870
that stimulation of
dorsolateral prefrontal cortex

00:22:39.870 --> 00:22:41.280
slows the heart rate down,

00:22:41.280 --> 00:22:43.230
transiently, but it slows it down,

00:22:43.230 --> 00:22:46.860
and seems to alleviate at least
some symptoms of depression,

00:22:46.860 --> 00:22:50.400
leads me to the question of
why would that be the case?

00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:53.520
Does it tell us anything
fundamental about depression

00:22:53.520 --> 00:22:56.550
that anxiety is inherent to depression?

00:22:56.550 --> 00:22:58.800
I think a faster heart rate is, you know,

00:22:58.800 --> 00:23:02.193
part and parcel with anxiety.

00:23:03.930 --> 00:23:07.080
In my laboratory, we've studied fear a bit

00:23:07.080 --> 00:23:08.280
in animals and in humans,

00:23:08.280 --> 00:23:10.920
and we often observe bradycardia

00:23:10.920 --> 00:23:14.250
where somebody or an animal
is afraid of something

00:23:14.250 --> 00:23:15.990
and rather than the
heart rate speeding up,

00:23:15.990 --> 00:23:17.550
it actually slows down,

00:23:17.550 --> 00:23:21.540
something that most people
don't think about or recognize.

00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:24.937
But given that stimulation

00:23:24.937 --> 00:23:27.150
of dorsolateral prefrontal
cortex slows the heart rate down

00:23:27.150 --> 00:23:29.580
and can alleviate depressive symptoms

00:23:29.580 --> 00:23:31.770
and that there are other
ways to slow the heart down,

00:23:31.770 --> 00:23:32.940
I have two questions.

00:23:32.940 --> 00:23:34.440
What do you think this tells us

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:37.770
about the basic architecture of depression

00:23:37.770 --> 00:23:40.530
and its physiology at
the level of the heart?

00:23:40.530 --> 00:23:43.380
And does the circuit run in
the opposite direction too?

00:23:43.380 --> 00:23:45.480
If one were to have or find other ways

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:48.630
to slow the heart rate down,
say with a beta blocker,

00:23:48.630 --> 00:23:50.550
does that help alleviate depression?

00:23:50.550 --> 00:23:51.690
- Yeah, no, that's a great question.

00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:55.410
So I'll answer the second question first.

00:23:55.410 --> 00:23:59.430
So we know that in the
ongoing trials of this,

00:23:59.430 --> 00:24:01.323
if you stimulate in the vagus nerve,

00:24:02.678 --> 00:24:04.500
in an implanted vagus nerve stimulator,

00:24:04.500 --> 00:24:07.527
you can actually, you know, have this,

00:24:07.527 --> 00:24:12.527
the afferent parts of the
vagus project ultimately up

00:24:12.540 --> 00:24:14.880
to the DLPFC through the cingulate

00:24:14.880 --> 00:24:17.130
through these anterior
insula, so that same,

00:24:17.130 --> 00:24:18.900
that obviously the same tract, right?

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:22.200
And you can stimulate there
and alleviate depression,

00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:24.240
which seems very unusual, right?

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:27.150
You're stimulating a cranial
nerve down on the neck.

00:24:27.150 --> 00:24:29.370
But if you can get up into the brain,

00:24:29.370 --> 00:24:31.830
you actually can improve
depressive symptoms.

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:33.990
And so, you know, more evidence

00:24:33.990 --> 00:24:37.830
that this is a kind of a
whole track and system.

00:24:37.830 --> 00:24:40.620
And if you stimulate
in part of that system,

00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:44.100
it appears that you can improve mood.

00:24:44.100 --> 00:24:45.540
- And what if I were somebody

00:24:45.540 --> 00:24:48.720
who did not have a stimulating
electrode in my vagus nerve

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:51.450
and I was dealing with minor depression

00:24:51.450 --> 00:24:54.480
and I decided I wanted to
take some other approach

00:24:54.480 --> 00:24:56.520
to slow my heart rate down by the vagus?

00:24:56.520 --> 00:24:59.640
For instance, exhale emphasized breathing

00:24:59.640 --> 00:25:04.260
or deliberately slow cadence
breathing, things of that sort.

00:25:04.260 --> 00:25:06.150
Is there any evidence that
behavioral interventions

00:25:06.150 --> 00:25:10.410
of those kinds can alleviate depression

00:25:10.410 --> 00:25:13.020
or some symptoms of depression?

00:25:13.020 --> 00:25:15.360
And is there any evidence
that it does indeed feed back

00:25:15.360 --> 00:25:17.100
to the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:19.140
to achieve some of that alleviation?

00:25:19.140 --> 00:25:19.973
- Absolutely, yeah.

00:25:19.973 --> 00:25:24.450
So there's a number of studies
implicating the dorsolateral

00:25:24.450 --> 00:25:27.660
in, say, you know, meditation,

00:25:27.660 --> 00:25:29.340
mindfulness, that sort of thing.

00:25:29.340 --> 00:25:31.530
And they're small studies,

00:25:31.530 --> 00:25:34.650
but pretty well designed
studies suggesting

00:25:34.650 --> 00:25:37.740
that behavioral interventions
in mild depression

00:25:37.740 --> 00:25:39.600
actually work quite well.

00:25:39.600 --> 00:25:43.140
There seems to be a volitional
threshold for depression

00:25:43.140 --> 00:25:46.020
where at some point you start losing,

00:25:46.020 --> 00:25:49.140
you go from being
completely in total volition

00:25:49.140 --> 00:25:51.240
to having kind of semi-volition.

00:25:51.240 --> 00:25:52.410
You have thoughts

00:25:52.410 --> 00:25:54.090
that you really have a
hard time controlling

00:25:54.090 --> 00:25:55.208
and that sort of thing.

00:25:55.208 --> 00:25:57.150
And when you go through that threshold,

00:25:57.150 --> 00:26:00.270
at some point it gets harder and harder

00:26:00.270 --> 00:26:03.210
for those sorts of things
to kind of kick in and work.

00:26:03.210 --> 00:26:05.430
And the extreme form of
that is catatonia, right?

00:26:05.430 --> 00:26:08.520
Where people in a very
severe form of depression get

00:26:08.520 --> 00:26:10.017
kind of stuck motorically, right?

00:26:10.017 --> 00:26:12.490
And they obviously can't,
they have no control

00:26:14.190 --> 00:26:16.260
or very limited control.

00:26:16.260 --> 00:26:19.200
And so, you know, I
think there's a threshold

00:26:19.200 --> 00:26:21.870
in which these sorts of
interventions will work.

00:26:21.870 --> 00:26:24.900
Exercise seems to really
be a good treatment

00:26:24.900 --> 00:26:27.480
for mild depression and it may work

00:26:27.480 --> 00:26:29.220
through the mechanism
you're describing, right?

00:26:29.220 --> 00:26:31.500
As we all know, you know,

00:26:31.500 --> 00:26:33.700
athletes hold a lower resting heart rate

00:26:34.650 --> 00:26:37.920
than folks that aren't, you
know, if you were an athlete,

00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:41.280
you had a lower resting heart
rate, you stopped exercising,

00:26:41.280 --> 00:26:42.900
and a couple years later
your resting heart rate

00:26:42.900 --> 00:26:44.640
in many cases goes up, right?

00:26:44.640 --> 00:26:48.360
And so maybe that's part of the process.

00:26:48.360 --> 00:26:52.980
I'm not aware of any
studies specifically looking

00:26:52.980 --> 00:26:57.300
at dorsolateral prefrontal physiology

00:26:57.300 --> 00:26:59.880
pre-post exercise, but it
would be a great study.

00:26:59.880 --> 00:27:02.640
I think that would be really
helpful to understanding this,

00:27:02.640 --> 00:27:06.120
especially if you had a
correlation of changes

00:27:06.120 --> 00:27:07.050
in kind of lowering of, say,

00:27:07.050 --> 00:27:09.243
heart rate with mood improvements.

00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:13.260
There's been a lot of work
with heart rate variability

00:27:13.260 --> 00:27:17.010
and depression and, you know,

00:27:17.010 --> 00:27:20.430
studies kind of point towards it.

00:27:20.430 --> 00:27:24.360
Not every study is positive for this,

00:27:24.360 --> 00:27:27.750
but quite a few studies say basically

00:27:27.750 --> 00:27:32.130
that lower heart rate
variability is associated

00:27:32.130 --> 00:27:34.860
with, you know, moderate
to severe depression.

00:27:34.860 --> 00:27:37.020
And that may be part of that mechanism

00:27:37.020 --> 00:27:40.170
of that heart brain risk.

00:27:40.170 --> 00:27:42.240
- So I'm both intrigued
and a little bit perplexed

00:27:42.240 --> 00:27:45.063
by this relationship between
heart rate and depression.

00:27:45.900 --> 00:27:48.870
On the face of it, I would think
of depression as depressed.

00:27:48.870 --> 00:27:51.150
So lower heart rate might
make somebody more depressed.

00:27:51.150 --> 00:27:52.320
You even mentioned catatonia

00:27:52.320 --> 00:27:54.840
or somebody that just
doesn't seem motivated

00:27:54.840 --> 00:27:56.310
or excited to do anything.

00:27:56.310 --> 00:27:59.790
I think of mania as elevated
heart rate and being excited.

00:27:59.790 --> 00:28:02.850
On the other hand, I realize that anxiety,

00:28:02.850 --> 00:28:05.700
which you know, brings about ideas

00:28:05.700 --> 00:28:09.030
as elevated heart rate is
also built into depression.

00:28:09.030 --> 00:28:10.770
Which brings me back to
what you said earlier,

00:28:10.770 --> 00:28:12.690
which is that when we say depression,

00:28:12.690 --> 00:28:14.820
are we really talking about
four or five different?

00:28:14.820 --> 00:28:16.230
- Yeah, that's right.

00:28:16.230 --> 00:28:19.260
- Disorders, for lack of a better word.

00:28:19.260 --> 00:28:23.310
And for what percentage of
people that have depression

00:28:23.310 --> 00:28:28.310
does some approach to
reducing heart rate work?

00:28:28.350 --> 00:28:29.550
Whether or not it's stimulation

00:28:29.550 --> 00:28:32.940
of the left dorsolateral prefrontal cortex

00:28:32.940 --> 00:28:34.860
by way of transcranial
magnetic stimulation

00:28:34.860 --> 00:28:38.940
or by taking a beta blocker
or by stimulating the vagus.

00:28:38.940 --> 00:28:40.950
Can we throw out a number, a rough number?

00:28:40.950 --> 00:28:43.410
Does that help, 30%, 50%?

00:28:43.410 --> 00:28:45.390
How long lasting is that relief?

00:28:45.390 --> 00:28:46.848
- Yeah, and to be clear,

00:28:46.848 --> 00:28:50.310
the deceleration of the heart rate is

00:28:50.310 --> 00:28:53.010
in the moment when the
stimulation is happening,

00:28:53.010 --> 00:28:55.050
but it's not something

00:28:55.050 --> 00:28:57.870
that's necessarily maintained chronically.

00:28:57.870 --> 00:29:01.170
It's more of an indicator that
you're in the right network

00:29:01.170 --> 00:29:05.070
more than it appears
to be itself, you know,

00:29:05.070 --> 00:29:06.120
central to the mechanism.

00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:08.730
The heart rate variability piece may be,

00:29:08.730 --> 00:29:11.070
and there's some studies
that link the two,

00:29:11.070 --> 00:29:13.320
but the actual deceleration
seems to be much more

00:29:13.320 --> 00:29:16.050
of a marker that you're
in the right system.

00:29:16.050 --> 00:29:17.400
But you know, it very well could be

00:29:17.400 --> 00:29:19.020
that the heart rate system

00:29:19.020 --> 00:29:21.090
and the mood system just
sit next to each other

00:29:21.090 --> 00:29:23.100
and the stimulation hits both.

00:29:23.100 --> 00:29:26.490
If you look at how much of
the variance in the mood is

00:29:26.490 --> 00:29:28.470
explained by the heart rate deceleration,

00:29:28.470 --> 00:29:32.220
it's not a huge amount, right?

00:29:32.220 --> 00:29:35.580
So it only explains a small percentage.

00:29:35.580 --> 00:29:40.580
And so it's unlikely that
simply reducing the heart rate.

00:29:41.400 --> 00:29:43.260
And in fact, you know, for many years,

00:29:43.260 --> 00:29:45.030
propranolol and these sorts of drugs

00:29:45.030 --> 00:29:47.520
actually were implicating
causing depression.

00:29:47.520 --> 00:29:52.020
And so that's been kind of
debunked, but it's unlikely

00:29:52.020 --> 00:29:53.820
that simply decelerating
the heart rate's going to

00:29:53.820 --> 00:29:55.290
improve depression.

00:29:55.290 --> 00:29:58.830
But what it does tell you is
that if you're in that area

00:29:58.830 --> 00:30:00.720
that is the mood regulatory area,

00:30:00.720 --> 00:30:04.230
there's some parasympathetic
cortical kind of process

00:30:04.230 --> 00:30:07.560
that's going on that gets in
and causes this to happen.

00:30:07.560 --> 00:30:10.140
And it's, you know, it's
independent of mood.

00:30:10.140 --> 00:30:14.580
You can take a normal healthy
individual and you can do this

00:30:14.580 --> 00:30:17.490
and they're going to
decelerate their heart rate.

00:30:17.490 --> 00:30:18.450
- I'm so glad you mentioned

00:30:18.450 --> 00:30:19.950
the parasympathetic nervous system,

00:30:19.950 --> 00:30:21.150
which of course is the,

00:30:21.150 --> 00:30:22.800
most people think of
as the rest and digest

00:30:22.800 --> 00:30:25.650
or the kind of calming side of
the autonomic nervous system.

00:30:25.650 --> 00:30:27.330
As I'm hearing you say all of this,

00:30:27.330 --> 00:30:30.150
and in particular what you just told me,

00:30:30.150 --> 00:30:31.620
which is that it's not

00:30:31.620 --> 00:30:34.200
as if having a lower
heart rate protects you

00:30:34.200 --> 00:30:35.033
against depression

00:30:35.033 --> 00:30:37.470
or a higher heart rate is
associated with depression,

00:30:37.470 --> 00:30:39.900
although at the extremes
that might be true,

00:30:39.900 --> 00:30:43.020
but rather it's something
about the regulatory network,

00:30:43.020 --> 00:30:46.080
the ability to control
your own nervous system

00:30:46.080 --> 00:30:47.043
to some extent.

00:30:48.030 --> 00:30:50.100
And when I think about the
autonomic nervous system,

00:30:50.100 --> 00:30:51.900
I like to think about it as a seesaw

00:30:51.900 --> 00:30:53.520
of, you know, alertness and calmness,

00:30:53.520 --> 00:30:55.320
and when you're asleep
it's a lot of calmness,

00:30:55.320 --> 00:30:58.140
and when you're panicking it's
a lot of alertness to the...

00:30:58.140 --> 00:31:01.110
But that, and I don't think
this has ever been defined,

00:31:01.110 --> 00:31:03.660
and when I teach the medical students

00:31:03.660 --> 00:31:05.520
at Stanford neuroanatomy, my wish is

00:31:05.520 --> 00:31:08.460
that someday I'll be able
to explain what the hinge

00:31:08.460 --> 00:31:10.020
in that process would be, right?

00:31:10.020 --> 00:31:11.160
Not the ends of the seesaw.

00:31:11.160 --> 00:31:12.870
We know what the sympathetic
nervous system is

00:31:12.870 --> 00:31:15.450
and it's to wake us up and make us panic

00:31:15.450 --> 00:31:18.030
or make us feel nicely alert and calm.

00:31:18.030 --> 00:31:21.480
We know what puts someone
into sleep or a coma

00:31:21.480 --> 00:31:23.400
or makes them feel relaxed.

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:26.310
But what shifts from one side
of the seesaw to the other

00:31:26.310 --> 00:31:28.620
and the tightness of
that hinge seems to be

00:31:28.620 --> 00:31:30.060
what you're describing,

00:31:30.060 --> 00:31:32.760
that depression is sort
of a lack of control

00:31:32.760 --> 00:31:34.800
over inner state so
that when I'm stressed,

00:31:34.800 --> 00:31:35.910
I can't get myself out of it.

00:31:35.910 --> 00:31:38.820
But when I'm feeling completely
collapsed with exhaustion,

00:31:38.820 --> 00:31:40.740
I can't get out of bed and be motivated

00:31:40.740 --> 00:31:41.790
to do the very things

00:31:41.790 --> 00:31:43.290
that would help me get out of depression,

00:31:43.290 --> 00:31:46.890
like a workout or social
connection or eat a quality meal,

00:31:46.890 --> 00:31:48.060
these kinds of things.

00:31:48.060 --> 00:31:50.460
So this is perhaps the first time

00:31:50.460 --> 00:31:54.000
that I've ever heard
about a potential circuit

00:31:54.000 --> 00:31:56.010
for the hinge, as I'm referring to it.

00:31:56.010 --> 00:31:57.665
Does that make any sense at all?

00:31:57.665 --> 00:31:58.778
- [Nolan] Yeah, absolutely, absolutely.

00:31:58.778 --> 00:31:59.611
- Okay, I just want to make sure

00:31:59.611 --> 00:32:01.027
that I'm framing this
correctly in my mind.

00:32:01.027 --> 00:32:01.860
- Yeah, yeah, absolutely.

00:32:01.860 --> 00:32:02.693
And in some studies,

00:32:02.693 --> 00:32:05.220
if you do the same identical stimulation

00:32:05.220 --> 00:32:07.970
on the right dorsolateral,
you can get an acceleration.

00:32:09.120 --> 00:32:11.790
You know, just kind of
further confirming this idea

00:32:11.790 --> 00:32:13.290
of lateralization, right?

00:32:13.290 --> 00:32:17.010
That it appears that even the
prefrontal cortex, you know,

00:32:17.010 --> 00:32:20.940
cortical areas seem to be
lateralized in this way.

00:32:20.940 --> 00:32:23.823
And, you know, it's less,

00:32:24.690 --> 00:32:26.940
the right finding is more variable

00:32:26.940 --> 00:32:28.170
depending upon the study.

00:32:28.170 --> 00:32:30.898
The left's very consistent in this way.

00:32:30.898 --> 00:32:32.141
So...

00:32:32.141 --> 00:32:34.230
- So we've talked about
transcranial magnetic stimulation

00:32:34.230 --> 00:32:35.940
for getting into these networks

00:32:35.940 --> 00:32:38.549
and I also just want to take
a brief tangent and say,

00:32:38.549 --> 00:32:40.290
'cause I've heard you say this before,

00:32:40.290 --> 00:32:42.600
I think it's so vital what you're saying,

00:32:42.600 --> 00:32:46.080
that it's really not
about stimulation of areas

00:32:46.080 --> 00:32:48.030
or any specific brain area

00:32:48.030 --> 00:32:51.330
or vagus nerve being important per se.

00:32:51.330 --> 00:32:53.460
It's really about a network, a connection,

00:32:53.460 --> 00:32:54.990
a series of connections.

00:32:54.990 --> 00:32:57.150
I think that's really important
for people to understand

00:32:57.150 --> 00:33:00.300
and is kind of a new
emerging theme really.

00:33:00.300 --> 00:33:03.630
The other thing that to me
seems extremely important

00:33:03.630 --> 00:33:06.167
for us to consider is

00:33:06.167 --> 00:33:11.167
what are these lateral
prefrontal cortices doing?

00:33:11.460 --> 00:33:14.070
Are they involved, for
instance, in sensation,

00:33:14.070 --> 00:33:15.120
sensing the heart rate?

00:33:15.120 --> 00:33:17.790
Are they involved in
thinking and planning?

00:33:17.790 --> 00:33:19.830
And this gets down to
a very simple question

00:33:19.830 --> 00:33:21.690
that I know a lot of
people have, which is,

00:33:21.690 --> 00:33:24.420
can we talk ourselves out of depression?

00:33:24.420 --> 00:33:25.710
If it's mild.

00:33:25.710 --> 00:33:29.760
Can we talk ourselves into a manic state

00:33:29.760 --> 00:33:30.810
or an excited state,

00:33:30.810 --> 00:33:33.690
a positively excited state
that doesn't qualify as mania?

00:33:33.690 --> 00:33:35.220
You know, other areas of the brain,

00:33:35.220 --> 00:33:37.800
I think of is responsible for perception

00:33:37.800 --> 00:33:39.690
or for motor control.

00:33:39.690 --> 00:33:42.540
But here we are in this
mysterious frontal cortex area,

00:33:42.540 --> 00:33:45.420
which people say executive
function, planning, et cetera.

00:33:45.420 --> 00:33:47.580
Are we talking about thoughts?

00:33:47.580 --> 00:33:49.190
Are we talking about structured thoughts

00:33:49.190 --> 00:33:51.420
or are we talking about
dreamlike thoughts?

00:33:51.420 --> 00:33:55.530
What in the world is going
on in the prefrontal cortex?

00:33:55.530 --> 00:33:57.660
And here I spend my career in neuroscience

00:33:57.660 --> 00:34:01.860
and I still can't really
understand what it's doing

00:34:01.860 --> 00:34:03.630
and maybe it's doing 50 things.

00:34:03.630 --> 00:34:05.430
- Yeah, no, it's a great question.

00:34:05.430 --> 00:34:07.620
So, you know, to...

00:34:07.620 --> 00:34:10.440
So one of the studies
that we've been working on

00:34:10.440 --> 00:34:12.540
in addition to the depression
work is actually trying

00:34:12.540 --> 00:34:15.030
to change trait hypnotizability.

00:34:15.030 --> 00:34:17.520
So David Spiegel and I
have been working on this

00:34:17.520 --> 00:34:21.720
and you know, he's found and
published this 10 years ago

00:34:21.720 --> 00:34:25.830
that a different part of
the left dorsolateral is

00:34:25.830 --> 00:34:30.330
functionally connected with
the dorsal anterior cingulate

00:34:30.330 --> 00:34:33.180
with a lot of functional
connectivity in high hypnotizables

00:34:33.180 --> 00:34:35.730
and not much in low hypnotizables.

00:34:35.730 --> 00:34:38.790
And that's kind of a different sub-region

00:34:38.790 --> 00:34:40.470
within this bigger brain region

00:34:40.470 --> 00:34:43.320
we call left dorsolateral
prefrontal cortex

00:34:43.320 --> 00:34:45.270
than the part that seems to be important

00:34:45.270 --> 00:34:47.010
for regulating mood.

00:34:47.010 --> 00:34:52.010
And so the left dorsolateral
seems to have connections

00:34:52.200 --> 00:34:54.540
that are location specific

00:34:54.540 --> 00:34:58.110
within the overall kind
of named brain region

00:34:58.110 --> 00:35:00.570
that connect to various
parts of the cingulate

00:35:00.570 --> 00:35:02.103
and seem to regulate it.

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:04.101
Right?

00:35:04.101 --> 00:35:05.250
And so if you knock out

00:35:05.250 --> 00:35:07.440
the left dorsolateral prefrontal cortex

00:35:07.440 --> 00:35:10.800
and you have people do the
Stroop task, for instance,

00:35:10.800 --> 00:35:13.827
which is a task where you
have, it's a simple task,

00:35:13.827 --> 00:35:15.300
you probably know this.

00:35:15.300 --> 00:35:19.140
You have people name the color of words.

00:35:19.140 --> 00:35:24.140
And so if I look at one of the
cards that they'll show you,

00:35:25.050 --> 00:35:27.600
it'll have the word red in red

00:35:27.600 --> 00:35:30.660
and that's very easy and
that's called a congruent.

00:35:30.660 --> 00:35:35.660
And then the incongruent
is red in the color blue

00:35:36.030 --> 00:35:39.870
and you have to name,
you have to say the word,

00:35:39.870 --> 00:35:41.940
you don't name the color.

00:35:41.940 --> 00:35:43.770
- So you have to suppress a response.

00:35:43.770 --> 00:35:44.603
- Yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:35:44.603 --> 00:35:48.240
And so, I'm sorry, you name the color

00:35:48.240 --> 00:35:50.427
and you see the word
written in a different way.

00:35:50.427 --> 00:35:55.380
And so basically if you stimulate in a way

00:35:55.380 --> 00:35:57.960
that inhibits the left
dorsolateral prefrontal cortex

00:35:57.960 --> 00:35:58.890
or either one,

00:35:58.890 --> 00:36:02.460
you can actually knock out
the ability to do that well

00:36:02.460 --> 00:36:06.480
and it'll take longer for
people on the incongruent cards

00:36:06.480 --> 00:36:08.730
to be able to name it.

00:36:08.730 --> 00:36:11.700
And so they have a kind of a time delay

00:36:11.700 --> 00:36:14.730
that's greater than they had
before they got stimulated.

00:36:14.730 --> 00:36:17.940
So that's a part of the prefrontal cortex

00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:20.130
that's different than the
part of the prefrontal cortex

00:36:20.130 --> 00:36:22.410
that's involved in mood regulation.

00:36:22.410 --> 00:36:25.470
The nice thing about TMS
is that you can go through

00:36:25.470 --> 00:36:29.490
and you can find these areas
that are functionally defined

00:36:29.490 --> 00:36:32.010
through brain imaging
and you can perturb them

00:36:32.010 --> 00:36:33.720
and answer the question
you're talking about.

00:36:33.720 --> 00:36:36.900
How do I understand this
part of the prefrontal cortex

00:36:36.900 --> 00:36:39.060
and its function, this part?

00:36:39.060 --> 00:36:44.060
And so we were able to
stimulate in an inhibitory way

00:36:44.100 --> 00:36:47.430
within the left dorsolateral
prefrontal cortex

00:36:47.430 --> 00:36:52.110
that's involved with this sort
of cognitive control area.

00:36:52.110 --> 00:36:55.170
And we were able to knock that area out

00:36:55.170 --> 00:36:58.320
and increase trait hypnotizability,

00:36:58.320 --> 00:37:03.090
so people had greater hypnotizability

00:37:03.090 --> 00:37:07.320
after they got active stimulation
versus when they got sham.

00:37:07.320 --> 00:37:11.949
And so it suggests that that
brain circuit is involved

00:37:11.949 --> 00:37:16.949
in the process of what therapeutic
hypnosis ends up being.

00:37:17.550 --> 00:37:18.960
But it's a very different region

00:37:18.960 --> 00:37:20.850
within the left dorsolateral than, say,

00:37:20.850 --> 00:37:24.480
we do when we do these very
intensive stimulation approaches

00:37:24.480 --> 00:37:26.400
to treat severe depression

00:37:26.400 --> 00:37:29.160
and we're able to get
people out of depression.

00:37:29.160 --> 00:37:30.993
You know, with the part
of the dorsolateral

00:37:30.993 --> 00:37:33.690
that seems to be lower in the, you know,

00:37:33.690 --> 00:37:38.690
kind of more lateral and
inferior on the DLPFC

00:37:40.830 --> 00:37:43.250
and connected with this
subgenual anterior cingulate,

00:37:43.250 --> 00:37:44.610
so the part of the anterior cingulate

00:37:44.610 --> 00:37:46.890
that processes emotion.

