00:39:01.140 --> 00:39:03.300
Based on what you told
us about the Stroop task

00:39:03.300 --> 00:39:06.570
and the role of the prefrontal
cortex in the Stroop task,

00:39:06.570 --> 00:39:09.630
to me the Stroop task is
a rule switching game.

00:39:09.630 --> 00:39:10.890
You're saying in one moment,

00:39:10.890 --> 00:39:14.100
the rule is you read
whatever the word says

00:39:14.100 --> 00:39:16.170
and then you switch and then you say,

00:39:16.170 --> 00:39:18.480
the rule now is you tell me

00:39:18.480 --> 00:39:20.400
what color the word is written in

00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:23.250
and you suppress whatever
it is the word says, okay?

00:39:23.250 --> 00:39:24.083
- [Nolan] That's right.

00:39:24.083 --> 00:39:27.600
- Okay, a rule in some
sense is, like that,

00:39:27.600 --> 00:39:31.260
is a transiently adopted belief system.

00:39:31.260 --> 00:39:33.210
So I could imagine that in depression,

00:39:33.210 --> 00:39:37.560
which has all sorts of backstory to it,

00:39:37.560 --> 00:39:39.600
that of course the
psychiatrist or psychologist

00:39:39.600 --> 00:39:41.100
or friend can pull on that thread.

00:39:41.100 --> 00:39:41.933
Like for instance,

00:39:41.933 --> 00:39:45.360
somebody might believe that they are bad

00:39:45.360 --> 00:39:46.950
or that they don't deserve love.

00:39:46.950 --> 00:39:49.050
I'm trying to bring this
into the typical language

00:39:49.050 --> 00:39:49.883
that they would talk about.

00:39:49.883 --> 00:39:52.080
Or that they will never succeed.

00:39:52.080 --> 00:39:54.390
Or that even if they keep succeeding,

00:39:54.390 --> 00:39:56.190
it's just going to get harder and harder

00:39:56.190 --> 00:39:58.050
and it will never feel good.

00:39:58.050 --> 00:40:00.720
These are sort of rules
like the Stroop task.

00:40:00.720 --> 00:40:01.553
At some level.

00:40:01.553 --> 00:40:03.300
There are rules that are
more pervasive over time,

00:40:03.300 --> 00:40:04.530
unfortunately.

00:40:04.530 --> 00:40:08.940
But I could imagine that
if the PFC is also contains

00:40:08.940 --> 00:40:10.770
some sort of maps or algorithms

00:40:10.770 --> 00:40:12.840
related to rules of emotionality

00:40:12.840 --> 00:40:15.600
or self representation or
things that we've heard,

00:40:15.600 --> 00:40:18.510
I think there must be data out
there saying that, you know,

00:40:18.510 --> 00:40:19.830
whatever we heard in middle school

00:40:19.830 --> 00:40:21.557
when someone made fun of
us, we can remember that.

00:40:21.557 --> 00:40:24.697
'Cause I can remember
things that people said

00:40:24.697 --> 00:40:26.880
about a jacket I wore one day

00:40:26.880 --> 00:40:28.890
or something in the fourth grade, crazy,

00:40:28.890 --> 00:40:30.393
I didn't even like the jacket.

00:40:31.320 --> 00:40:33.630
Now I think it was kind
of cool, but anyway.

00:40:33.630 --> 00:40:35.580
The point being that we
have an intense memory

00:40:35.580 --> 00:40:38.280
for these things to set up a
sort of rule or a question.

00:40:38.280 --> 00:40:42.390
Like maybe I don't really know
how to dress, for instance.

00:40:42.390 --> 00:40:44.820
Maybe that's why I always
wear the same black shirt.

00:40:44.820 --> 00:40:45.990
But in all seriousness,

00:40:45.990 --> 00:40:48.390
it seems like the dorsolateral
prefrontal cortex is

00:40:48.390 --> 00:40:53.390
in this amazing position to
access rules which are beliefs

00:40:53.610 --> 00:40:54.750
and beliefs are rules,

00:40:54.750 --> 00:40:58.710
and then for moments or
longer, to switch those rules.

00:40:58.710 --> 00:41:01.230
And so for somebody who's depressed

00:41:01.230 --> 00:41:02.760
to just simply look
themselves in the mirror

00:41:02.760 --> 00:41:05.190
and say, "You are great,
you are fantastic,"

00:41:05.190 --> 00:41:08.370
it feels like a lie if you
feel like garbage to say that.

00:41:08.370 --> 00:41:10.140
It doesn't fit with the rule.

00:41:10.140 --> 00:41:12.900
It's like saying that card is not red,

00:41:12.900 --> 00:41:17.127
that card is green, when your
eyes tell you that it's red.

00:41:17.127 --> 00:41:21.540
And it seems like there's
something about prefrontal cortex

00:41:21.540 --> 00:41:25.110
that in principle gives
flexibility to rules

00:41:25.110 --> 00:41:27.060
based on what we know
about the Stroop task.

00:41:27.060 --> 00:41:29.700
So given its connectivity,

00:41:29.700 --> 00:41:33.450
can we assume that the
talk therapy that occurs

00:41:33.450 --> 00:41:36.210
in the psychiatrist
office or with a friend

00:41:36.210 --> 00:41:38.013
or through journaling out something,

00:41:39.030 --> 00:41:41.730
because we do know that
reporting things about trauma

00:41:41.730 --> 00:41:44.310
or difficult circumstances
or the rules that we contain

00:41:44.310 --> 00:41:47.670
and tend to hide inside of us
about how we feel miserable

00:41:47.670 --> 00:41:50.580
about ourselves or anything really,

00:41:50.580 --> 00:41:53.340
that in rescripting that,

00:41:53.340 --> 00:41:57.030
that somehow it allows us
to do a sort of Stroop task

00:41:57.030 --> 00:41:58.470
on our beliefs.

00:41:58.470 --> 00:42:00.540
Is that a tremendous leap?

00:42:00.540 --> 00:42:02.430
I'm just really trying to
frame this in the context

00:42:02.430 --> 00:42:04.950
of what I and most people
think of as depression.

00:42:04.950 --> 00:42:05.970
- [Nolan] Yeah, totally.

00:42:05.970 --> 00:42:07.950
- Because the network components
are vitally important,

00:42:07.950 --> 00:42:09.420
but I guess what I'm
trying to figure out is

00:42:09.420 --> 00:42:14.420
like what are the algorithms
that govern prefrontal cortex?

00:42:15.030 --> 00:42:15.863
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:42:15.863 --> 00:42:17.580
So in a kind of standard

00:42:17.580 --> 00:42:20.666
cognitive behavioral
therapy session, right,

00:42:20.666 --> 00:42:25.320
what the therapist is trying
to do is identify those beliefs

00:42:25.320 --> 00:42:29.940
and you know, kind of
determine how fixed they are,

00:42:29.940 --> 00:42:32.070
you know, if they're
flexible as you're saying,

00:42:32.070 --> 00:42:37.070
and then help folks to find
another explanation for them

00:42:37.710 --> 00:42:42.710
and to kind of reintegrate that
potential other explanation

00:42:43.410 --> 00:42:45.603
into their memory system, right?

00:42:46.860 --> 00:42:49.650
Where I think TMS is really
interesting, actually,

00:42:49.650 --> 00:42:51.150
we had a lot of patients who've told me,

00:42:51.150 --> 00:42:54.960
like my therapist told me that
I wasn't trying hard enough

00:42:54.960 --> 00:42:59.430
in therapy, and you know,
and I really am trying hard,

00:42:59.430 --> 00:43:01.110
but these are, you know, moderate,

00:43:01.110 --> 00:43:03.150
pretty severe depressed patients.

00:43:03.150 --> 00:43:06.660
And as soon as we get them
well with the TMS approaches,

00:43:06.660 --> 00:43:09.180
you know, kind of rapid five day approach

00:43:09.180 --> 00:43:10.950
and the next week we come in and see them

00:43:10.950 --> 00:43:11.783
and they'll say,

00:43:11.783 --> 00:43:12.616
"You know what I did all weekend is

00:43:12.616 --> 00:43:15.600
I looked at my therapy books
and now I can understand it."

00:43:15.600 --> 00:43:18.990
And so, you know, I
actually see TMS as a way

00:43:18.990 --> 00:43:23.990
of having kind of exogenous
sorts of cognitive functions

00:43:24.270 --> 00:43:27.030
that in milder forms of depression

00:43:27.030 --> 00:43:29.640
we can pull off with psychotherapy.

00:43:29.640 --> 00:43:31.110
You know, this idea of being able

00:43:31.110 --> 00:43:34.260
to kind of turn that prefrontal cortex on

00:43:34.260 --> 00:43:37.320
and have it govern these deeper regions.

00:43:37.320 --> 00:43:38.153
In depression,

00:43:38.153 --> 00:43:41.910
the deeper regions govern
the prefrontal cortex.

00:43:41.910 --> 00:43:45.270
They precede the prefrontal
cortex timing-wise.

00:43:45.270 --> 00:43:46.980
And we've got some data in review now

00:43:46.980 --> 00:43:50.220
where we're seeing that
in depressed individuals

00:43:50.220 --> 00:43:52.443
that are responsive to
our rapid TMS approach,

00:43:52.443 --> 00:43:53.370
what we call

00:43:53.370 --> 00:43:56.760
Stanford Accelerated Intelligent
Neuromodulation Therapy,

00:43:56.760 --> 00:44:00.840
or SNT, or SAINT, if you look at the brain

00:44:00.840 --> 00:44:05.840
before people get this, they
will have a temporal delay

00:44:06.360 --> 00:44:11.343
where the cingulate is
in front of the DLPFC.

00:44:12.450 --> 00:44:15.420
And in people that are
normal healthy controls,

00:44:15.420 --> 00:44:18.990
no depression, the dorsolateral
prefrontal cortex is

00:44:18.990 --> 00:44:23.040
temporally in front of
the anterior cingulate.

00:44:23.040 --> 00:44:28.040
With effective treatment, we
can flip the timing of things

00:44:28.110 --> 00:44:32.160
so the dorsolateral is in front
of the anterior cingulate,

00:44:32.160 --> 00:44:33.960
just like in a normal person.

00:44:33.960 --> 00:44:34.793
- So you're not talking

00:44:34.793 --> 00:44:36.480
about obviously physically
moving these structures,

00:44:36.480 --> 00:44:38.730
you're talking about in
time, their activation.

00:44:38.730 --> 00:44:39.870
So in one case,

00:44:39.870 --> 00:44:42.300
it's like the coach telling
the player what to do.

00:44:42.300 --> 00:44:45.057
And the other is like a player
telling the coach what to do.

00:44:45.057 --> 00:44:46.860
And you restore order to the game.

00:44:46.860 --> 00:44:48.210
- You restore order to the game.

00:44:48.210 --> 00:44:52.080
And what it looks like is
depression, to your point,

00:44:52.080 --> 00:44:55.350
is a bunch of kind of spontaneous content

00:44:55.350 --> 00:44:58.740
that's semi-volitional that's
being kind of generated

00:44:58.740 --> 00:45:02.220
out of this conflict detection system.

00:45:02.220 --> 00:45:06.480
The cingulate seems to sense conflict

00:45:06.480 --> 00:45:08.460
and kind of feed that information,

00:45:08.460 --> 00:45:10.920
gets overactive in depression.

00:45:10.920 --> 00:45:15.420
And then in depression, it
looks like the left dorsolateral

00:45:15.420 --> 00:45:19.110
does not sufficiently clamp down on it.

00:45:19.110 --> 00:45:23.310
And what therapy appears to
do is to kind of restore that.

00:45:23.310 --> 00:45:26.460
What we see with TMS over that region is

00:45:26.460 --> 00:45:28.980
that we just exogenously
do the same sort of thing.

00:45:28.980 --> 00:45:33.240
We restore the governance
of the left dorsolateral

00:45:33.240 --> 00:45:35.220
over the cingulate area,

00:45:35.220 --> 00:45:38.490
and that is correlated
with treatment improvement.

00:45:38.490 --> 00:45:42.420
So the degree in which you can re-time,

00:45:42.420 --> 00:45:45.450
re-regulate in time the left dorsolateral

00:45:45.450 --> 00:45:46.710
over the cingulate,

00:45:46.710 --> 00:45:49.010
the more of an antidepressant
effect you have.

00:45:50.040 --> 00:45:54.540
- Can we therefore say in crude terms

00:45:54.540 --> 00:45:58.410
that the dorsolateral
prefrontal cortex really is

00:45:58.410 --> 00:46:01.500
the governor of how we
interpret physiological signals

00:46:01.500 --> 00:46:02.880
and spontaneous thoughts?

00:46:02.880 --> 00:46:05.970
- It places a lens

00:46:05.970 --> 00:46:09.090
that the rest of the
brain sees things through.

00:46:09.090 --> 00:46:11.040
And you can do these experiments

00:46:11.040 --> 00:46:15.030
where you can put a normal
healthy control person

00:46:15.030 --> 00:46:17.640
in the scanner and you can make them feel

00:46:17.640 --> 00:46:19.620
like they have a loss of control

00:46:19.620 --> 00:46:22.830
and then you can see that
region come offline, right?

00:46:22.830 --> 00:46:26.010
So you can experimentally
manipulate the system,

00:46:26.010 --> 00:46:28.500
and so kind of buffing it up,

00:46:28.500 --> 00:46:32.880
it's like TMS is almost like
exercise for the brain, right?

00:46:32.880 --> 00:46:35.880
You're kind of exercising this
region over and over again

00:46:35.880 --> 00:46:39.180
with a physiologically relevant signal

00:46:39.180 --> 00:46:41.370
and kind of turning that system on.

00:46:41.370 --> 00:46:43.050
And what's interesting,

00:46:43.050 --> 00:46:46.110
I think really interesting
for this show is to, you know,

00:46:46.110 --> 00:46:48.330
we had a couple of folks, you know,

00:46:48.330 --> 00:46:50.250
probably five or six folks that
have actually told me this,

00:46:50.250 --> 00:46:52.230
where if they remit
early enough in the week,

00:46:52.230 --> 00:46:54.450
we have this very dense
stimulation approach

00:46:54.450 --> 00:46:57.450
where we can stimulate
people really rapidly

00:46:57.450 --> 00:46:59.070
over a five day block.

00:46:59.070 --> 00:47:00.870
We don't discriminate when they get better

00:47:00.870 --> 00:47:01.890
to when they stop.

00:47:01.890 --> 00:47:03.810
So if they get better on day one,

00:47:03.810 --> 00:47:05.400
we still give them the other four days

00:47:05.400 --> 00:47:06.627
because it's in the protocol to do that.

00:47:06.627 --> 00:47:08.580
And we can't, we're getting to a point

00:47:08.580 --> 00:47:10.560
where we can tell how
long it's going to take,

00:47:10.560 --> 00:47:12.180
but we're not there yet.

00:47:12.180 --> 00:47:13.290
And so, you know,

00:47:13.290 --> 00:47:15.720
every time somebody gets
better at day one or two,

00:47:15.720 --> 00:47:17.520
at the beginning when we first
started doing this, we'd say,

00:47:17.520 --> 00:47:19.410
you know, we're not sure, you know,

00:47:19.410 --> 00:47:20.910
we think this is safe to keep going,

00:47:20.910 --> 00:47:22.800
but you know, what do you want to do?

00:47:22.800 --> 00:47:25.440
And everybody was like,
no, I want to keep going.

00:47:25.440 --> 00:47:27.630
And so, you know, by Wednesday,

00:47:27.630 --> 00:47:30.330
they're like totally zeroed
out on the depression scales,

00:47:30.330 --> 00:47:33.210
you know, even better than
most people walking around.

00:47:33.210 --> 00:47:36.000
Like really no anxiety,
no depression or anything.

00:47:36.000 --> 00:47:39.270
By Thursday, the first
guy that told me this,

00:47:39.270 --> 00:47:40.117
he came in and he said,

00:47:40.117 --> 00:47:42.600
"You know, I was driving back to my hotel

00:47:42.600 --> 00:47:44.940
and I decided to go to the
beach and I just sat there

00:47:44.940 --> 00:47:47.790
and I was totally present
in the present moment

00:47:47.790 --> 00:47:48.623
for an hour."

00:47:48.623 --> 00:47:49.783
And he's like,

00:47:49.783 --> 00:47:50.790
"I read about this in
my mindfulness books,

00:47:50.790 --> 00:47:53.400
but I experienced it last night

00:47:53.400 --> 00:47:55.350
and I've never experienced
anything like this before."

00:47:55.350 --> 00:47:57.270
And I was like, hmm, that's interesting,

00:47:57.270 --> 00:47:58.830
but kind of wasn't sure.

00:47:58.830 --> 00:48:02.040
And then I didn't tell
obviously any more patients

00:48:02.040 --> 00:48:03.300
about that, and then about five

00:48:03.300 --> 00:48:04.830
over the last couple of years,

00:48:04.830 --> 00:48:06.990
when they remit early in the week,

00:48:06.990 --> 00:48:09.150
by the end of the week they're
like going to the beach

00:48:09.150 --> 00:48:11.790
and they're like totally
having what people describe

00:48:11.790 --> 00:48:15.960
as a pretty mindful present
moment sort of experience,

00:48:15.960 --> 00:48:18.090
which is really interesting,
you know, what that is.

00:48:18.090 --> 00:48:21.450
I mean, I don't have full on
scientific data to tell you,

00:48:21.450 --> 00:48:25.530
but it's just an
interesting anecdote, right?

00:48:25.530 --> 00:48:27.810
That folks, when you push
them through this point

00:48:27.810 --> 00:48:30.870
of feeling kind of clinically well

00:48:30.870 --> 00:48:33.660
that some people end up
reporting this additional set

00:48:33.660 --> 00:48:34.493
of features.

00:48:35.370 --> 00:48:36.600
- You mentioned the cingulate

00:48:36.600 --> 00:48:38.673
and the anterior cingulate in particular.

00:48:39.630 --> 00:48:42.000
Because now I feel like for
the first time in my career,

00:48:42.000 --> 00:48:44.640
I have some sense of
what prefrontal cortex

00:48:44.640 --> 00:48:47.940
might actually be doing
besides providing a bumper

00:48:47.940 --> 00:48:49.383
for the rest of the brain.

00:48:52.110 --> 00:48:55.260
The cingulate, it seems, is
a more primitive structure

00:48:55.260 --> 00:49:00.260
in the sense that ideally
it's under the regulation

00:49:00.540 --> 00:49:02.760
of this top down control
from prefrontal cortex,

00:49:02.760 --> 00:49:04.620
but what's mapped in the cingulate?

00:49:04.620 --> 00:49:07.290
And for the non neuroscientist
out there, when I say mapped,

00:49:07.290 --> 00:49:09.420
if we were to put someone in a scanner

00:49:09.420 --> 00:49:11.940
and focus in on cingulate or
put an electrode in there,

00:49:11.940 --> 00:49:14.040
what makes the neurons in there fire?

00:49:14.040 --> 00:49:16.920
What sorts of things in
the body and in the mind

00:49:16.920 --> 00:49:21.120
and out in the world light up,
for lack of a better phrase,

00:49:21.120 --> 00:49:21.953
the cingulate?

00:49:21.953 --> 00:49:23.400
What does the cingulate like?

00:49:23.400 --> 00:49:25.530
- Yeah, yeah, so that Stroop task,

00:49:25.530 --> 00:49:28.440
those incongruent word color associations,

00:49:28.440 --> 00:49:30.573
the dorsal part of that.

00:49:31.800 --> 00:49:34.260
For obsessive compulsive
disorder patients,

00:49:34.260 --> 00:49:36.780
certain kind of triggers.

00:49:36.780 --> 00:49:39.570
You'll see some of the neural
imaging studies will point

00:49:39.570 --> 00:49:41.520
to anterior cingulate.

00:49:41.520 --> 00:49:44.880
In the kind of very crude
psychosurgery world 50 years ago,

00:49:44.880 --> 00:49:47.010
the anterior cingulotomy was a way

00:49:47.010 --> 00:49:49.937
of treating obsessive
compulsive disorder, right?

00:49:49.937 --> 00:49:52.770
'Cause that area seems to
be overactive in people

00:49:52.770 --> 00:49:55.440
who are experiencing
obsessive compulsive disorder.

00:49:55.440 --> 00:49:59.550
You can kind of walk, the
cingulate wraps around, you know,

00:49:59.550 --> 00:50:03.270
this white matter track like
bundles, it wraps around that.

00:50:03.270 --> 00:50:05.070
And so there's a part that's above that,

00:50:05.070 --> 00:50:07.290
around that, and below that,

00:50:07.290 --> 00:50:12.100
and depending upon how much
of the conflict task has

00:50:13.410 --> 00:50:14.880
an emotional component,

00:50:14.880 --> 00:50:19.880
the more ventral and
subgenual that activation is.

00:50:22.890 --> 00:50:27.060
So the dorsal part of the
anterior cingulate seems

00:50:27.060 --> 00:50:30.090
to be kind of more of a pure cognitive,

00:50:30.090 --> 00:50:34.200
maybe obsessive compulsive
disorder sort of area.

00:50:34.200 --> 00:50:37.110
Whereas when you start getting
into mood sorts of triggers,

00:50:37.110 --> 00:50:42.110
like facial expression conflicts
where you're supposed to,

00:50:42.207 --> 00:50:44.220
you know, there's an emotional Stroop task

00:50:44.220 --> 00:50:45.780
where you show the word happy

00:50:45.780 --> 00:50:49.860
and then you have a face
of a person that looks mad,

00:50:49.860 --> 00:50:52.350
then that's another way
of having the same sort

00:50:52.350 --> 00:50:53.700
of Stroop conflict.

00:50:53.700 --> 00:50:57.360
That seems to be more perigenual,
subgenual areas, right?

00:50:57.360 --> 00:50:58.560
So you can kind of,

00:50:58.560 --> 00:51:01.200
you can trigger the
cingulate based off the level

00:51:01.200 --> 00:51:05.100
of emotional valence
from none down to a lot.

00:51:05.100 --> 00:51:08.730
And that seems to be how it's distributed.

00:51:08.730 --> 00:51:11.190
There are, you know, heart
rate kind of components to it

00:51:11.190 --> 00:51:13.320
and autonomic components in there too.

00:51:13.320 --> 00:51:15.418
There's something called akinetic mutism,

00:51:15.418 --> 00:51:19.170
you know, I'm a board
certified neuropsychiatrist,

00:51:19.170 --> 00:51:21.870
behavioral neurologist,
and I've seen, you know,

00:51:21.870 --> 00:51:24.420
a lot of these what we call
zebra cases in neurology

00:51:24.420 --> 00:51:25.950
where people have, you know,

00:51:25.950 --> 00:51:28.410
these unusual neurological presentations

00:51:28.410 --> 00:51:30.840
and one of them is akinetic mutism.

00:51:30.840 --> 00:51:33.630
So if you have a glioma sitting

00:51:33.630 --> 00:51:35.730
in the inner hemispheric fissure

00:51:35.730 --> 00:51:39.630
and kind of having
pressure on the cingulate,

00:51:39.630 --> 00:51:42.270
people can get into an almost
catatonic looking state

00:51:42.270 --> 00:51:44.610
where they kind of get
stuck and they don't speak.

00:51:44.610 --> 00:51:46.380
And so that tells you something

00:51:46.380 --> 00:51:50.070
about how the cingulate
works as well, right?

00:51:50.070 --> 00:51:55.070
It's like if it's not functioning,

00:51:55.410 --> 00:51:58.590
then people have a hard
time kind of connecting

00:51:58.590 --> 00:51:59.423
with reality.

00:51:59.423 --> 00:52:03.540
It seems to need to be constantly online

00:52:03.540 --> 00:52:05.970
to be able to interact
with the exterior world.

00:52:05.970 --> 00:52:08.250
- Is it involved in some
of the dissociative states

00:52:08.250 --> 00:52:10.770
that sometimes people
who are very stressed

00:52:10.770 --> 00:52:12.390
or depressed experience?

00:52:12.390 --> 00:52:15.240
You said catatonia being an extreme one,

00:52:15.240 --> 00:52:16.890
but I know someone for instance,

00:52:16.890 --> 00:52:18.810
that when they get really stressed,

00:52:18.810 --> 00:52:22.920
and it can even be if
someone yells at them

00:52:22.920 --> 00:52:25.590
or even if someone's angry with them

00:52:25.590 --> 00:52:27.180
or they perceive
someone's angry with them,

00:52:27.180 --> 00:52:28.350
there's a developmental backstory

00:52:28.350 --> 00:52:30.273
to why they likely feel this way,

00:52:31.320 --> 00:52:33.540
they sort of just kind of can't...

00:52:33.540 --> 00:52:36.570
This is a high functioning
individual normally,

00:52:36.570 --> 00:52:38.670
and they just sort of can't function.

00:52:38.670 --> 00:52:40.800
They can't complete
simple things like email

00:52:40.800 --> 00:52:43.620
or groceries or things for a short while.

00:52:43.620 --> 00:52:45.840
It's almost like a catatonia

00:52:45.840 --> 00:52:49.263
and they refer to it as
a dissociative state.

00:52:50.670 --> 00:52:51.870
Do you see that in depression?

00:52:51.870 --> 00:52:53.520
And I mean, we're speculating here

00:52:53.520 --> 00:52:55.140
as to whether or not that
involves a cingulate,

00:52:55.140 --> 00:52:57.960
but what you're saying holds
a lot of salience for me

00:52:57.960 --> 00:52:59.220
in thinking about this example.

00:52:59.220 --> 00:53:00.053
- Yeah, yeah.

00:53:00.053 --> 00:53:04.050
There's, so you see catatonia
as an extreme outcome

00:53:04.050 --> 00:53:07.050
of depression and sometimes schizophrenia

00:53:07.050 --> 00:53:08.073
and other illnesses.

00:53:09.090 --> 00:53:11.490
Dissociation is an extreme outcome,

00:53:11.490 --> 00:53:13.830
or even in some cases,
a less extreme outcome

00:53:13.830 --> 00:53:16.410
of PTSD and trauma.

00:53:16.410 --> 00:53:17.547
And you know,

00:53:17.547 --> 00:53:21.060
and it's also a phenomenon
that happens naturally

00:53:21.060 --> 00:53:23.387
in some people that are
highly hypnotizable.

00:53:23.387 --> 00:53:26.580
And so if you ask David Spiegel,
he'd say that, you know,

00:53:26.580 --> 00:53:28.350
some of the work that
he's been working on is

00:53:28.350 --> 00:53:32.430
around posterior cingulate in
the capacity to disassociate.

00:53:32.430 --> 00:53:37.430
But yeah, you know, with our
stimulation approach to DLPFC,

00:53:37.920 --> 00:53:39.420
dorsal anterior cingulate,

00:53:39.420 --> 00:53:42.630
one of the subscales
that moved the most was

00:53:42.630 --> 00:53:46.440
the dissociative subscale
for hypnotizability.

00:53:46.440 --> 00:53:49.590
So even in a normal individual, you know,

00:53:49.590 --> 00:53:51.300
you see that change

00:53:51.300 --> 00:53:54.600
in that kind of experience
of dissociation.

00:53:54.600 --> 00:53:56.850
- I am highly hypnotizable.

00:53:56.850 --> 00:53:58.696
David's hypnotized me a number of times.

00:53:58.696 --> 00:53:59.570
In fact, we have a clip of that

00:53:59.570 --> 00:54:01.500
on our Huberman Live clips channel.

00:54:01.500 --> 00:54:04.380
I've always, well, always.

00:54:04.380 --> 00:54:07.110
Starting at my early teens,
I started exploring hypnosis.

00:54:07.110 --> 00:54:09.300
I'm extremely hypnotizable.

00:54:09.300 --> 00:54:14.068
And self hypnosis or assisted hypnosis.

00:54:14.068 --> 00:54:16.290
I don't know that I ever go
into dissociative states.

00:54:16.290 --> 00:54:18.450
I'll try and avoid forcing you

00:54:18.450 --> 00:54:20.370
into running a clinical session right now,

00:54:20.370 --> 00:54:22.620
but to assess anything like that.

00:54:22.620 --> 00:54:26.220
But this brings about something
really interesting, I think,

00:54:26.220 --> 00:54:28.770
which is I'm aware that some

00:54:28.770 --> 00:54:32.520
of the more popular emerging
treatments for depression

00:54:32.520 --> 00:54:34.800
include things like ketamine,

00:54:34.800 --> 00:54:36.960
which is a dissociative anesthetic.

00:54:36.960 --> 00:54:38.190
Is that right?
- Yep.

00:54:38.190 --> 00:54:42.480
- And my assumption is that
as a dissociative anesthetic,

00:54:42.480 --> 00:54:44.400
that it leads to dissociative states

00:54:44.400 --> 00:54:46.800
where people can sort of
third person themselves

00:54:46.800 --> 00:54:50.253
and feel somewhat distanced
from their emotions.

00:54:51.300 --> 00:54:55.713
I've also been hearing that
there are emerging treatments,

00:54:56.670 --> 00:54:59.460
psilocybin being one of them,
but some other treatments,

00:54:59.460 --> 00:55:02.940
MDMA, et cetera, that we'll
parse each of these in detail,

00:55:02.940 --> 00:55:05.160
that lead to the exact opposite state

00:55:05.160 --> 00:55:06.750
during the effect of the drug,

00:55:06.750 --> 00:55:11.100
which is a highly engaged emotionality

00:55:11.100 --> 00:55:13.053
and heart rate and sense of self.

00:55:13.980 --> 00:55:16.710
And can also lead to relief of depression.

00:55:16.710 --> 00:55:18.150
Now, whether or not this, again,

00:55:18.150 --> 00:55:20.760
reflects that depression
is many conditions

00:55:20.760 --> 00:55:21.990
as opposed to just one,

00:55:21.990 --> 00:55:24.630
or whether or not somehow tickling

00:55:24.630 --> 00:55:27.240
or in some cases pushing really
hard on the opposite ends

00:55:27.240 --> 00:55:30.810
of the scale really matter,
I am absolutely fascinated,

00:55:30.810 --> 00:55:33.240
and again, also perplexed by this.

00:55:33.240 --> 00:55:37.560
Why would it be that a drug
that induces dissociative states

00:55:37.560 --> 00:55:39.510
and a drug taken separately

00:55:39.510 --> 00:55:43.890
that induces hyper-associative
states would lead to relief

00:55:43.890 --> 00:55:45.482
of the same condition?

00:55:45.482 --> 00:55:46.920
- Yeah, no, that's a great question.

00:55:46.920 --> 00:55:50.250
Yeah, so for ketamine, you know,

00:55:50.250 --> 00:55:53.370
the level of dissociation
appears to be correlated

00:55:53.370 --> 00:55:55.320
with the therapeutic effect.

00:55:55.320 --> 00:55:58.920
It appears to be necessary
but not sufficient

00:55:58.920 --> 00:56:00.990
to produce an antidepressant effect.

00:56:00.990 --> 00:56:05.990
And so folks that don't have
any psychological change

00:56:07.080 --> 00:56:10.270
from the ketamine or don't
experience any dissociation

00:56:10.270 --> 00:56:15.240
typically tend to have less
potent antidepressant effects

00:56:15.240 --> 00:56:16.290
from ketamine.

00:56:16.290 --> 00:56:17.730
We did a study a couple of years ago,

00:56:17.730 --> 00:56:18.563
it was really interesting.

00:56:18.563 --> 00:56:21.030
So we gave folks naltrexone,

00:56:21.030 --> 00:56:23.430
which is an opiate antagonist,

00:56:23.430 --> 00:56:26.400
mu and kappa opiate receptor antagonist.

00:56:26.400 --> 00:56:29.820
And we gave folk, the same individuals,

00:56:29.820 --> 00:56:32.370
a pill of that or a pill of placebo,

00:56:32.370 --> 00:56:34.170
and they had no idea which
one they were getting.

00:56:34.170 --> 00:56:35.730
- Was this low dose naltrexone?

00:56:35.730 --> 00:56:37.350
- [Nolan] 50 milligrams,
so it's pretty high dose.

00:56:37.350 --> 00:56:38.183
- Okay.

00:56:38.183 --> 00:56:41.340
- Yeah, and so we gave a typical
ketamine therapeutic dose,

00:56:41.340 --> 00:56:45.120
and then we gave 50 milligrams
of naltrexone or placebo.

00:56:45.120 --> 00:56:46.950
And then in the same individuals,

00:56:46.950 --> 00:56:51.267
we gave two infusions, one
with each of those conditions.

00:56:51.267 --> 00:56:53.190
And if they had an antidepressant effect,

00:56:53.190 --> 00:56:54.960
we waited until they relapsed

00:56:54.960 --> 00:56:56.790
and then we gave 'em the other condition.

00:56:56.790 --> 00:57:00.450
And then we looked to see what effect

00:57:00.450 --> 00:57:02.583
of blocking the opioid receptor,

00:57:03.420 --> 00:57:04.530
what effect would you see

00:57:04.530 --> 00:57:06.780
on the antidepressant effect
of blocking the opioid receptor

00:57:06.780 --> 00:57:09.300
with the idea that if
ketamine works the way

00:57:09.300 --> 00:57:13.050
that a lot of researchers
at the time thought that it,

00:57:13.050 --> 00:57:14.100
you know, completely worked in,

00:57:14.100 --> 00:57:15.930
which is the glutamate system,

00:57:15.930 --> 00:57:19.320
then you would have no
effect of naltrexone.

00:57:19.320 --> 00:57:21.840
'Cause naltrexone just interacts
with the opioid system.

00:57:21.840 --> 00:57:24.390
It doesn't do anything
with any other systems.

00:57:24.390 --> 00:57:27.270
Ketamine has a lot of
effects over, you know,

00:57:27.270 --> 00:57:30.660
it has clear opioid effects in mice

00:57:30.660 --> 00:57:32.940
in various ways of looking at that,

00:57:32.940 --> 00:57:37.650
and an MDA receptor antagonism
and glutamate effects.

00:57:37.650 --> 00:57:41.700
And so if it's just that
the glutamate part is

00:57:41.700 --> 00:57:44.040
the part driving the
antidepressant effect,

00:57:44.040 --> 00:57:46.320
you shouldn't have any difference

00:57:46.320 --> 00:57:49.230
in the antidepressant effect
between the two conditions.

00:57:49.230 --> 00:57:53.670
If, however, the antidepressant
effect is primarily is

00:57:53.670 --> 00:57:58.140
the opioid properties of
ketamine are necessary

00:57:58.140 --> 00:57:59.820
for the antidepressant effect,

00:57:59.820 --> 00:58:02.460
then you should have a loss
of antidepressant effect

00:58:02.460 --> 00:58:05.370
during the ketamine plus
naltrexone condition

00:58:05.370 --> 00:58:09.000
that you observed in the
ketamine plus placebo condition.

00:58:09.000 --> 00:58:14.000
And what we saw was that
there was a dramatic blockade

00:58:14.550 --> 00:58:17.830
of the antidepressant effect
when naltrexone was present.

00:58:17.830 --> 00:58:22.830
Yeah, in the people that
had an antidepressant effect

00:58:23.310 --> 00:58:26.490
with ketamine plus placebo alone.

00:58:26.490 --> 00:58:31.490
And then some friends of mine
did a TMS study with pain

00:58:31.890 --> 00:58:33.570
and they stimulated

00:58:33.570 --> 00:58:35.247
over the left dorsolateral
prefrontal cortex

00:58:35.247 --> 00:58:37.230
and they gave IV naloxone,

00:58:37.230 --> 00:58:39.660
which works basically the
same way as naltrexone,

00:58:39.660 --> 00:58:43.890
and they were able to block
the anti-pain effects of TMS

00:58:43.890 --> 00:58:45.420
with a opiate blocker.

00:58:45.420 --> 00:58:49.500
So this idea that another kind
of convergent point, right?

00:58:49.500 --> 00:58:53.400
This idea that the opioid
receptor may have a role

00:58:53.400 --> 00:58:55.470
in mood regulation.

00:58:55.470 --> 00:58:57.510
What's also interesting
is if you look at people

00:58:57.510 --> 00:58:58.920
that are getting a total knee operation,

00:58:58.920 --> 00:59:01.050
very painful operation, right?

00:59:01.050 --> 00:59:05.670
You know, total knee replacement
and you age, sex, you know,

00:59:05.670 --> 00:59:07.350
everything match the individuals

00:59:07.350 --> 00:59:08.400
that are going through that.

00:59:08.400 --> 00:59:10.827
But you have a group of people
that don't have depression

00:59:10.827 --> 00:59:13.140
and a group of people
that do have depression.

00:59:13.140 --> 00:59:14.970
The presence of depression triples

00:59:14.970 --> 00:59:19.470
the oral opioid dose by day four, right?

00:59:19.470 --> 00:59:20.550
- That's required.

00:59:20.550 --> 00:59:22.980
- That's required to cover the pain

00:59:22.980 --> 00:59:24.210
but what may be happening is

00:59:24.210 --> 00:59:26.400
it's not just treating physical pain,

00:59:26.400 --> 00:59:28.530
may be treating emotional
pain as well, right?

00:59:28.530 --> 00:59:30.360
At least transiently,

00:59:30.360 --> 00:59:32.850
it seems to have an antidepressant effect.

00:59:32.850 --> 00:59:35.550
Chronically, it seems to have
a very pro-depressant effect.

00:59:35.550 --> 00:59:37.800
It can make people treatment resistant.

00:59:37.800 --> 00:59:40.440
But, you know, it's an
interesting phenomenon.

00:59:40.440 --> 00:59:43.920
But yeah, the opioid system
seems to be pretty involved.

00:59:43.920 --> 00:59:47.550
But what's interesting there
with the ketamine trial is

00:59:47.550 --> 00:59:50.970
that we didn't see any
effect on the dissociation.

00:59:50.970 --> 00:59:54.750
And so the dissociation
was the same each time.

00:59:54.750 --> 00:59:57.840
So the psychological effect
of what we call the trip

00:59:57.840 --> 01:00:00.120
or the kind of dissociative effect

01:00:00.120 --> 01:00:02.400
where people are having a
psychological phenomenon

01:00:02.400 --> 01:00:06.180
from ketamine, that was
identical both times.

01:00:06.180 --> 01:00:10.410
And so it kind of, it
also challenged this idea

01:00:10.410 --> 01:00:13.080
that the psychological experience

01:00:13.080 --> 01:00:16.830
of the psychedelic effect
may be all that's necessary

01:00:16.830 --> 01:00:17.880
to produce an effect

01:00:17.880 --> 01:00:20.130
and that the pharmacology doesn't matter

01:00:20.130 --> 01:00:22.560
as long as you can achieve that state.

01:00:22.560 --> 01:00:23.460
And so, you know,

01:00:23.460 --> 01:00:26.460
we think we pretty
clearly debunked that idea

01:00:26.460 --> 01:00:31.460
that the underlying pharmacology
and the state, you know,

01:00:31.590 --> 01:00:33.510
seem to be important.

01:00:33.510 --> 01:00:35.700
We don't know for sure if you can,

01:00:35.700 --> 01:00:36.840
a lot of people are working on this,

01:00:36.840 --> 01:00:38.430
if you can take out, you know,

01:00:38.430 --> 01:00:39.990
essentially the psychological effect

01:00:39.990 --> 01:00:44.220
and still have a drug that
works to treat the illness

01:00:44.220 --> 01:00:45.300
that you're trying to target.

01:00:45.300 --> 01:00:48.510
And there was a mouse study out this week

01:00:48.510 --> 01:00:50.490
where they had an LSD analog

01:00:50.490 --> 01:00:54.930
and they were able to see
some animal level data

01:00:54.930 --> 01:00:56.880
to suggest that could be true.

01:00:56.880 --> 01:00:58.530
But until we figure that out in humans,

01:00:58.530 --> 01:01:00.690
it's kind of to be determined.

01:01:00.690 --> 01:01:02.790
But it is curious, right?

01:01:02.790 --> 01:01:06.390
Being able to kind of use
experimental manipulations

01:01:06.390 --> 01:01:10.590
to try to separate, you know,
some of these phenomenon apart

01:01:10.590 --> 01:01:13.080
and really understand what's doing what.

01:01:13.080 --> 01:01:13.980
- It's so critical

01:01:13.980 --> 01:01:16.950
and it's so critical to
the other conversation

01:01:16.950 --> 01:01:18.090
that we'll surely get to,

01:01:18.090 --> 01:01:20.940
which is the progression of psychedelics

01:01:20.940 --> 01:01:25.680
from illicit illegal drugs
to clinically validated,

01:01:25.680 --> 01:01:27.120
and presumably at some point,

01:01:27.120 --> 01:01:29.520
either decriminalized or legal drugs,

01:01:29.520 --> 01:01:32.103
which has not yet happened,
at least not in the US.

01:01:33.300 --> 01:01:36.070
But just to make sure that
people are getting this

01:01:37.230 --> 01:01:38.790
and how crucial this is.

01:01:38.790 --> 01:01:42.662
What we're really talking
about here is the fact that,

01:01:42.662 --> 01:01:46.440
you know, if somebody takes a
multi gram dose of psilocybin

01:01:46.440 --> 01:01:50.190
or somebody takes MDMA
or they take ketamine

01:01:50.190 --> 01:01:52.020
and they experience
relief from their trauma,

01:01:52.020 --> 01:01:53.430
their depression, their addiction,

01:01:53.430 --> 01:01:54.690
or any number of the other things

01:01:54.690 --> 01:01:57.030
that indeed those compounds
have be shown to be useful for

01:01:57.030 --> 01:02:00.900
in certain contexts, clinically
supported, et cetera.

01:02:00.900 --> 01:02:03.840
There's this like
gravitational pull to the idea

01:02:03.840 --> 01:02:06.060
that, oh, it was the hallucinations.

01:02:06.060 --> 01:02:08.040
It was the dissociative state.

01:02:08.040 --> 01:02:10.290
It was the feeling of connectedness.

01:02:10.290 --> 01:02:11.430
And what we're really saying is

01:02:11.430 --> 01:02:14.310
that while that certainly could be true,

01:02:14.310 --> 01:02:18.240
it may be the case that a major
source of the positive shift

01:02:18.240 --> 01:02:21.720
that occurs after the
effect of the drug is

01:02:21.720 --> 01:02:23.100
some underlying biology,

01:02:23.100 --> 01:02:25.500
like shifts in the mu opioid receptor,

01:02:25.500 --> 01:02:27.810
a la your experiments with naltrexone,

01:02:27.810 --> 01:02:30.240
or a change in the
underlying neuromodulation

01:02:30.240 --> 01:02:33.750
that had anywhere from nothing

01:02:33.750 --> 01:02:36.690
to something to do with the real shift.

01:02:36.690 --> 01:02:38.970
And I know there's a group up at UC Davis

01:02:38.970 --> 01:02:42.360
that published a paper in
nature about a year ago

01:02:42.360 --> 01:02:46.440
also looking at this is a
chemistry lab essentially,

01:02:46.440 --> 01:02:48.090
modifying psychedelics

01:02:48.090 --> 01:02:51.210
to remove the hallucinogenic properties,

01:02:51.210 --> 01:02:52.440
the mood altering properties,

01:02:52.440 --> 01:02:54.540
and actually seeing some
pretty impressive effects

01:02:54.540 --> 01:02:57.720
and shifts in mood after
the drug wears off.

01:02:57.720 --> 01:03:01.590
And I know this gets people
upset when they hear it.

01:03:01.590 --> 01:03:03.570
This gets a lot of people upset really.

01:03:03.570 --> 01:03:05.010
Because people think, oh no,

01:03:05.010 --> 01:03:07.500
it's the intense experience that matters.

01:03:07.500 --> 01:03:12.240
But in fact, that may
not be the case at all.

01:03:12.240 --> 01:03:14.790
In fact, it's so powerful for people

01:03:14.790 --> 01:03:17.137
that sometimes I liken it
in my mind to, you know,

01:03:17.137 --> 01:03:19.410
it's like the birth of a new child

01:03:19.410 --> 01:03:21.360
and it's such an incredible experience

01:03:21.360 --> 01:03:22.860
and then people feel so much connection.

01:03:22.860 --> 01:03:25.080
And then they sort of
connect the experience

01:03:25.080 --> 01:03:26.880
of the actual birth to the connection,

01:03:26.880 --> 01:03:28.950
when in fact they're,
that's true it turns out,

01:03:28.950 --> 01:03:31.710
but there are a bunch of
other things happening too.

01:03:31.710 --> 01:03:33.210
That's simply the reflection of the fact

01:03:33.210 --> 01:03:34.230
that you're holding a child

01:03:34.230 --> 01:03:35.790
and the pheromonal effects et cetera.

01:03:35.790 --> 01:03:38.820
So anyway, I think it's very important

01:03:38.820 --> 01:03:43.820
that these different
variables be figured out.

01:03:44.310 --> 01:03:46.200
Along those lines, I want to make sure

01:03:46.200 --> 01:03:48.180
that before we dive a bit deeper

01:03:48.180 --> 01:03:49.773
into ketamine and psilocybin,

01:03:50.610 --> 01:03:53.790
that we do touch on really important topic

01:03:53.790 --> 01:03:56.130
that has been in the press a lot lately,

01:03:56.130 --> 01:03:59.100
which is SSRI, selective
serotonin reuptake inhibitors.

01:03:59.100 --> 01:04:01.140
'Cause we can't really have
a discussion about depression

01:04:01.140 --> 01:04:02.730
without talking about SSRIs.

01:04:02.730 --> 01:04:05.643
And then I want to circle back
to ketamine and psilocybin.

01:04:06.780 --> 01:04:10.140
It seems that there are now data

01:04:10.140 --> 01:04:14.790
that essentially state
that there's no direct link

01:04:14.790 --> 01:04:18.480
between serotonin levels and depression.

01:04:18.480 --> 01:04:21.120
Although my understanding is

01:04:21.120 --> 01:04:23.880
that the SSRIs are powerfully effective

01:04:23.880 --> 01:04:26.790
for certain forms of
obsessive compulsive disorder

01:04:26.790 --> 01:04:29.430
and may also be effective
for treatment of depression,

01:04:29.430 --> 01:04:32.280
but it may again be through
some effect unrelated

01:04:32.280 --> 01:04:33.570
to serotonin itself.

01:04:33.570 --> 01:04:34.403
Is that right?

01:04:34.403 --> 01:04:35.880
And how should we think about SSRIs?

01:04:35.880 --> 01:04:37.730
Are they useful, are they not useful?

01:04:39.390 --> 01:04:41.850
What's going on with
SSRIs in your patients

01:04:41.850 --> 01:04:44.512
and in other people as well?

01:04:44.512 --> 01:04:47.867
- Yeah, so the experiment
that I described a bit ago

01:04:47.867 --> 01:04:52.380
around the naltrexone and
ketamine was the first time

01:04:52.380 --> 01:04:56.400
I'm aware of where we were
able to essentially eliminate

01:04:56.400 --> 01:04:59.580
an antidepressant's effect
by using a second drug

01:04:59.580 --> 01:05:01.770
as a kind of a blockade.

01:05:01.770 --> 01:05:03.930
And it highlights a bigger issue, right?

01:05:03.930 --> 01:05:07.290
The issue that we haven't had a good way

01:05:07.290 --> 01:05:10.800
of really understanding
how these drugs work.

01:05:10.800 --> 01:05:12.330
And so it's the difference.

01:05:12.330 --> 01:05:14.880
I think a lot of the controversy there is

01:05:14.880 --> 01:05:18.150
that it's been been difficult, I think,

01:05:18.150 --> 01:05:23.150
for folks to see that
something can on one hand work

01:05:24.390 --> 01:05:27.990
and on the other hand, we
don't know how it works, right?

01:05:27.990 --> 01:05:31.920
And so SSRIs clearly work.

01:05:31.920 --> 01:05:34.890
You know, many, many meta analyses

01:05:34.890 --> 01:05:36.990
kind of proving that out, right?

01:05:36.990 --> 01:05:39.180
That in a subpopulation of individuals,

01:05:39.180 --> 01:05:43.200
they achieve great benefit
from depression, you know,

01:05:43.200 --> 01:05:46.530
for depression, for obsessive
compulsive disorder,

01:05:46.530 --> 01:05:48.840
for generalized anxiety
disorder, panic, you know,

01:05:48.840 --> 01:05:49.673
all these things,

01:05:49.673 --> 01:05:52.830
you can see an improvement
in those symptoms

01:05:52.830 --> 01:05:54.960
with what we call SSRIs

01:05:54.960 --> 01:05:57.030
or selective serotonin
reuptake inhibitors.

01:05:57.030 --> 01:05:58.920
The issue there is

01:05:58.920 --> 01:06:02.170
that these selective serotonin
reuptake inhibitors end up

01:06:03.090 --> 01:06:05.760
blocking the reuptake of serotonin

01:06:05.760 --> 01:06:08.530
and leaving the serotonin, you know,

01:06:08.530 --> 01:06:13.530
in this kind of in between,
between two neurons for a while

01:06:14.460 --> 01:06:17.460
and allowing for more
serotonin to kind of be there.

01:06:17.460 --> 01:06:21.960
The issue is that they don't
work immediately, right?

01:06:21.960 --> 01:06:25.410
So they don't work like the
same day you start taking them.

01:06:25.410 --> 01:06:29.520
And that suggests that
probably it's not exactly

01:06:29.520 --> 01:06:33.060
the serotonin being in there
that's directly driving it,

01:06:33.060 --> 01:06:36.590
that it's much more likely
that it may have some, say,

01:06:36.590 --> 01:06:38.820
brain plasticity effects, right?

01:06:38.820 --> 01:06:43.290
We know that things like brain
derived neurotrophic factor

01:06:43.290 --> 01:06:47.580
get upregulated with chronic
oral antidepressant use.

01:06:47.580 --> 01:06:52.580
And so that's kind of the idea
is that these things work,

01:06:53.220 --> 01:06:54.690
but what's powerful,

01:06:54.690 --> 01:06:57.240
and I think with the
authors of this paper,

01:06:57.240 --> 01:07:00.210
this extremely controversial paper,

01:07:00.210 --> 01:07:03.000
were in part trying to say was

01:07:03.000 --> 01:07:07.620
that there's not a deficit of serotonin.

01:07:07.620 --> 01:07:12.060
You're not born with what people
call a chemical imbalance.

01:07:12.060 --> 01:07:13.260
And psychiatry's known this.

01:07:13.260 --> 01:07:15.627
This is not actually new
information, anybody, you know,

01:07:15.627 --> 01:07:18.180
and it's kind of a rehashing

01:07:18.180 --> 01:07:20.370
of a bunch of information
we've known for a while now,

01:07:20.370 --> 01:07:21.960
but in the lay press,

01:07:21.960 --> 01:07:24.360
it's kind of hit in a
way that it didn't seem

01:07:24.360 --> 01:07:29.280
to grab attention before
with previous publications.

01:07:29.280 --> 01:07:32.793
But this idea that this chemical
imbalance idea is wrong.

01:07:34.170 --> 01:07:38.223
I really think that part's
important because I think that,

01:07:39.240 --> 01:07:42.810
you know, for a while, I think psychiatry,

01:07:42.810 --> 01:07:46.230
you know, what I'll call
psychiatry 1.0, right?

01:07:46.230 --> 01:07:48.840
This kind of idea of
Freud and psychotherapy

01:07:48.840 --> 01:07:50.463
and its origins.

01:07:52.140 --> 01:07:54.690
It was a lot around, you know, your family

01:07:54.690 --> 01:07:57.300
and those experiences and
psychotherapy kind of going in

01:07:57.300 --> 01:08:00.420
and correcting or helping
you to figure out,

01:08:00.420 --> 01:08:03.720
and you know, you being able to see,

01:08:03.720 --> 01:08:06.120
or people hear you so that
you can eventually come

01:08:06.120 --> 01:08:09.750
to the conclusion of certain cognitions

01:08:09.750 --> 01:08:11.280
that aren't helping you, right?

01:08:11.280 --> 01:08:13.620
And there's a huge importance there,

01:08:13.620 --> 01:08:16.620
but there's a history where, you know,

01:08:16.620 --> 01:08:19.370
things like the schizophrenogenic
mother and all of that,

01:08:19.370 --> 01:08:21.270
you know, that was a concept
at some point, right?

01:08:21.270 --> 01:08:24.780
And so we've transitioned
from that to, you know,

01:08:24.780 --> 01:08:26.790
for a long time the chemical imbalance,

01:08:26.790 --> 01:08:30.060
which I'll call psychiatry 2.0.

01:08:30.060 --> 01:08:31.195
You know,

01:08:31.195 --> 01:08:34.710
this idea that there's
something chemically missing

01:08:34.710 --> 01:08:39.710
and I think that the
trouble there for a patient

01:08:40.860 --> 01:08:43.080
who's not a physician,

01:08:43.080 --> 01:08:48.000
who's not someone who's steeped
in these sorts of ideas,

01:08:49.770 --> 01:08:54.690
who's, you know, more of kind of a person,

01:08:54.690 --> 01:08:56.820
kind of average American out there, right,

01:08:56.820 --> 01:08:59.430
is that it's sending a message

01:08:59.430 --> 01:09:01.950
of there's something missing with me,

01:09:01.950 --> 01:09:04.890
whether it be my experiences
I had no control over

01:09:04.890 --> 01:09:08.670
when I was a child or
a chemical in my brain.

01:09:08.670 --> 01:09:11.853
What I think is really powerful with TMS,

01:09:13.200 --> 01:09:15.867
you know, really powerful with TMS,

01:09:15.867 --> 01:09:18.480
and even powerful with
the psychedelic story is

01:09:18.480 --> 01:09:20.760
it's saying something different.

01:09:20.760 --> 01:09:24.510
You know, TMS works and
there's no serotonin coming in

01:09:24.510 --> 01:09:25.920
or out of the brain, right?

01:09:25.920 --> 01:09:28.170
And we're doing a rapid form of TMS

01:09:28.170 --> 01:09:29.880
that works in one to five days.

01:09:29.880 --> 01:09:32.130
There's no, it's very unlikely

01:09:32.130 --> 01:09:35.160
that there's some long
term kind of upregulation

01:09:35.160 --> 01:09:36.780
of serotonin that's driving that.

01:09:36.780 --> 01:09:39.240
So our work actually kind of pushes back

01:09:39.240 --> 01:09:41.330
on this serotonin hypothesis

01:09:41.330 --> 01:09:43.980
as being kind of the center of depression

01:09:43.980 --> 01:09:44.850
because it says, look,

01:09:44.850 --> 01:09:47.250
we're not giving anybody any serotonin.

01:09:47.250 --> 01:09:49.890
We're simply turning
these brain regions on

01:09:49.890 --> 01:09:51.990
and we're focused on the circuitry.

01:09:51.990 --> 01:09:53.400
And that's psychiatry 3.0.

01:09:53.400 --> 01:09:55.182
It's not just like neuromodulation.

01:09:55.182 --> 01:09:57.030
Neuromodulations are
really nice, you know,

01:09:57.030 --> 01:09:58.560
use case for psychiatry 3.0

01:09:58.560 --> 01:10:00.720
'cause it's a way to focally

01:10:00.720 --> 01:10:03.030
and directly perturb brain regions

01:10:03.030 --> 01:10:05.550
in whatever modality you're using.

01:10:05.550 --> 01:10:07.830
But you know, there are a lot of groups

01:10:07.830 --> 01:10:10.080
that are actually doing
neuroimaging before and after,

01:10:10.080 --> 01:10:12.600
and they're able to see
circuit level changes

01:10:12.600 --> 01:10:15.690
for something like psilocybin or ketamine

01:10:15.690 --> 01:10:18.540
long after the drug is gone, right?

01:10:18.540 --> 01:10:22.290
Suggesting in those same
brain regions converge,

01:10:22.290 --> 01:10:24.540
so the subgenual default
mode network connection

01:10:24.540 --> 01:10:26.190
that we see is changing

01:10:26.190 --> 01:10:30.093
with our Stanford neuromodulation
therapy technique.

01:10:30.093 --> 01:10:32.910
It's that same set of brain regions

01:10:32.910 --> 01:10:36.960
that ketamine and
psilocybin seem to act on,

01:10:36.960 --> 01:10:40.290
act on these connections
between brain networks

01:10:40.290 --> 01:10:41.820
that seem to shift.

01:10:41.820 --> 01:10:44.640
And so it refocuses the story

01:10:44.640 --> 01:10:48.240
on something that's highly correctable.

01:10:48.240 --> 01:10:51.630
And it's basically electrophysiology

01:10:51.630 --> 01:10:56.400
and it's basically kind
of recalibrating a circuit

01:10:56.400 --> 01:11:00.870
that is recalibrate-able instead
of I have something missing

01:11:00.870 --> 01:11:04.941
or I have some set of
experiences early in life

01:11:04.941 --> 01:11:08.100
that are going to forever trap me

01:11:08.100 --> 01:11:10.350
in these psychiatric diagnoses.

01:11:10.350 --> 01:11:12.060
And so it kind of challenges that idea.

01:11:12.060 --> 01:11:15.650
And I think that's what's so
powerful about psychiatry 3.0.

01:11:16.530 --> 01:11:20.100
This idea of focusing on the
circuit because it gets us

01:11:20.100 --> 01:11:22.890
into thinking about psychiatry
and psychiatric illnesses

01:11:22.890 --> 01:11:25.440
as something that are recoverable.

01:11:25.440 --> 01:11:26.490
People can get better.

01:11:26.490 --> 01:11:29.100
People, you know, we've seen
with our TMS techniques,

01:11:29.100 --> 01:11:31.230
we've seen with some of the
psychedelic work that we've done

01:11:31.230 --> 01:11:35.310
where people are actually
in normal levels of mood

01:11:35.310 --> 01:11:37.320
for sustained periods of time or-

01:11:37.320 --> 01:11:38.310
- Within five days.

01:11:38.310 --> 01:11:39.870
- Within five or less days.

01:11:39.870 --> 01:11:41.880
And in the case of the psychedelics,

01:11:41.880 --> 01:11:43.020
within a few days, right?

01:11:43.020 --> 01:11:46.170
So we can get people out of these states.

01:11:46.170 --> 01:11:47.550
They're totally well,

01:11:47.550 --> 01:11:49.410
there's no drug in their
system in that point,

01:11:49.410 --> 01:11:50.520
in the case of the psychedelics.

01:11:50.520 --> 01:11:54.330
It was never a drug in their
system, in the case of TMS.

01:11:54.330 --> 01:11:57.960
And it just tells us that it's fixable.

01:11:57.960 --> 01:11:58.950
It's just like the heart.

01:11:58.950 --> 01:12:01.500
It's just like an arrhythmia in the heart.

01:12:01.500 --> 01:12:03.780
It's just like, you know,
these other illnesses,

01:12:03.780 --> 01:12:05.100
that it's like a broken leg.

01:12:05.100 --> 01:12:06.870
We can go in and do something

01:12:06.870 --> 01:12:08.490
and we can get somebody better.

01:12:08.490 --> 01:12:10.770
Then I think what's empowering

01:12:10.770 --> 01:12:13.729
and what a lot of patients
have told me is they say,

01:12:13.729 --> 01:12:17.610
you know, some people will
relapse and need more stimulation

01:12:17.610 --> 01:12:19.860
or need more psychedelics
or whatever it is,

01:12:19.860 --> 01:12:24.360
but they'll tell me, I've
relapsed and I'm depressed again,

01:12:24.360 --> 01:12:26.730
but I'll never think
about killing myself again

01:12:26.730 --> 01:12:30.663
because I know that if I
go get stimulated again,

01:12:31.500 --> 01:12:33.489
it improves, it gets better.

01:12:33.489 --> 01:12:36.480
I will be able to
reachieve it and I can't.

01:12:36.480 --> 01:12:39.510
And I don't fear that
I'm chronically broken.

01:12:39.510 --> 01:12:42.540
I don't fear that the
chemical imbalance is

01:12:42.540 --> 01:12:43.560
still imbalanced.

01:12:43.560 --> 01:12:46.170
I don't fear that these
things that I couldn't control

01:12:46.170 --> 01:12:47.790
in my childhood, you know,

01:12:47.790 --> 01:12:50.610
are going to be there and
drive this problem forever.

01:12:50.610 --> 01:12:53.760
And I think that's what's
so powerful about this.

01:12:53.760 --> 01:12:54.810
- [Andrew] The sense of control.

01:12:54.810 --> 01:12:56.580
- The sense of control, the sense of...

01:12:56.580 --> 01:12:58.740
They're not doing the
stimulation themselves.

01:12:58.740 --> 01:13:00.420
They're not administering the drug

01:13:00.420 --> 01:13:01.470
in these trials themselves.

01:13:01.470 --> 01:13:02.490
And they probably never will.

01:13:02.490 --> 01:13:04.530
These will probably be medical treatments.

01:13:04.530 --> 01:13:06.420
But they are choosing to do it.

01:13:06.420 --> 01:13:08.730
And in that sense, they are in control.

01:13:08.730 --> 01:13:11.460
- Yeah, I have a good
friend, I won't out him

01:13:11.460 --> 01:13:13.170
for reasons that'll
become clear in a moment,

01:13:13.170 --> 01:13:18.170
who was quite obese and
lost a lot of weight

01:13:18.390 --> 01:13:20.130
and was really proud of himself.

01:13:20.130 --> 01:13:22.470
And then I guess we could
say he sort of relapsed

01:13:22.470 --> 01:13:23.670
in a sense.

01:13:23.670 --> 01:13:26.430
Not all the way, but far along.

01:13:26.430 --> 01:13:28.740
But his tone around it was very different.

01:13:28.740 --> 01:13:32.370
He knew he had accomplished
what his goal once before.

01:13:32.370 --> 01:13:33.780
He was disappointed in himself,

01:13:33.780 --> 01:13:36.750
but he knew exactly why he had relapsed.

01:13:36.750 --> 01:13:37.583
It was very clear.

01:13:37.583 --> 01:13:39.780
He had essentially relapsed
to the previous set

01:13:39.780 --> 01:13:42.690
of eating behaviors and
lack of exercise behaviors

01:13:42.690 --> 01:13:44.703
and has now brought himself back again.

01:13:45.600 --> 01:13:49.860
And it just resonates with
your story that, you know,

01:13:49.860 --> 01:13:51.630
once somebody understands they can do it

01:13:51.630 --> 01:13:52.890
because they've been there before,

01:13:52.890 --> 01:13:57.140
this idea again of considering
new rules, that there's...

01:13:57.990 --> 01:14:01.200
And that brings me to this
question about psychedelics

01:14:01.200 --> 01:14:05.149
and frankly the altered thinking
and perception that occurs

01:14:05.149 --> 01:14:09.843
in high dose psilocybin clinical sessions.

01:14:11.160 --> 01:14:12.840
It seems that the disordered thinking,

01:14:12.840 --> 01:14:16.080
even though it could be random, right?

01:14:16.080 --> 01:14:18.990
Hearing colors and seeing sounds is

01:14:18.990 --> 01:14:20.760
always the kind of cliche statement

01:14:20.760 --> 01:14:22.593
of the Timothy Leary area.

01:14:23.850 --> 01:14:27.246
Also, you know, right there,
that's a Stroop task of sorts.

01:14:27.246 --> 01:14:29.790
It's a synesthesia, it's a
combining of perceptions,

01:14:29.790 --> 01:14:32.280
but it's sort of Stroop task-ish

01:14:32.280 --> 01:14:36.930
in that it's a new set of rules
for the same stuff, right?

01:14:36.930 --> 01:14:41.880
And many people do report improvements

01:14:41.880 --> 01:14:45.210
in trauma related
symptomology and depression,

01:14:45.210 --> 01:14:47.760
as I understand it from my
read of the clinical trials,

01:14:47.760 --> 01:14:48.900
after taking psilocybin.

01:14:48.900 --> 01:14:50.070
Because during those sessions,

01:14:50.070 --> 01:14:53.460
something comes to mind spontaneously.

01:14:53.460 --> 01:14:57.060
As you and I were talking about
earlier, they will report,

01:14:57.060 --> 01:15:01.140
for instance, a new way
of seeing the old problem.

01:15:01.140 --> 01:15:03.428
And the old problem could be
the voice that they're no good,

01:15:03.428 --> 01:15:04.950
nothing will ever work out,

01:15:04.950 --> 01:15:06.990
or could be even more subtle than that.

01:15:06.990 --> 01:15:09.660
So that raises two questions.

01:15:09.660 --> 01:15:13.440
One is about the basic
functioning of the human brain,

01:15:13.440 --> 01:15:18.440
which is why do you think
the brain would ever hold on

01:15:19.170 --> 01:15:22.650
to rules that don't serve us well?

01:15:22.650 --> 01:15:23.880
That's one question.

01:15:23.880 --> 01:15:25.947
And then the second question is,

01:15:27.060 --> 01:15:30.660
what is it about psilocybin
and related molecules

01:15:30.660 --> 01:15:32.100
in terms of their neurochemistry,

01:15:32.100 --> 01:15:35.070
in terms of the ways they
disrupt thinking and feeling,

01:15:35.070 --> 01:15:37.590
et cetera, during the session

01:15:37.590 --> 01:15:42.590
that allow this novel rule
consideration phenomenon?

01:15:44.010 --> 01:15:44.910
- Yeah.

01:15:44.910 --> 01:15:46.020
So the first question,

01:15:46.020 --> 01:15:51.020
I think it's an evolutionary
neurobiology answer, right?

01:15:51.390 --> 01:15:55.500
I think that at the individual
person level, you know,

01:15:55.500 --> 01:15:56.910
it doesn't make a whole lot of sense

01:15:56.910 --> 01:15:58.110
that when we're really stressed out,

01:15:58.110 --> 01:15:59.880
some of us want to eat more, right?

01:15:59.880 --> 01:16:01.620
At the individual person level

01:16:01.620 --> 01:16:02.453
'cause it's like,

01:16:02.453 --> 01:16:04.440
that's not particularly
that good for my health

01:16:04.440 --> 01:16:06.300
in the long term.

01:16:06.300 --> 01:16:08.820
But if you think about it, like, you know,

01:16:08.820 --> 01:16:12.060
in some 500 years ago, 1,000 years ago,

01:16:12.060 --> 01:16:13.620
if I'm highly stressed out,

01:16:13.620 --> 01:16:16.260
it's most likely that I'm
about to not have food

01:16:16.260 --> 01:16:18.210
at some point and I
should eat a bunch of food

01:16:18.210 --> 01:16:20.880
that is high fat, high
sugar, high carb food

01:16:20.880 --> 01:16:24.540
to put on weight for that next phase

01:16:24.540 --> 01:16:26.880
where in this stress I may be in battle

01:16:26.880 --> 01:16:27.900
and I don't have food

01:16:27.900 --> 01:16:30.240
and I have enough fuel on board, right?

01:16:30.240 --> 01:16:35.130
And so we end up being a result

01:16:35.130 --> 01:16:37.740
of probably a lot of biology
that's not that useful

01:16:37.740 --> 01:16:39.120
in the modern era.

01:16:39.120 --> 01:16:43.410
And I think in the brain for,
say, let's say PTSD, right?

01:16:43.410 --> 01:16:44.700
A lot of veterans come back

01:16:44.700 --> 01:16:46.410
and they experience these PTSD symptoms

01:16:46.410 --> 01:16:50.850
and they're not at all
useful back home, right?

01:16:50.850 --> 01:16:53.010
You know, they hear some loud noise

01:16:53.010 --> 01:16:54.660
and all of a sudden they're behind a car

01:16:54.660 --> 01:16:57.150
or they're behind a, you
know, I've heard of folks,

01:16:57.150 --> 01:16:59.040
you know, jump and run behind a trashcan

01:16:59.040 --> 01:17:01.170
or whatever in the middle of San Francisco

01:17:01.170 --> 01:17:02.970
when they hear a loud noise.

01:17:02.970 --> 01:17:05.613
But if you put them
back in the battlefield.

01:17:07.320 --> 01:17:08.153
- [Andrew] Highly adaptive.

01:17:08.153 --> 01:17:09.180
- That's highly adaptive, right?

01:17:09.180 --> 01:17:14.180
And so I think what's
interesting is that we,

01:17:17.070 --> 01:17:22.070
in the absence of using
substances like psychedelics,

01:17:22.200 --> 01:17:26.550
end up having these
very persistent memories

01:17:26.550 --> 01:17:29.100
that are attached to
negatively balanced emotion,

01:17:29.100 --> 01:17:31.770
predominantly, as you were saying earlier,

01:17:31.770 --> 01:17:34.638
the jacket in elementary school, you know,

01:17:34.638 --> 01:17:36.660
I had various things like
that for me too, right?

01:17:36.660 --> 01:17:38.493
You remember these things.

01:17:40.169 --> 01:17:43.350
And we hold onto those things

01:17:43.350 --> 01:17:46.350
from I think an evolutionary
neurobiology standpoint,

01:17:46.350 --> 01:17:49.263
but what seems to, for whatever reason,

01:17:50.100 --> 01:17:53.960
kind of alleviate that
are these substances,

01:17:55.110 --> 01:17:57.330
some new like MDMA,

01:17:57.330 --> 01:17:59.670
some that have been around
for thousands of years,

01:17:59.670 --> 01:18:04.340
like psilocybin, and used as
a sacrament in traditions,

01:18:09.510 --> 01:18:12.470
seem to have a therapeutic effect.

01:18:12.470 --> 01:18:15.270
It seems to be pretty long
lasting for these phenomenon.

01:18:15.270 --> 01:18:17.610
And so it's just curious, right?

01:18:17.610 --> 01:18:22.610
It's curious that in the absence of that,

01:18:22.710 --> 01:18:24.720
these things will keep going on and on,

01:18:24.720 --> 01:18:28.350
but in the presence of that exposure,

01:18:28.350 --> 01:18:31.590
then all of a sudden you see
a resolution of the problem.

01:18:31.590 --> 01:18:33.750
And we have some work now
we're treating folks with,

01:18:33.750 --> 01:18:37.650
Navy SEALs, and the data's
still being analyzed.

01:18:37.650 --> 01:18:39.960
But the anecdotes that
we're getting, right,

01:18:39.960 --> 01:18:41.829
are folks are coming
back and they're saying

01:18:41.829 --> 01:18:44.540
it's finally gone, right?

01:18:44.540 --> 01:18:48.360
These set of PTSD
symptoms are finally gone.

01:18:48.360 --> 01:18:51.360
And so this idea that for whatever reason,

01:18:51.360 --> 01:18:54.060
going into what's probably
a highly plastic state

01:18:54.060 --> 01:18:55.080
like we were talking about earlier,

01:18:55.080 --> 01:18:57.270
upregulation of brain
derived neurotrophic factor

01:18:57.270 --> 01:19:00.453
in the case of ibogaine, glial
derived neurotrophic factor,

01:19:01.290 --> 01:19:04.200
this highly plastic state and the ability

01:19:04.200 --> 01:19:07.770
to kind of re-experience memories.

01:19:07.770 --> 01:19:09.419
And then as you know,

01:19:09.419 --> 01:19:13.950
we always reconsolidate a
memory when we bring it back up,

01:19:13.950 --> 01:19:14.783
we always reconsolidate it.

01:19:14.783 --> 01:19:19.050
But reconsolidating it in that
state, for whatever reason,

01:19:19.050 --> 01:19:22.710
may drive a therapeutic effect.

01:19:22.710 --> 01:19:27.390
And, you know, the jury's still out.

01:19:27.390 --> 01:19:32.130
I would say that I'm kind of an agnostic

01:19:32.130 --> 01:19:35.130
to what tool I'm using kind of guy.

01:19:35.130 --> 01:19:40.020
Like my business is to find
treatments that help people.

01:19:40.020 --> 01:19:43.710
And so I'm much more like
pragmatic about it, you know.

01:19:43.710 --> 01:19:48.710
If this sort of thing, which
has a lot of cultural baggage,

01:19:49.800 --> 01:19:50.850
but if this sort of thing

01:19:50.850 --> 01:19:52.470
ultimately ends up being therapeutic,

01:19:52.470 --> 01:19:55.350
if we can design trials
that convince me and others

01:19:55.350 --> 01:19:58.080
that it is, then we
should absolutely use it.

01:19:58.080 --> 01:19:58.913
You know?

01:19:58.913 --> 01:20:03.913
And if it doesn't, then we
clearly shouldn't use it, right?

01:20:04.140 --> 01:20:06.870
And I think that's a big question

01:20:06.870 --> 01:20:08.070
the field's going to have to work out.

01:20:08.070 --> 01:20:10.110
We have a hard time blinding these trials

01:20:10.110 --> 01:20:13.230
because the placebo condition is not easy

01:20:13.230 --> 01:20:15.330
to pull off, obviously.

01:20:15.330 --> 01:20:18.963
- A placebo for a psilocybin
journey is hard to imagine.

01:20:20.419 --> 01:20:22.050
- We've got, you know, we've
been thinking about this

01:20:22.050 --> 01:20:24.510
and maybe that ketamine study

01:20:24.510 --> 01:20:25.470
that I was talking about earlier,

01:20:25.470 --> 01:20:28.260
if we could give people
naltrexone and ketamine,

01:20:28.260 --> 01:20:32.747
maybe that's a good sort of
placebo condition, right?

01:20:32.747 --> 01:20:34.800
'Cause we know that we can block any

01:20:34.800 --> 01:20:36.810
of the actual antidepressant
effects of ketamine,

01:20:36.810 --> 01:20:38.367
they still have an experience, you know.

01:20:38.367 --> 01:20:40.470
And so that's one way of doing it.

01:20:40.470 --> 01:20:41.817
But thinking about ways to do that

01:20:41.817 --> 01:20:43.860
and really kind of proving this out.

01:20:43.860 --> 01:20:46.320
And that's been, yeah,

01:20:46.320 --> 01:20:48.030
I think that's been kind of central

01:20:48.030 --> 01:20:50.400
to the way I've been thinking about this.

01:20:50.400 --> 01:20:53.220
But yeah, I think there's the
work that's been done so far,

01:20:53.220 --> 01:20:55.170
the first psilocybin trial,

01:20:55.170 --> 01:20:57.810
the first MDMA trial was published

01:20:57.810 --> 01:20:59.820
in "Nature Medicine" recently.

01:20:59.820 --> 01:21:01.170
- And what do those generally say?

01:21:01.170 --> 01:21:04.290
I mean, that they are effective
for a number of people

01:21:04.290 --> 01:21:06.720
after one session, two sessions?

01:21:06.720 --> 01:21:08.370
What's sort of the general contour of?

01:21:08.370 --> 01:21:11.310
And let's start with psilocybin and MDMA.

01:21:11.310 --> 01:21:16.310
- Yeah, so MDMA appears to,
in one to a few MDMA sessions,

01:21:16.950 --> 01:21:21.690
have an anti PTSD effect
that seems to be, you know,

01:21:21.690 --> 01:21:26.070
outside of the kind of
standard assumed levels

01:21:26.070 --> 01:21:29.520
of PTSD improvement that you
can observe in individuals

01:21:29.520 --> 01:21:31.350
with this level of PTSD, right?

01:21:31.350 --> 01:21:33.150
So what we call the effect size,

01:21:33.150 --> 01:21:37.723
which is essentially like a effect size,

01:21:38.640 --> 01:21:39.960
the measure that allows for you

01:21:39.960 --> 01:21:42.510
to compare different
treatments to each other

01:21:42.510 --> 01:21:44.640
for different conditions
that are, you know, agnostic

01:21:44.640 --> 01:21:46.680
to what the actual illness is.

01:21:46.680 --> 01:21:49.890
You know, the effect
size is there, you know,

01:21:49.890 --> 01:21:52.770
approach effect size is the
things that are pretty effective

01:21:52.770 --> 01:21:55.800
like antacids for heartburn, right?

01:21:55.800 --> 01:21:59.070
And you see that with MDMA treatment.

01:21:59.070 --> 01:22:02.770
- So does that mean that
for people that have trauma,

01:22:02.770 --> 01:22:05.580
and again, we're talking
about in a clinical setting,

01:22:05.580 --> 01:22:08.400
they take a one or two doses of MDMA.

01:22:08.400 --> 01:22:12.420
I think the standard maps
dose is 150 to 175 milligrams.

01:22:12.420 --> 01:22:14.280
Again, doing this with
a physician, et cetera,

01:22:14.280 --> 01:22:16.293
control clinical trial, legal.

01:22:17.220 --> 01:22:18.180
- [Nolan] Yep, exactly.

01:22:18.180 --> 01:22:20.280
- They do it once or twice.

01:22:20.280 --> 01:22:22.110
And broadly speaking,

01:22:22.110 --> 01:22:24.600
what percentage of people
who had trauma report

01:22:24.600 --> 01:22:28.740
feeling significant relief
from their trauma afterward?

01:22:28.740 --> 01:22:30.300
- It's about 2/3 of people had

01:22:30.300 --> 01:22:35.300
a clinically significant
change in their PTSD.

01:22:35.550 --> 01:22:36.383
- That's impressive.

01:22:36.383 --> 01:22:37.380
- [Nolan] Which is impressive, right?

01:22:37.380 --> 01:22:38.723
- And how long lasting was that?

01:22:38.723 --> 01:22:41.350
I mean, these trials were
ended pretty recently, so...

01:22:41.350 --> 01:22:43.140
- Yeah, it appears to last for a while.

01:22:43.140 --> 01:22:45.090
In the earlier trials where
they followed people out,

01:22:45.090 --> 01:22:47.340
it seemed to last for
kind of in the years range

01:22:47.340 --> 01:22:48.173
for some people.

01:22:48.173 --> 01:22:52.110
And so it's, you know,
it's pretty compelling.

01:22:52.110 --> 01:22:55.740
Psilocybin, you know, and
contrast that with ketamine,

01:22:55.740 --> 01:22:58.230
which only on average lasts
about a week and a half

01:22:58.230 --> 01:23:01.800
for a single infusion,
so it's a much shorter.

01:23:01.800 --> 01:23:03.840
- So they have to get
repeated infusions of ketamine

01:23:03.840 --> 01:23:06.570
every 10 days or so?

01:23:06.570 --> 01:23:07.830
Forever?

01:23:07.830 --> 01:23:09.420
- For some people, or they end up getting

01:23:09.420 --> 01:23:12.990
like a bunch of doses
for a couple of weeks.

01:23:12.990 --> 01:23:15.873
And then for some people
that seems to last a while.

01:23:17.277 --> 01:23:19.650
You know, that's where I
think the psilocybin story

01:23:19.650 --> 01:23:23.070
for depression and the
MDMA story for PTSD seemed

01:23:23.070 --> 01:23:24.180
more interesting to me.

01:23:24.180 --> 01:23:28.050
- So for psilocybin, what
is the rough percentages on,

01:23:28.050 --> 01:23:29.640
and this would be relief not from trauma,

01:23:29.640 --> 01:23:31.470
but from depression, correct?

01:23:31.470 --> 01:23:32.760
- Yeah, yeah, exactly.

01:23:32.760 --> 01:23:34.680
So it's, you know, in open label studies,

01:23:34.680 --> 01:23:37.140
it's closer to like half
to 2/3 of people end up

01:23:37.140 --> 01:23:38.580
getting better depending upon their level

01:23:38.580 --> 01:23:39.916
of treatment resistance.

01:23:39.916 --> 01:23:43.950
In the blinded trials it was
more like 1/3 or so of people,

01:23:43.950 --> 01:23:45.900
you know, experienced relief.

01:23:45.900 --> 01:23:49.767
And this is, you know, this is
a press release of the data,

01:23:49.767 --> 01:23:51.780
you know, and so it
hasn't, to my knowledge,

01:23:51.780 --> 01:23:53.100
it hasn't been published yet.

01:23:53.100 --> 01:23:55.440
And so I'm looking forward
to seeing the full paper

01:23:55.440 --> 01:23:56.273
on that one.

01:23:56.273 --> 01:23:59.760
But it, you know, separated from placebo

01:23:59.760 --> 01:24:02.040
and looks pretty good as well.

01:24:02.040 --> 01:24:03.570
It looks like it's, you know,

01:24:03.570 --> 01:24:05.430
the first of two trials
that need to be done

01:24:05.430 --> 01:24:07.050
to get this thing approved

01:24:07.050 --> 01:24:08.520
for treatment resistant depression.

01:24:08.520 --> 01:24:11.793
And so that stuff looks good.

01:24:12.810 --> 01:24:17.160
- In terms of MDMA, for
many years it was reported

01:24:17.160 --> 01:24:18.150
in the popular press

01:24:18.150 --> 01:24:19.503
and there was a paper published in science

01:24:19.503 --> 01:24:21.630
that MDMA was neurotoxic,

01:24:21.630 --> 01:24:23.730
that it would kill serotonin neurons.

01:24:23.730 --> 01:24:25.620
This was what was always said.

01:24:25.620 --> 01:24:29.070
Then I saw another paper
published in science

01:24:29.070 --> 01:24:31.440
that wasn't a retraction
of the previous paper,

01:24:31.440 --> 01:24:33.690
but rather was a second
paper in the same group

01:24:33.690 --> 01:24:37.020
that essentially admitted
that the first time around,

01:24:37.020 --> 01:24:42.020
they had injected these
monkeys with not MDMA,

01:24:42.660 --> 01:24:45.960
but with methamphetamine, which
is known to be neurotoxic.

01:24:45.960 --> 01:24:49.440
So it was kind of a
public admittance of oops

01:24:49.440 --> 01:24:53.910
or like really big screw up, so oops,

01:24:53.910 --> 01:24:55.380
but never a retraction

01:24:55.380 --> 01:24:59.220
and then never really a
publicly acknowledged correction

01:24:59.220 --> 01:25:01.320
in the popular press.

01:25:01.320 --> 01:25:04.710
So it seems that in the
appropriate dosage range,

01:25:04.710 --> 01:25:07.800
and with these one or two sessions,

01:25:07.800 --> 01:25:11.160
my assumption, and this
again is an assumption,

01:25:11.160 --> 01:25:12.930
tell me if I'm right or wrong here,

01:25:12.930 --> 01:25:17.640
is that MDMA is not neurotoxic
for serotonergic neurons

01:25:17.640 --> 01:25:19.770
at appropriate doses and
with appropriate sourcing,

01:25:19.770 --> 01:25:20.603
et cetera.

01:25:20.603 --> 01:25:23.610
- So it was an interesting study that,

01:25:23.610 --> 01:25:26.790
I think the guy's name is
Halpern, last name's Halpern.

01:25:26.790 --> 01:25:27.780
- [Andrew] Not Casey Halpern.

01:25:27.780 --> 01:25:29.190
- Not, different Halpern.

01:25:29.190 --> 01:25:30.390
I think Joshua Halpern,

01:25:30.390 --> 01:25:32.700
I'm blanking on his first name, but...

01:25:32.700 --> 01:25:34.607
- Casey Halpern was a
guest on this podcast

01:25:34.607 --> 01:25:36.780
and is a former colleague
of ours at Stanford,

01:25:36.780 --> 01:25:39.630
who unfortunately we lost to
University of Pennsylvania

01:25:39.630 --> 01:25:42.240
and maybe someday we'll bring him back.

01:25:42.240 --> 01:25:43.073
- Yeah, that's right.

01:25:43.073 --> 01:25:45.180
So this individual, you know,

01:25:45.180 --> 01:25:48.120
received some NIH funding
to actually, NIDA, you know,

01:25:48.120 --> 01:25:50.070
National Institute for Drug Abuse funding

01:25:50.070 --> 01:25:55.070
to explore individuals of
the Mormon faith in Utah

01:25:57.960 --> 01:26:02.820
who partake in only MDMA.

01:26:02.820 --> 01:26:06.270
So the way this works is

01:26:06.270 --> 01:26:11.270
that MDMA happened kind of after a lot

01:26:12.150 --> 01:26:15.600
of the religious documents were developed.

01:26:15.600 --> 01:26:19.560
And so MDMA isn't on the
prohibited drug list.

01:26:19.560 --> 01:26:20.924
- The banned substance list.

01:26:20.924 --> 01:26:22.167
- [Nolan] The banned substance list.

01:26:22.167 --> 01:26:23.400
- I have some good friends who are LDS.

01:26:23.400 --> 01:26:24.837
- Yeah, great people.

01:26:24.837 --> 01:26:27.090
I do as well, you know,

01:26:27.090 --> 01:26:28.860
just a kind of set of facts, you know.

01:26:28.860 --> 01:26:33.860
And so these folks only
use MDMA but they don't,

01:26:35.220 --> 01:26:36.060
they're not, you know,

01:26:36.060 --> 01:26:37.920
the problem with some people using drugs,

01:26:37.920 --> 01:26:39.900
they're poly substance users, right?

01:26:39.900 --> 01:26:42.330
So you can't say it's the MDMA

01:26:42.330 --> 01:26:44.130
if they've also taken other psychedelics

01:26:44.130 --> 01:26:45.960
and they've taken opiates
and they've taken cocaine,

01:26:45.960 --> 01:26:47.280
and you have this picture

01:26:47.280 --> 01:26:50.916
where you can't really
tease out that problem.

01:26:50.916 --> 01:26:52.320
But with this, right,

01:26:52.320 --> 01:26:55.380
it was just individuals that
were part of the Mormon faith.

01:26:55.380 --> 01:26:58.260
And so they were kind of purist

01:26:58.260 --> 01:27:00.030
in the sense they only used MDMA

01:27:00.030 --> 01:27:01.890
and he confirmed all of that.

01:27:01.890 --> 01:27:04.620
And it was a brilliant study, right?

01:27:04.620 --> 01:27:06.180
Because then he was able to go in

01:27:06.180 --> 01:27:08.190
and look at their cognitive profiles

01:27:08.190 --> 01:27:12.180
versus individuals of the same
geography, the same faith,

01:27:12.180 --> 01:27:15.600
all of that, that
happened to not take MDMA

01:27:15.600 --> 01:27:19.470
and found there were no
neurocognitive differences.

01:27:19.470 --> 01:27:22.740
- So does that mean that
it was not damaging?

01:27:22.740 --> 01:27:23.880
- It was not damaging.

01:27:23.880 --> 01:27:27.780
It's hard to know because to
really do this study well,

01:27:27.780 --> 01:27:29.400
you'd have to track these folks down

01:27:29.400 --> 01:27:32.760
before they ever took
MDMA and do a pre-post

01:27:32.760 --> 01:27:34.680
and compare to people that didn't.

01:27:34.680 --> 01:27:36.900
But, you know, this is
about as good of a study

01:27:36.900 --> 01:27:41.070
as you can do, given the situation,

01:27:41.070 --> 01:27:42.993
to be able to check this out.

01:27:43.980 --> 01:27:45.990
Additionally, when I
was back in Charleston

01:27:45.990 --> 01:27:48.750
and working in the Medical
University of South Carolina,

01:27:48.750 --> 01:27:52.593
one of my mentors there, Dr. Wagner,

01:27:53.670 --> 01:27:56.167
was a neuropsychologist at MUSC

01:27:56.167 --> 01:27:58.290
and he was also the neuropsychologist

01:27:58.290 --> 01:28:00.480
for the early MDMA trials.

01:28:00.480 --> 01:28:03.150
And so he did all the
neurocognitive batteries

01:28:03.150 --> 01:28:05.340
for individuals pre-post

01:28:05.340 --> 01:28:08.400
and similarly did not see any changes

01:28:08.400 --> 01:28:11.310
in neurocognitive profiles
in a negative way.

01:28:11.310 --> 01:28:13.140
And so, you know,

01:28:13.140 --> 01:28:15.660
there's data from experimental
patients receiving this.

01:28:15.660 --> 01:28:18.300
There's data from people
that are chronic users,

01:28:18.300 --> 01:28:20.673
you know, who only take MDMA.

01:28:21.660 --> 01:28:26.490
And that combination of data suggests

01:28:26.490 --> 01:28:30.180
that there's certainly no apparent risk

01:28:30.180 --> 01:28:33.390
in the kind of one to
two to three dose range.

01:28:33.390 --> 01:28:37.470
And it's probably unlikely
that at least, you know,

01:28:37.470 --> 01:28:41.250
modest dose exposure over
a lifetime doesn't appear

01:28:41.250 --> 01:28:46.250
to have a profound neurocognitive
damaging effect, yeah.

01:28:46.650 --> 01:28:47.490
- Interesting.

01:28:47.490 --> 01:28:50.670
Yeah, I know that sourcing
is key and we're here,

01:28:50.670 --> 01:28:54.300
we're talking about clinical
trials where purity is assured.

01:28:54.300 --> 01:28:55.350
And you know,

01:28:55.350 --> 01:28:58.140
years ago when so-called
raves were really popular,

01:28:58.140 --> 01:29:00.240
maybe they're still
popular, never been to one,

01:29:00.240 --> 01:29:03.090
so wouldn't know if
they're happening or not.

01:29:03.090 --> 01:29:05.160
That's how in the know I am.

01:29:05.160 --> 01:29:07.680
But it was clear that, you know,

01:29:07.680 --> 01:29:09.090
testing for purity was important

01:29:09.090 --> 01:29:13.417
because sometimes the drugs are made

01:29:14.340 --> 01:29:17.550
such that there are contaminants
like methamphetamine,

01:29:17.550 --> 01:29:20.130
which we know is highly neurotoxic.

01:29:20.130 --> 01:29:22.560
I think that one reason why people think

01:29:22.560 --> 01:29:27.560
that MDMA might be neurotoxic
is the reported drop in energy

01:29:27.660 --> 01:29:30.330
or sort of feeling fatigued
for a few days afterward.

01:29:30.330 --> 01:29:32.220
I spoke to a physician colleague of ours

01:29:32.220 --> 01:29:35.790
who said that that very
likely has something to do

01:29:35.790 --> 01:29:40.110
with the surge in prolactin
that arrives subsequent

01:29:40.110 --> 01:29:43.530
to the big dopamine surge
that occurs in MDMA.

01:29:43.530 --> 01:29:46.770
And I mention that because I
know a number of people talk

01:29:46.770 --> 01:29:50.340
about serotonin depletion
after taking MDMA.

01:29:50.340 --> 01:29:52.770
He has it in mind that
while that could be true,

01:29:52.770 --> 01:29:54.990
it's likely that anytime
somebody takes something

01:29:54.990 --> 01:29:57.990
or does something where there's
a huge lift in dopamine,

01:29:57.990 --> 01:30:00.780
that there's very likely a
huge compensatory increase

01:30:00.780 --> 01:30:01.890
in prolactin that follows

01:30:01.890 --> 01:30:04.110
and prolactin has a
kind of sedative effect,

01:30:04.110 --> 01:30:06.930
numbing effect on mood
and libido, et cetera,

01:30:06.930 --> 01:30:08.790
that eventually also wears off.

01:30:08.790 --> 01:30:10.710
Does that make sense
to you as a physician?

01:30:10.710 --> 01:30:11.543
- Yeah, it makes sense.

01:30:11.543 --> 01:30:12.376
I mean, you know,

01:30:12.376 --> 01:30:15.600
the difference between,
say, MDMA and psilocybin is

01:30:15.600 --> 01:30:17.850
that MDMA is kind of an
amphetamine of sorts, right?

01:30:17.850 --> 01:30:20.460
So it has effects in dopamine

01:30:20.460 --> 01:30:24.510
and psilocybin's, you
know, pretty neutral,

01:30:24.510 --> 01:30:26.760
and you know, maybe a little
bit of dopamine effects,

01:30:26.760 --> 01:30:30.420
but kind of much more of a
serotonergic focused drug.

01:30:30.420 --> 01:30:31.650
And so yeah,

01:30:31.650 --> 01:30:34.170
I think you're going to see kind
of a different profile after.

01:30:34.170 --> 01:30:35.760
And that makes, I
haven't heard that story,

01:30:35.760 --> 01:30:37.310
but that makes sense to me too.

01:30:38.250 --> 01:30:40.020
- Since you mentioned psilocybin,
let's talk a little bit

01:30:40.020 --> 01:30:41.193
about the neurochemistry of psilocybin.

01:30:41.193 --> 01:30:45.210
As a serotonergic agent, my
understanding is it operates

01:30:45.210 --> 01:30:50.070
on these, is it the 5HT
serotonin 2C receptor?

01:30:50.070 --> 01:30:51.350
- 2A.
- 2A, excuse me.

01:30:51.350 --> 01:30:53.370
2A and receptors.

01:30:53.370 --> 01:30:55.620
And that I've seen a
bunch of different reports

01:30:55.620 --> 01:30:57.420
in terms of what it's
actually doing to the brain

01:30:57.420 --> 01:30:59.610
while people are under
the effects of the drug.

01:30:59.610 --> 01:31:01.620
And this is important
for us to segment out

01:31:01.620 --> 01:31:03.240
because there are the effects that happen

01:31:03.240 --> 01:31:04.650
while people are under the influence

01:31:04.650 --> 01:31:06.900
and then the more long lasting effects.

01:31:06.900 --> 01:31:09.480
But some of the effects I've
heard about are, for instance,

01:31:09.480 --> 01:31:11.780
and tell me again if
these are right or wrong,

01:31:13.020 --> 01:31:18.020
that there is increased
activation of lateral connection,

01:31:18.090 --> 01:31:21.030
sort of broader areas of
the brain being coactive

01:31:21.030 --> 01:31:23.040
than would normally occur.

01:31:23.040 --> 01:31:25.500
Maybe that explains some of
the synesthesias, you know,

01:31:25.500 --> 01:31:27.900
seeing sounds and hearing colors

01:31:27.900 --> 01:31:29.550
and that as the trivial example,

01:31:29.550 --> 01:31:33.453
but rule breaking within the mind.

01:31:34.290 --> 01:31:38.760
But then I've also heard that
perhaps it's lack of gating

01:31:38.760 --> 01:31:39.660
of sensory input.

01:31:39.660 --> 01:31:41.250
So normally if I'm looking at something,

01:31:41.250 --> 01:31:43.860
I'm not thinking about the
sensation in my right toe

01:31:43.860 --> 01:31:46.080
unless it's relevant.

01:31:46.080 --> 01:31:48.000
But if I'm thinking about the
sensation in my right toe,

01:31:48.000 --> 01:31:48.870
I'm generally not thinking

01:31:48.870 --> 01:31:49.950
about the truck around the corner.

01:31:49.950 --> 01:31:51.780
So we have these attentional spotlights,

01:31:51.780 --> 01:31:56.000
but that somehow it creates
a more, it adds spotlights.

01:31:56.000 --> 01:31:57.383
- Yeah, degates the thalamus.

01:31:57.383 --> 01:31:58.950
- Degates the thalamus, right,

01:31:58.950 --> 01:32:01.560
through the particular thalamic structure.

01:32:01.560 --> 01:32:05.610
So what is the evidence
that any of that is true?

01:32:05.610 --> 01:32:07.560
And are there other phenomena?

01:32:07.560 --> 01:32:09.750
Is there involvement of
dorsolateral prefrontal cortex

01:32:09.750 --> 01:32:10.860
that we are aware of?

01:32:10.860 --> 01:32:13.773
And what I'm really headed
here in a few minutes is,

01:32:14.670 --> 01:32:16.440
you know, is there a place

01:32:16.440 --> 01:32:19.590
for combining directed
stimulation of the brain

01:32:19.590 --> 01:32:21.630
with psychedelics so that the effects

01:32:21.630 --> 01:32:26.250
of serotonin could be
primarily within the structures

01:32:26.250 --> 01:32:29.610
that you know from your work
to be relevant to depression.

01:32:29.610 --> 01:32:31.110
So, but to simplify it first,

01:32:31.110 --> 01:32:33.300
what's going on when one takes psilocybin

01:32:33.300 --> 01:32:36.780
and why is it interesting
in light of depression?

01:32:36.780 --> 01:32:37.613
- Yeah, definitely.

01:32:37.613 --> 01:32:42.613
So David Nutt and Robin
Carhart-Harris' work

01:32:42.660 --> 01:32:44.883
around neuroimaging
psychedelics are kind of

01:32:44.883 --> 01:32:47.580
some of the first folks to do that work.

01:32:47.580 --> 01:32:50.130
And to their great surprise,

01:32:50.130 --> 01:32:52.860
they thought there was going
to be an increase in activity

01:32:52.860 --> 01:32:55.020
on psychedelics and what they
found is the opposite, right?

01:32:55.020 --> 01:32:57.060
There's kind of an overall decrease

01:32:57.060 --> 01:33:00.930
in the level of activity in
the brain with psychedelics,

01:33:00.930 --> 01:33:03.690
but they've also looked at connectivity

01:33:03.690 --> 01:33:06.270
and there's this kind of
small world, you know,

01:33:06.270 --> 01:33:08.880
large world connectivity
that you think about.

01:33:08.880 --> 01:33:10.920
And so, you know, small world meaning

01:33:10.920 --> 01:33:13.860
there's kind of a much
more kind of focused

01:33:13.860 --> 01:33:15.000
kind of cortical function

01:33:15.000 --> 01:33:17.670
or, you know, sub-cortical
function or whatever it is.

01:33:17.670 --> 01:33:20.443
And what you see is a difference

01:33:20.443 --> 01:33:25.020
in that level of engagement
of brain regions,

01:33:25.020 --> 01:33:27.240
the connectivity, kind
of global connectivity,

01:33:27.240 --> 01:33:29.370
to your point, kind of increases.

01:33:29.370 --> 01:33:32.513
And so, you know, it's
interesting, you know,

01:33:32.513 --> 01:33:36.090
I think to kind of have a
conversion theory on this.

01:33:36.090 --> 01:33:38.700
It's still, you know, to be determined.

01:33:38.700 --> 01:33:41.430
There's still a lot of work I
think that needs to be done.

01:33:41.430 --> 01:33:44.850
But it's certainly suggestive

01:33:44.850 --> 01:33:49.260
that there's pretty profound
changes in brain activity

01:33:49.260 --> 01:33:51.390
and brain connectivity after.

01:33:51.390 --> 01:33:53.823
And what we've found to
be really interesting is

01:33:53.823 --> 01:33:58.800
that the anti-depressant
effects of psilocybin have

01:33:58.800 --> 01:34:02.430
a particular connectivity change

01:34:02.430 --> 01:34:05.940
that we also see with our
TMS approaches, right?

01:34:05.940 --> 01:34:07.740
And it's this connectivity

01:34:07.740 --> 01:34:10.080
between the subgenual anterior cingulate

01:34:10.080 --> 01:34:11.940
and the default mode network.

01:34:11.940 --> 01:34:13.140
And so when we do

01:34:13.140 --> 01:34:16.800
this effective Stanford
neuromodulation therapy stimulation,

01:34:16.800 --> 01:34:20.340
we see a down regulation, the
connectivity between them,

01:34:20.340 --> 01:34:22.170
negatively balanced mood state

01:34:22.170 --> 01:34:23.880
in the case of depressed individuals

01:34:23.880 --> 01:34:26.310
and the self-representation of the brain.

01:34:26.310 --> 01:34:29.460
And you see that same
connectivity change occur

01:34:29.460 --> 01:34:31.860
post-psilocybin, you know,

01:34:31.860 --> 01:34:33.930
suggesting there's a convergent mechanism

01:34:33.930 --> 01:34:34.830
and it makes sense, right?

01:34:34.830 --> 01:34:35.790
You've kind of got

01:34:35.790 --> 01:34:39.330
an overconnected,
negatively balanced system,

01:34:39.330 --> 01:34:43.080
conflict system that's kind of attached

01:34:43.080 --> 01:34:47.220
onto the self representation
and people feel stuck, right?

01:34:47.220 --> 01:34:49.890
And then when you do whatever
you do that's effective,

01:34:49.890 --> 01:34:53.190
it unpairs those two systems.

01:34:53.190 --> 01:34:57.240
- I want to ask you about this
phenomenon I've heard about

01:34:57.240 --> 01:34:58.500
during psilocybin journeys.

01:34:58.500 --> 01:35:00.870
I heard about this from
Dr. Matthew Johnson,

01:35:00.870 --> 01:35:04.890
who's running a lot of the
clinical trials at Johns Hopkins

01:35:04.890 --> 01:35:07.230
and has been a guest on this podcast.

01:35:07.230 --> 01:35:11.310
He said that there's something
seems to be important

01:35:11.310 --> 01:35:13.740
about the patient who's depressed

01:35:13.740 --> 01:35:16.920
or who's under the influence of psilocybin

01:35:16.920 --> 01:35:19.230
or the patient who's
trying to get over smoking

01:35:19.230 --> 01:35:21.450
or an eating disorder
who's taking psilocybin

01:35:21.450 --> 01:35:22.500
and is in the clinic.

01:35:23.550 --> 01:35:24.720
That there's something important

01:35:24.720 --> 01:35:27.450
to this notion of letting go,

01:35:27.450 --> 01:35:30.600
that people will feel as if their thoughts

01:35:30.600 --> 01:35:31.433
and their feelings

01:35:31.433 --> 01:35:33.852
and maybe even their body
aren't under their control,

01:35:33.852 --> 01:35:36.780
and that the clinicians' job
under those circumstances is

01:35:36.780 --> 01:35:38.400
of course to make sure that
they're physically safe

01:35:38.400 --> 01:35:40.290
so they don't jump out a window or try...

01:35:40.290 --> 01:35:42.930
Actually give an example of
a patient who thought that,

01:35:42.930 --> 01:35:43.763
I think it was a she,

01:35:43.763 --> 01:35:46.020
could move into the painting in the wall,

01:35:46.020 --> 01:35:48.630
and obviously that wasn't
true in the real world,

01:35:48.630 --> 01:35:49.890
although it was true in her mind.

01:35:49.890 --> 01:35:51.900
So they prevented her from doing that.

01:35:51.900 --> 01:35:53.190
But that letting go,

01:35:53.190 --> 01:35:58.110
that somehow untethering
from the autonomic arousal

01:35:58.110 --> 01:35:59.853
that's occurring is important.

01:36:01.050 --> 01:36:03.810
Which brings us back to this
idea or me back to this idea

01:36:03.810 --> 01:36:06.390
of like a seesaw where
you're sort of letting go

01:36:06.390 --> 01:36:09.570
of the hinge and just sort of,
your heart rate's going up,

01:36:09.570 --> 01:36:11.880
like just go with it and trust, you know?

01:36:11.880 --> 01:36:13.890
Your heart rate's going down,
just go with it and trust.

01:36:13.890 --> 01:36:16.950
You're thinking about
something very powerful

01:36:16.950 --> 01:36:19.230
and depressing related to your childhood,

01:36:19.230 --> 01:36:21.570
you're just supposed to
go there without fear.

01:36:21.570 --> 01:36:23.190
You're thinking about what's possible

01:36:23.190 --> 01:36:24.420
in terms of what could happen.

01:36:24.420 --> 01:36:27.033
So anyway, you get the picture.

01:36:28.890 --> 01:36:29.760
Can we think of that

01:36:29.760 --> 01:36:34.760
as just the willingness to do
a million different variations

01:36:35.130 --> 01:36:36.930
on the emotional Stroop task?

01:36:36.930 --> 01:36:41.190
You know, you'll entertain
the full array of rules

01:36:41.190 --> 01:36:43.350
within your head and consider them.

01:36:43.350 --> 01:36:45.150
Or is there something more to it?

01:36:45.150 --> 01:36:46.606
You know, and again,

01:36:46.606 --> 01:36:50.130
we're in the outer margins
of understanding here,

01:36:50.130 --> 01:36:53.580
but what are your thoughts
on this notion of letting go

01:36:53.580 --> 01:36:57.240
as such a key variable
for relief from depression

01:36:57.240 --> 01:36:58.470
during the psychedelic journey?

01:36:58.470 --> 01:37:00.120
- Yeah, so I'll talk a little bit

01:37:00.120 --> 01:37:01.350
about something called exposure

01:37:01.350 --> 01:37:02.820
and response prevention therapy,

01:37:02.820 --> 01:37:06.450
that's a typical kind of gold
standard treatment for OCD,

01:37:06.450 --> 01:37:10.350
and I'll help this a
little bit conceptually.

01:37:10.350 --> 01:37:14.250
And so what that really is,
it's a letting go therapy.

01:37:14.250 --> 01:37:18.090
And so, you know, exposure
response prevention,

01:37:18.090 --> 01:37:22.290
the idea is that you have
to expose the individual

01:37:22.290 --> 01:37:24.840
to something that, you know,

01:37:24.840 --> 01:37:27.000
something that triggers an obsession

01:37:27.000 --> 01:37:30.477
that they then want to do
whatever the compulsion is, right?

01:37:30.477 --> 01:37:31.680
And so I'll give you, you know,

01:37:31.680 --> 01:37:35.100
my first exposure and
response prevention patient

01:37:35.100 --> 01:37:36.690
when I was a resident,

01:37:36.690 --> 01:37:41.690
he was very concerned about
leaving the lights on his car.

01:37:42.300 --> 01:37:45.250
And so what we did is we went out

01:37:46.494 --> 01:37:50.580
and we turned the lights on in
his car and locked his door.

01:37:50.580 --> 01:37:51.810
So his lights were on,

01:37:51.810 --> 01:37:54.960
and he was super worried, this
is going to kill his battery.

01:37:54.960 --> 01:37:57.987
And we went and we spent an
hour talking about things,

01:37:57.987 --> 01:38:02.040
and we went back out to his
car and his battery was fine,

01:38:02.040 --> 01:38:03.840
and his lights were on.

01:38:03.840 --> 01:38:08.010
And he cranked the car and we
did it maybe one other time,

01:38:08.010 --> 01:38:10.950
and then all of a sudden
that was gone, right?

01:38:10.950 --> 01:38:12.780
And that's the idea is that, you know,

01:38:12.780 --> 01:38:14.670
you're essentially exposing.

01:38:14.670 --> 01:38:16.080
And you want to do it at levels

01:38:16.080 --> 01:38:20.100
that are, from an anxiety
standpoint, tolerable,

01:38:20.100 --> 01:38:22.080
but exposing the person to something

01:38:22.080 --> 01:38:24.000
and then letting them see

01:38:24.000 --> 01:38:27.570
that that exposure ends
up being fine, right?

01:38:27.570 --> 01:38:29.250
It ends up not causing the thing

01:38:29.250 --> 01:38:31.383
that they end up being worried about.

01:38:32.760 --> 01:38:34.950
And so, you know, in some sense,

01:38:34.950 --> 01:38:36.870
being in the psychedelic state,

01:38:36.870 --> 01:38:40.440
and we are all taught at a level

01:38:40.440 --> 01:38:43.290
to retain some level of control.

01:38:43.290 --> 01:38:46.770
You know, people have
more or less of that,

01:38:46.770 --> 01:38:50.580
but we're all effectively
retaining some level of control.

01:38:50.580 --> 01:38:52.710
We all wake up in the
morning and put clothes on

01:38:52.710 --> 01:38:53.820
to go into society.

01:38:53.820 --> 01:38:55.890
We all try to say, you know,

01:38:55.890 --> 01:38:58.710
most people try to say the right things.

01:38:58.710 --> 01:39:00.000
They don't try to do things

01:39:00.000 --> 01:39:02.310
that are outside of cultural norms

01:39:02.310 --> 01:39:03.690
when they're in conversation.

01:39:03.690 --> 01:39:05.850
And so we're constantly

01:39:05.850 --> 01:39:08.820
at some level controlling
the situation that we're in.

01:39:08.820 --> 01:39:11.040
And so it's, you know, it's not,

01:39:11.040 --> 01:39:14.190
it makes a lot of sense
that in that state,

01:39:14.190 --> 01:39:16.110
part of the therapeutic effect

01:39:16.110 --> 01:39:19.590
that may be linked to the
neural circuitry is this idea

01:39:19.590 --> 01:39:24.480
of letting go and essentially
letting the system, you know,

01:39:24.480 --> 01:39:27.333
the network configuration
maybe, whatever it is,

01:39:28.230 --> 01:39:29.700
assume a state

01:39:29.700 --> 01:39:33.570
that you've essentially been
fighting the whole time.

01:39:33.570 --> 01:39:38.570
The same way that my OCD
patient was fighting this need

01:39:39.210 --> 01:39:44.210
to click the off button on
the lights of his car 50 times

01:39:44.790 --> 01:39:48.330
before he would go and do
whatever he needed to do.

01:39:48.330 --> 01:39:50.070
And in some level, letting go there,

01:39:50.070 --> 01:39:52.230
meaning letting us just turn the lights on

01:39:52.230 --> 01:39:54.180
and him not do anything,

01:39:54.180 --> 01:39:56.280
or letting go meaning in
the psychedelic state,

01:39:56.280 --> 01:39:57.840
you're just letting go

01:39:57.840 --> 01:39:59.940
of whatever it is you're holding onto,

01:39:59.940 --> 01:40:03.210
negatively balanced
thoughts about yourself

01:40:03.210 --> 01:40:05.880
in the setting of having depression

01:40:05.880 --> 01:40:09.653
or, you know, re-experiencing
a trauma memory

01:40:11.280 --> 01:40:13.170
and allowing that to just happen

01:40:13.170 --> 01:40:16.083
and seeing it again
through a different light.

01:40:17.040 --> 01:40:18.080
You know, it feels the same

01:40:18.080 --> 01:40:21.780
in the sense that that's
allowing for whatever's going on

01:40:21.780 --> 01:40:24.900
with these psychedelic states
to do whatever they do.

01:40:24.900 --> 01:40:26.040
- It's fascinating.

01:40:26.040 --> 01:40:28.457
You said it's exposure response therapy is

01:40:28.457 --> 01:40:29.290
the traditional name?

01:40:29.290 --> 01:40:31.030
- [Nolan] Exposure response
prevention therapy.

01:40:31.030 --> 01:40:31.863
- Prevention therapy.

01:40:31.863 --> 01:40:34.560
Done outside of the psychedelic journey.

01:40:34.560 --> 01:40:36.390
- It's done outside the
psychedelic journey.

01:40:36.390 --> 01:40:40.740
But that idea of letting go
is present in both of those.

01:40:40.740 --> 01:40:44.340
You know, psychotherapy
kind of straight up,

01:40:44.340 --> 01:40:46.530
totally sober, non psychedelic,

01:40:46.530 --> 01:40:48.990
non anything psycho manualized,

01:40:48.990 --> 01:40:52.440
that psychotherapy that we
know works really well for OCD.

01:40:52.440 --> 01:40:54.870
And then, you know, in
that psychedelic state,

01:40:54.870 --> 01:40:58.590
and so people have done
studies with psilocybin,

01:40:58.590 --> 01:41:01.020
and now there's some studies
with MDMA trying to look

01:41:01.020 --> 01:41:04.440
at treating OCD, you know,

01:41:04.440 --> 01:41:08.130
with this same sort of
idea of letting go, right?

01:41:08.130 --> 01:41:11.340
And how do you have an OCD
patient kind of let go?

01:41:11.340 --> 01:41:14.490
Maybe even letting go of not
washing their hands anymore,

01:41:14.490 --> 01:41:16.680
you know, kind of accepting the idea

01:41:16.680 --> 01:41:18.810
they're not going to
get germs in their hands

01:41:18.810 --> 01:41:19.727
or whatever it is, you know?

01:41:19.727 --> 01:41:21.750
And so it's kind of part and parcel,

01:41:21.750 --> 01:41:23.790
that same sort of thinking.

01:41:23.790 --> 01:41:24.630
- When I was in college,

01:41:24.630 --> 01:41:27.000
I developed a compulsive superstition.

01:41:27.000 --> 01:41:28.110
I'm not afraid to admit this.

01:41:28.110 --> 01:41:32.010
I somehow developed a
knock on wood superstition.

01:41:32.010 --> 01:41:35.670
And I was actually kind of ashamed of it

01:41:35.670 --> 01:41:38.430
because it rationally made no sense.

01:41:38.430 --> 01:41:40.590
I don't consider myself
a superstitious person,

01:41:40.590 --> 01:41:42.660
never was a superstitious kid.

01:41:42.660 --> 01:41:44.619
You know, I'd step on the sidewalk cracks,

01:41:44.619 --> 01:41:46.677
I'd walk under ladders, you know,

01:41:46.677 --> 01:41:49.710
I'd probably even try
to walk under a ladder,

01:41:49.710 --> 01:41:52.080
even though I don't suggest it.

01:41:52.080 --> 01:41:53.940
But somehow I picked this thing up

01:41:53.940 --> 01:41:57.090
and I used to sneak it at times.

01:41:57.090 --> 01:41:58.620
I told my girlfriend at
the time that I had it

01:41:58.620 --> 01:42:01.560
in hopes that that would
prevent me from doing it.

01:42:01.560 --> 01:42:02.610
And it's tricky.

01:42:02.610 --> 01:42:04.560
Sometimes it actually comes
back where I think, gosh,

01:42:04.560 --> 01:42:06.150
I didn't say, you know, knock on wood,

01:42:06.150 --> 01:42:08.675
I didn't knock on wood, I hope
that doesn't actually happen.

01:42:08.675 --> 01:42:11.400
And it's quote unquote crazy, right?

01:42:11.400 --> 01:42:14.430
But crazy in the sense that
it makes no sense rationally

01:42:14.430 --> 01:42:16.290
why the events would be linked.

01:42:16.290 --> 01:42:18.510
And yet I think a lot of
people out there do have

01:42:18.510 --> 01:42:21.030
internal superstitions.

01:42:21.030 --> 01:42:23.820
Maybe by talking about
it now, it'll go away.

01:42:23.820 --> 01:42:25.675
Clearly I just need to challenge it.

01:42:25.675 --> 01:42:29.460
You know, anyway, I mention
it because I consider myself,

01:42:29.460 --> 01:42:31.080
you know, generally rational person,

01:42:31.080 --> 01:42:35.940
but it's interesting how these
motor patterns get activated

01:42:35.940 --> 01:42:37.620
and this notion of letting go,

01:42:37.620 --> 01:42:41.400
because I don't actually
know what consequence I fear.

01:42:41.400 --> 01:42:43.627
And the fear, as I was
hearing the example you gave,

01:42:43.627 --> 01:42:45.570
you know, the fear of the
car battery running down,

01:42:45.570 --> 01:42:46.403
I was about to say,

01:42:46.403 --> 01:42:48.000
"Well, what if the battery
actually did run out?"

01:42:48.000 --> 01:42:49.770
Then the therapy would be undermined.

01:42:49.770 --> 01:42:51.840
And yet that could also
be interesting too,

01:42:51.840 --> 01:42:53.100
because it's not that big of a deal.

01:42:53.100 --> 01:42:54.420
You jump the car.

01:42:54.420 --> 01:42:55.253
But in my case,

01:42:55.253 --> 01:42:58.230
I need to think about
what the ultimate fear is.

01:42:58.230 --> 01:43:01.140
- Yeah, and you know, I
think a lot of people,

01:43:01.140 --> 01:43:04.530
so it's interesting if you
look at, say, the OCD scale

01:43:04.530 --> 01:43:06.480
or the depression scale or whatever,

01:43:06.480 --> 01:43:08.940
we don't define normal as zero.

01:43:08.940 --> 01:43:13.940
We define normal as
some number range above.

01:43:14.070 --> 01:43:16.320
So zero to, in the case

01:43:16.320 --> 01:43:19.560
of the Montgomery-Asberg
Depression Rating Scale,

01:43:19.560 --> 01:43:22.410
one of the depression
scales we use, 10, right?

01:43:22.410 --> 01:43:23.640
That's the normal range.

01:43:23.640 --> 01:43:26.250
And so people could have some sadness

01:43:26.250 --> 01:43:27.720
and still be considered normal.

01:43:27.720 --> 01:43:30.330
In the case of the OCD scale,
it's about the same 10, right?

01:43:30.330 --> 01:43:33.930
Where we say it's kind of
starts to be, you know,

01:43:33.930 --> 01:43:36.030
mildly abnormal or something.

01:43:36.030 --> 01:43:38.107
And I'd always tell the medical students,

01:43:38.107 --> 01:43:40.800
"Look, my friends that
are surf instructors,

01:43:40.800 --> 01:43:42.780
they're more like a zero on the Y bar.

01:43:42.780 --> 01:43:46.260
People that are professionals,
you know, they're non-zero,

01:43:46.260 --> 01:43:48.810
but it's still within the normal range."

01:43:48.810 --> 01:43:49.980
And especially, you know,

01:43:49.980 --> 01:43:51.960
in the case that you're talking about,

01:43:51.960 --> 01:43:53.550
it doesn't sound like it got in your way.

01:43:53.550 --> 01:43:54.383
It doesn't sound, I mean,

01:43:54.383 --> 01:43:57.570
you're obviously highly
successful tenured professor

01:43:57.570 --> 01:44:00.963
at Stanford and do all the
great things that you do.

01:44:02.151 --> 01:44:05.220
And so it's very much kind
of within the normal range,

01:44:05.220 --> 01:44:09.630
and I think totally assumed

01:44:09.630 --> 01:44:12.150
that a lot of people have
these sorts of things.

01:44:12.150 --> 01:44:15.870
And as long, I think something
as a psychiatric diagnosis

01:44:15.870 --> 01:44:19.440
when it severely impairs
your ability to function

01:44:19.440 --> 01:44:22.710
and that's when we kind
of cross that threshold.

01:44:22.710 --> 01:44:25.650
But, you know, I think
that a lot of people,

01:44:25.650 --> 01:44:27.000
and it's great that
you're bringing this up.

01:44:27.000 --> 01:44:28.740
I mean, it's very anti-stigmatizing

01:44:28.740 --> 01:44:29.580
that you're bringing up, right?

01:44:29.580 --> 01:44:32.280
Because I think a lot of
people hold that stuff in

01:44:32.280 --> 01:44:35.310
and they don't want to talk
about it because they're worried

01:44:35.310 --> 01:44:36.990
that somebody else may think something.

01:44:36.990 --> 01:44:39.420
But the reality is, as a psychiatrist,

01:44:39.420 --> 01:44:41.430
I talk to a lot of patients,

01:44:41.430 --> 01:44:43.860
a lot of people that are,
you know, family members,

01:44:43.860 --> 01:44:46.170
you know, folks that are just going

01:44:46.170 --> 01:44:48.849
through a death in the
family, whatever it is.

01:44:48.849 --> 01:44:49.710
And what you figure out is like,

01:44:49.710 --> 01:44:52.380
everybody's got a little
something here and there.

01:44:52.380 --> 01:44:55.200
Everybody has the knock in
some way, if that makes sense.

01:44:55.200 --> 01:44:59.640
And it's just, and we're all
just kind of more predisposed

01:44:59.640 --> 01:45:00.630
not to talk about it.

01:45:00.630 --> 01:45:02.790
But I think it's
important to talk about it

01:45:02.790 --> 01:45:05.310
because I think that when we
start all talking about it,

01:45:05.310 --> 01:45:08.220
then we realize that we're
all kind of in this together

01:45:08.220 --> 01:45:09.120
in a way.

01:45:09.120 --> 01:45:14.120
And then some folks that
have to knock 100 times,

01:45:14.910 --> 01:45:17.520
we call that OCD, you know,

01:45:17.520 --> 01:45:20.370
and they're worried about germs
and all these other things.

01:45:20.370 --> 01:45:21.990
We call that OCD.

01:45:21.990 --> 01:45:24.450
And then in that circumstance, you know,

01:45:24.450 --> 01:45:25.680
they need treatment, right?

01:45:25.680 --> 01:45:27.300
But it is really on,

01:45:27.300 --> 01:45:29.460
just like blood sugar,
just like blood pressure,

01:45:29.460 --> 01:45:30.990
it's on a range, you know,

01:45:30.990 --> 01:45:33.090
and it's not just these
discreet diagnoses.

01:45:33.090 --> 01:45:34.800
You have them or you don't.

01:45:34.800 --> 01:45:36.366
- It's good to know.

01:45:36.366 --> 01:45:37.793
I actually feel some
relief just hearing this,

01:45:37.793 --> 01:45:39.870
because I am slightly...

01:45:39.870 --> 01:45:41.790
I wouldn't say ashamed as
sort of embarrassed by it,

01:45:41.790 --> 01:45:46.110
but I offer it as a, you know,

01:45:46.110 --> 01:45:48.180
it is what it is, as they say.

01:45:48.180 --> 01:45:52.530
And it certainly doesn't
seem to hinder my life much,

01:45:52.530 --> 01:45:54.089
knock on wood.

01:45:54.089 --> 01:45:54.922
- [Nolan] [laughs] Nice.

01:45:54.922 --> 01:45:58.088
- So if we could talk
a bit about ibogaine.

01:45:58.088 --> 01:46:00.510
I don't know much about ibogaine,

01:46:00.510 --> 01:46:02.970
although anytime I hear
the, you know, A-I-N-E,

01:46:02.970 --> 01:46:05.770
you know, lidocaine, ibogaine,
I think of an anesthetic.

01:46:07.410 --> 01:46:08.520
And going to the dentist,

01:46:08.520 --> 01:46:11.133
which is an unpleasant
experience for me, generally.

01:46:13.530 --> 01:46:14.703
What is ibogaine?

01:46:15.810 --> 01:46:19.410
Does this have anything to
do with the so-called toad?

01:46:19.410 --> 01:46:22.443
You know, people talk about
smoking frog skin, toad skin.

01:46:24.180 --> 01:46:26.160
What is it used for clinically?

01:46:26.160 --> 01:46:29.613
Is it legal in the US as a clinical tool?

01:46:31.080 --> 01:46:33.453
Who's using it and for what purposes?

01:46:34.380 --> 01:46:35.730
If you could educate me on ibogaine,

01:46:35.730 --> 01:46:37.380
I truly know nothing about it,

01:46:37.380 --> 01:46:39.270
except I think I know how
to spell it correctly.

01:46:39.270 --> 01:46:40.560
- Yeah, that's fair, yeah.

01:46:40.560 --> 01:46:45.513
So ibogaine is one of the alkaloids

01:46:46.380 --> 01:46:51.380
that you can extract from
a iboga tree root bark

01:46:52.140 --> 01:46:57.140
that's typically growing in
the country of Gabon, Africa.

01:46:59.820 --> 01:47:04.820
So Gabon is one of the
West African countries,

01:47:05.130 --> 01:47:09.150
kind of middle of Africa
and on the west coast.

01:47:09.150 --> 01:47:14.107
And Gabon has a group of folks, you know,

01:47:17.550 --> 01:47:19.560
called the Bwiti.

01:47:19.560 --> 01:47:23.520
It's a religious kind of sacramental group

01:47:23.520 --> 01:47:28.520
that sacramentally uses iboga root bark

01:47:28.650 --> 01:47:32.370
as part of the sacrament.

01:47:32.370 --> 01:47:37.370
And they've been using iboga
root bark for a very long time.

01:47:38.945 --> 01:47:41.310
And it's, you know, part of the tradition.

01:47:41.310 --> 01:47:46.140
There's a whole set of
kind of ceremony around it.

01:47:46.140 --> 01:47:47.190
If you're interested in this,

01:47:47.190 --> 01:47:49.170
there's a book called
"Breaking Open the Head"

01:47:49.170 --> 01:47:51.840
by Daniel Pinchbeck that goes through

01:47:51.840 --> 01:47:54.780
and talks about this whole process.

01:47:54.780 --> 01:47:58.110
But essentially the Gabonese
have been using this

01:47:58.110 --> 01:48:03.110
for a long time and it's a kind
of an atypical psychedelic.

01:48:04.216 --> 01:48:09.216
It's not a psychedelic that
we normally think about

01:48:09.480 --> 01:48:10.770
with psilocybin and LSD

01:48:10.770 --> 01:48:13.320
where there are visual
perceptual changes, right?

01:48:13.320 --> 01:48:16.530
So if you take psilocybin or LSD,

01:48:16.530 --> 01:48:19.210
what you experience is you experience

01:48:19.210 --> 01:48:22.710
these kind of visual
perceptual differences

01:48:22.710 --> 01:48:24.117
in the external world, right?

01:48:24.117 --> 01:48:26.520
And on enough LSD or psilocybin,

01:48:26.520 --> 01:48:30.180
an individual can actually
perceive something visually

01:48:30.180 --> 01:48:32.460
in the external world that isn't there,

01:48:32.460 --> 01:48:33.810
as we talked about earlier.

01:48:35.010 --> 01:48:36.510
Ibogaine doesn't do that.

01:48:36.510 --> 01:48:37.920
Ibogaine does something different.

01:48:37.920 --> 01:48:40.680
It's kind of like, have you
ever seen "Minority Report,"

01:48:40.680 --> 01:48:41.970
you know, the movie with Tom Cruise,

01:48:41.970 --> 01:48:44.670
I think 15 or 20 years ago or something?

01:48:44.670 --> 01:48:45.540
So it dates us a little bit,

01:48:45.540 --> 01:48:49.650
but it was this movie where
he would be able to go

01:48:49.650 --> 01:48:51.390
and see these kind of pre crimes.

01:48:51.390 --> 01:48:56.390
And he had this big screen
where he could look at scenes

01:48:56.478 --> 01:49:00.180
from time and like kind of go
through that scene and see it.

01:49:00.180 --> 01:49:03.780
And so what individuals
taking ibogaine will say is

01:49:03.780 --> 01:49:06.210
that open eyes, they don't see anything,

01:49:06.210 --> 01:49:08.760
but closed eyes, they'll go back through

01:49:08.760 --> 01:49:13.110
and re-experience earlier life memories

01:49:13.110 --> 01:49:15.690
and they will be able to experience it

01:49:15.690 --> 01:49:20.690
from a place of empathy,
not only for themselves,

01:49:21.360 --> 01:49:24.810
but from others, and
kind of detached empathy

01:49:24.810 --> 01:49:28.260
and being able to see this
as almost a third party,

01:49:28.260 --> 01:49:30.240
even though they were there.

01:49:30.240 --> 01:49:32.640
But they're able to see it,
you know, as a third party.

01:49:32.640 --> 01:49:36.090
So Claudia Naranjo, a
psychiatrist from Argentina,

01:49:36.090 --> 01:49:38.730
described this for a lot
of books that he wrote,

01:49:38.730 --> 01:49:41.100
in I think the '80s and '90s around this.

01:49:41.100 --> 01:49:44.880
And so, you know, ibogaine's
been around for a long time.

01:49:44.880 --> 01:49:46.380
Howard Lotsof,

01:49:46.380 --> 01:49:49.350
American guy that brought
it over from Africa.

01:49:49.350 --> 01:49:50.790
He was a polysubstance user,

01:49:50.790 --> 01:49:54.570
used every drug that he
could get his hands on,

01:49:54.570 --> 01:49:55.950
took ibogaine,

01:49:55.950 --> 01:49:57.780
and including a lot of other
psychedelics, by the way,

01:49:57.780 --> 01:49:58.613
took ibogaine

01:49:58.613 --> 01:50:00.930
and then never did another
drug again, supposedly

01:50:00.930 --> 01:50:04.500
because he had such a
profound ibogaine experience.

01:50:04.500 --> 01:50:07.620
Ibogaine is in no way a
recreational substance.

01:50:07.620 --> 01:50:09.150
It's not a recreational substance

01:50:09.150 --> 01:50:11.130
if you want it to be a
recreational substance,

01:50:11.130 --> 01:50:13.380
because you're essentially having this,

01:50:13.380 --> 01:50:14.970
what they call life review.

01:50:14.970 --> 01:50:17.610
They also call it 10 years
of psychotherapy in a night.

01:50:17.610 --> 01:50:19.530
So these are the terminology

01:50:19.530 --> 01:50:22.061
that people talk about the issue.

01:50:22.061 --> 01:50:22.894
- How long does it last?

01:50:22.894 --> 01:50:24.300
Is it truly one night?

01:50:24.300 --> 01:50:26.340
- It's usually, you know, it can go,

01:50:26.340 --> 01:50:27.960
depending upon if you
get re-dosed or anything,

01:50:27.960 --> 01:50:31.350
go sometimes, depending upon
how fast you metabolize it,

01:50:31.350 --> 01:50:33.690
sometimes 24, sometimes 36 hours.

01:50:33.690 --> 01:50:37.497
Sometimes it can be shorter,
but it is a long time.

01:50:37.497 --> 01:50:38.330
It's a very long time.

01:50:38.330 --> 01:50:42.060
So it's definitely the longest
acting psychedelic substance

01:50:42.060 --> 01:50:43.290
I know of.

01:50:43.290 --> 01:50:48.290
And so people, you will take this,

01:50:48.750 --> 01:50:52.650
and they'll have this
reevaluation of a given memory.

01:50:52.650 --> 01:50:54.180
And then as we were talking about earlier,

01:50:54.180 --> 01:50:56.280
reconsolidate that memory again,

01:50:56.280 --> 01:50:58.680
and then it seems to
have, you know, an effect

01:50:58.680 --> 01:51:00.510
of that reconsolidation process.

01:51:00.510 --> 01:51:04.710
And so, you know, about
five, four, five years ago,

01:51:04.710 --> 01:51:06.810
I was tapped by Robert Malenka,

01:51:06.810 --> 01:51:10.020
one of the senior
neuroscientists we both know

01:51:10.020 --> 01:51:10.853
in the university.

01:51:10.853 --> 01:51:13.680
And he says, "Well,
there's an unnamed donor

01:51:13.680 --> 01:51:18.410
that's very interested
in funding a scientific,

01:51:19.800 --> 01:51:23.580
kind of open label study
of these Navy SEALs

01:51:23.580 --> 01:51:25.350
that have been going down to Mexico

01:51:25.350 --> 01:51:29.730
and taking ibogaine and also 5-MeO-DMT,"

01:51:29.730 --> 01:51:33.600
which I'll talk about in
a second, to treat PTSD.

01:51:33.600 --> 01:51:36.960
You know, they claim to
have traumatic brain injury,

01:51:36.960 --> 01:51:40.380
depression, you know, that
whole constellation of symptoms.

01:51:40.380 --> 01:51:42.900
You know, and as it was described to me

01:51:42.900 --> 01:51:45.300
by various people that had done this,

01:51:45.300 --> 01:51:48.900
by their spouses and
and whatnot, you know,

01:51:48.900 --> 01:51:50.220
John, we'll just say John,

01:51:50.220 --> 01:51:53.940
John couldn't screw a light bulb

01:51:53.940 --> 01:51:55.862
into a light fixture, right?

01:51:55.862 --> 01:52:00.660
They were just so debilitated
they couldn't do simple tasks,

01:52:00.660 --> 01:52:03.000
what we call activities of daily living.

01:52:03.000 --> 01:52:04.050
And they were coming back

01:52:04.050 --> 01:52:06.930
and having these really
dramatic improvements

01:52:06.930 --> 01:52:10.830
in all aspects of life.

01:52:10.830 --> 01:52:11.940
And so, you know,

01:52:11.940 --> 01:52:15.442
we have over the last
couple of years been able

01:52:15.442 --> 01:52:19.440
to do this first in human,

01:52:19.440 --> 01:52:22.200
kind of full neurobiological clinical

01:52:22.200 --> 01:52:26.820
neurocognitive evaluation
of what ibogaine is doing.

01:52:26.820 --> 01:52:29.430
In this case, in special operations,

01:52:29.430 --> 01:52:32.400
special forces individuals,
former Navy SEALs,

01:52:32.400 --> 01:52:35.550
former Army Rangers, that
kind of crew of folks,

01:52:35.550 --> 01:52:37.230
and look at the pre-post changes

01:52:37.230 --> 01:52:39.570
that their experience to be able

01:52:39.570 --> 01:52:41.130
to totally quantitate all of that.

01:52:41.130 --> 01:52:44.520
And so we've been able to
capture all the clinical scales,

01:52:44.520 --> 01:52:46.590
you know, depression scales, PTSD scales,

01:52:46.590 --> 01:52:48.630
all that standard stuff,
neurocognitive batteries.

01:52:48.630 --> 01:52:51.210
So how does your executive
function work specifically?

01:52:51.210 --> 01:52:53.670
How does your verbal memory, all of that?

01:52:53.670 --> 01:52:55.860
And then neuroimaging and EEG.

01:52:55.860 --> 01:52:59.910
So this will be the first human
study of ibogaine for those.

01:52:59.910 --> 01:53:03.750
And the reason why is because
ibogaine is kind of the...

01:53:03.750 --> 01:53:05.730
Both seemingly the most potent

01:53:05.730 --> 01:53:10.500
and most seemingly, to me at least,

01:53:10.500 --> 01:53:13.110
most powerful psychedelic,

01:53:13.110 --> 01:53:15.570
but the one that has the most risk too,

01:53:15.570 --> 01:53:17.460
because it has a cardiac effect.

01:53:17.460 --> 01:53:19.530
It seems to be that you
can screen people out

01:53:19.530 --> 01:53:22.260
that have risk off of
their electrocardiogram

01:53:22.260 --> 01:53:24.030
and reduce the risk quite a bit.

01:53:24.030 --> 01:53:25.440
And that's what we all did.

01:53:25.440 --> 01:53:29.310
But that's why people haven't
really studied it as much.

01:53:29.310 --> 01:53:32.771
And it isn't as, in addition,

01:53:32.771 --> 01:53:34.530
nobody goes to a rave on ibogaine.

01:53:34.530 --> 01:53:36.033
There's no recreation at all with this.

01:53:36.033 --> 01:53:37.740
- It's not fun.

01:53:37.740 --> 01:53:40.530
- It's, people say that it's relieving,

01:53:40.530 --> 01:53:42.600
but it's hard work, right?

01:53:42.600 --> 01:53:45.840
Because yeah, you're reexamining things.

01:53:45.840 --> 01:53:50.840
And you know, and so then
we see these folks after,

01:53:50.940 --> 01:53:52.110
and I'll tell you, you know,

01:53:52.110 --> 01:53:54.060
we haven't fully analyzed the data yet,

01:53:54.060 --> 01:53:56.790
but I'll tell you that, you know,

01:53:56.790 --> 01:54:00.060
from what my folks are telling
me, it's pretty dramatic.

01:54:00.060 --> 01:54:03.780
You know, people come back and
they're doing a lot better.

01:54:03.780 --> 01:54:04.710
They're doing a lot better.

01:54:04.710 --> 01:54:07.620
And nobody, I'll knock on wood,

01:54:07.620 --> 01:54:12.390
nobody's had any sort
of cardiac issue at all

01:54:12.390 --> 01:54:15.540
in the cohort that we've studied

01:54:15.540 --> 01:54:20.370
and they look a lot better and
they feel a lot better too.

01:54:20.370 --> 01:54:22.650
And they describe these experiences

01:54:22.650 --> 01:54:25.000
of being able to go back
through and, you know,

01:54:26.430 --> 01:54:29.010
soldiers experienced something
called moral injury, right?

01:54:29.010 --> 01:54:32.280
Where maybe they accidentally
blew something up

01:54:32.280 --> 01:54:34.177
and had a kid in it or
something like that.

01:54:34.177 --> 01:54:37.020
You know, if they're in Afghanistan, Iraq,

01:54:37.020 --> 01:54:39.150
maybe, you know, a child died on accident

01:54:39.150 --> 01:54:43.317
or maybe a civilian died
or whatever it was, right?

01:54:43.317 --> 01:54:46.080
And they suffer these moral
injuries as part of the job.

01:54:46.080 --> 01:54:49.440
And it's almost one of the
kind of vocational risks.

01:54:49.440 --> 01:54:53.520
They come back and say that
they've forgiven themselves,

01:54:53.520 --> 01:54:55.530
you know, which is huge, right?

01:54:55.530 --> 01:54:58.530
And part of that is being
able to see themselves

01:54:58.530 --> 01:55:00.930
in a different light and
having empathy finally

01:55:00.930 --> 01:55:02.160
for themselves and being able

01:55:02.160 --> 01:55:05.280
to kind of have that
experience of forgiving.

01:55:05.280 --> 01:55:07.680
And so, very cool.

01:55:07.680 --> 01:55:11.430
The study, you know,
what was happening was

01:55:11.430 --> 01:55:12.360
they were taking ibogaine

01:55:12.360 --> 01:55:14.520
and then taking something
called 5-MeO-DMT.

01:55:14.520 --> 01:55:15.480
People call it the Toad,

01:55:15.480 --> 01:55:19.680
it's the Sonoran River Toad.

01:55:19.680 --> 01:55:21.720
I think it's like you
can find these in Mexico,

01:55:21.720 --> 01:55:23.013
find 'em in Arizona.

01:55:23.940 --> 01:55:28.940
In the back of the toad produces
something called 5-MeO-DMT,

01:55:29.880 --> 01:55:33.030
which is a flavor of DMT

01:55:33.030 --> 01:55:35.970
that produces a particular
psychedelic effect

01:55:35.970 --> 01:55:38.520
also used as a sacrament.

01:55:38.520 --> 01:55:40.590
- Is it dimethyltryptamine?

01:55:40.590 --> 01:55:44.460
- It is a 5-MeO dimethyltryptamine.

01:55:44.460 --> 01:55:46.140
So it's a kind of a dimethyltryptamine

01:55:46.140 --> 01:55:47.790
with a kind of addition to it.

01:55:47.790 --> 01:55:52.790
The deal there is that it lasts
longer than traditional DMT.

01:55:54.805 --> 01:55:56.730
You know, it's like 20
minutes to five, three,

01:55:56.730 --> 01:55:58.560
or whatever kind of thing.

01:55:58.560 --> 01:56:02.580
And so these guys were taking ibogaine

01:56:02.580 --> 01:56:05.850
and then they would take
the 5-MeO-DMT after.

01:56:05.850 --> 01:56:07.986
We had to kind of divorce those two things

01:56:07.986 --> 01:56:08.819
to be able to do the study

01:56:08.819 --> 01:56:10.560
and just understand what
the ibogaine was doing.

01:56:10.560 --> 01:56:11.910
And they'd go back down a month later

01:56:11.910 --> 01:56:14.420
and they'll do the 5-MeO-DMT.

01:56:14.420 --> 01:56:16.350
- So two completely separate sessions.

01:56:16.350 --> 01:56:17.880
- [Nolan] Two completely
separate sessions.

01:56:17.880 --> 01:56:19.380
- And then one quick
question about ibogaine

01:56:19.380 --> 01:56:21.783
before a bit more on 5-MeO-DMT.

01:56:22.620 --> 01:56:25.110
Is the ibogaine journey guided,

01:56:25.110 --> 01:56:26.730
or the person just closes their eyes

01:56:26.730 --> 01:56:27.960
and they just start falling

01:56:27.960 --> 01:56:30.060
into the back catalog of memories?

01:56:30.060 --> 01:56:32.490
- They have a bunch of
preparatory sessions,

01:56:32.490 --> 01:56:34.290
and then they have a
bunch of sessions after

01:56:34.290 --> 01:56:37.560
that they're able to
kind of rehash things.

01:56:37.560 --> 01:56:39.450
During, there's a sitter that sits there

01:56:39.450 --> 01:56:43.350
and kind of sits with
them and helps them out,

01:56:43.350 --> 01:56:48.000
but it's not, it's pretty, the
phenomenon of the drug seems

01:56:48.000 --> 01:56:49.890
to drive a lot of this, right?

01:56:49.890 --> 01:56:51.360
And so a lot of it ends up being

01:56:51.360 --> 01:56:52.800
what we call supportive psychotherapy.

01:56:52.800 --> 01:56:54.330
You're just kind of being there

01:56:54.330 --> 01:56:56.250
and, you know, maybe you're
holding the person's hand,

01:56:56.250 --> 01:56:58.230
maybe you're just saying "I'm here,"

01:56:58.230 --> 01:56:59.370
or maybe whatever it is,

01:56:59.370 --> 01:57:01.530
but you're making sure
they know you're around.

01:57:01.530 --> 01:57:05.190
But there's not really
an interaction per se.

01:57:05.190 --> 01:57:08.100
And then the whole kind
of goal there is just

01:57:08.100 --> 01:57:09.750
to get folks to kind go back through

01:57:09.750 --> 01:57:11.880
and reexamine these memories

01:57:11.880 --> 01:57:15.180
and ultimately look like
they reconsolidate them.

01:57:15.180 --> 01:57:18.000
And you know, it's very interesting.

01:57:18.000 --> 01:57:22.200
I mean, there's this kind
of, as you said earlier,

01:57:22.200 --> 01:57:25.530
Timothy Leary kind of
sociocultural construct

01:57:25.530 --> 01:57:28.530
that ends up being
overlaid over psychedelics.

01:57:28.530 --> 01:57:33.530
And what I think is
that if you rid yourself

01:57:33.660 --> 01:57:36.000
of all of those preconceived notions

01:57:36.000 --> 01:57:37.620
of what it is and isn't,

01:57:37.620 --> 01:57:38.910
and the counterculture movement,

01:57:38.910 --> 01:57:40.843
all that stuff that neither
of us were ever involved in,

01:57:40.843 --> 01:57:43.910
neither of us are ever
partake in, you know,

01:57:43.910 --> 01:57:46.260
as kind of straight scientists
looking at this, right?

01:57:46.260 --> 01:57:47.400
If you can kind of rid yourself

01:57:47.400 --> 01:57:49.980
of all those sociocultural constructions

01:57:49.980 --> 01:57:52.290
and then reexamine this,

01:57:52.290 --> 01:57:54.750
if we just discovered these today,

01:57:54.750 --> 01:57:58.200
we would say that these sorts of drugs are

01:57:58.200 --> 01:57:59.970
a huge breakthrough in psychiatry

01:57:59.970 --> 01:58:02.370
because they allow for us to do a lot

01:58:02.370 --> 01:58:05.850
of the sorts of things we've
been thinking about with SSRIs,

01:58:05.850 --> 01:58:09.120
with psychotherapy, but
kind of combined, right?

01:58:09.120 --> 01:58:12.210
Psychotherapy plus drugs

01:58:12.210 --> 01:58:14.610
in a substance that kind of allows you

01:58:14.610 --> 01:58:16.410
to reexamine these things.

01:58:16.410 --> 01:58:18.255
And so it's interesting.

01:58:18.255 --> 01:58:22.080
There's a lot to do to try
to figure out if that's true,

01:58:22.080 --> 01:58:22.913
you know?

01:58:22.913 --> 01:58:25.200
And I can say that as it stands right now,

01:58:25.200 --> 01:58:28.500
we don't know if that
statement is true, right?

01:58:28.500 --> 01:58:30.450
There's a lot more work
that needs to happen

01:58:30.450 --> 01:58:32.100
for that statement to
be proven to be true.

01:58:32.100 --> 01:58:35.880
But the hypothesis is, if it is true,

01:58:35.880 --> 01:58:39.510
then it's very likely
that this will be seen

01:58:39.510 --> 01:58:41.970
as a breakthrough because it allows you

01:58:41.970 --> 01:58:44.340
to do these sorts of
things that you can't do

01:58:44.340 --> 01:58:46.770
with normal waking consciousness.

01:58:46.770 --> 01:58:50.040
But also why we have to
really think about this.

01:58:50.040 --> 01:58:55.040
And, you know, these drugs
can't be recreational drugs.

01:58:55.290 --> 01:58:58.590
They really shouldn't be
recreational drugs, right?

01:58:58.590 --> 01:59:01.770
They're really too powerful to be used

01:59:01.770 --> 01:59:03.420
in the context of recreation

01:59:03.420 --> 01:59:06.360
because they can put
you into these states.

01:59:06.360 --> 01:59:10.110
And this generation of
psychedelic researchers are

01:59:10.110 --> 01:59:12.245
really clear about that.

01:59:12.245 --> 01:59:14.580
You know, I think the '60s
folks were not clear about that,

01:59:14.580 --> 01:59:16.980
and they felt like there
was this whole kind

01:59:16.980 --> 01:59:19.320
of cultural thing that was going on there.

01:59:19.320 --> 01:59:23.250
But I think this cohort of
individuals really understands

01:59:23.250 --> 01:59:25.650
that in order to really make this happen,

01:59:25.650 --> 01:59:26.790
we have to understand

01:59:26.790 --> 01:59:30.420
that if you need a
prescription for an SSRI,

01:59:30.420 --> 01:59:33.900
which doesn't change your
consciousness a whole lot,

01:59:33.900 --> 01:59:35.730
and we're very worried about that,

01:59:35.730 --> 01:59:38.610
and the doctor has to evaluate
you for that every week,

01:59:38.610 --> 01:59:42.090
that the idea that some of these
substances would go outside

01:59:42.090 --> 01:59:45.570
of very strict medical supervision is

01:59:45.570 --> 01:59:47.040
kind of preposterous actually.

01:59:47.040 --> 01:59:49.770
It's kind of a dumb moment, I think,

01:59:49.770 --> 01:59:51.570
for all of medicine to say,

01:59:51.570 --> 01:59:53.850
look, if we're going to do this right,

01:59:53.850 --> 01:59:56.850
we've got to do it such a
way that's so protected,

01:59:56.850 --> 02:00:00.210
that's so safe, that we
make sure people know

02:00:00.210 --> 02:00:02.070
these things are not recreational

02:00:02.070 --> 02:00:04.170
and they're really for the pure purposes

02:00:04.170 --> 02:00:08.520
of really powerfully changing
cognition for a while

02:00:08.520 --> 02:00:11.730
and letting people have these
what seem to be, you know,

02:00:11.730 --> 02:00:14.363
relatively therapeutic states.

02:00:14.363 --> 02:00:15.720
- I think it's great that
you're doing this study.

02:00:15.720 --> 02:00:19.260
And along the lines of the
sort of the early iterations

02:00:19.260 --> 02:00:22.380
of psychedelics and the
counterculture of the '60s and '70s,

02:00:22.380 --> 02:00:23.580
some of which took place,

02:00:23.580 --> 02:00:24.900
like "One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest"

02:00:24.900 --> 02:00:28.920
I think is actually based
on the Menlo Park VA,

02:00:28.920 --> 02:00:31.503
which is in our neighborhood of Stanford.

02:00:32.940 --> 02:00:34.740
And things are quite a bit different now.

02:00:34.740 --> 02:00:38.220
I know you and I have spent
some time with the operators

02:00:38.220 --> 02:00:40.110
and former operators at an event,

02:00:40.110 --> 02:00:42.090
and last Veterans Day, in fact,

02:00:42.090 --> 02:00:43.860
the so-called Veteran Solutions group

02:00:43.860 --> 02:00:47.010
that's pioneering a lot of
these psychedelic treatments

02:00:47.010 --> 02:00:49.380
for former special operators
and current special operators.

02:00:49.380 --> 02:00:52.170
And what's interesting to me about that is

02:00:52.170 --> 02:00:55.170
in contrast to the counterculture movement

02:00:55.170 --> 02:00:58.650
of the '60s and '70s, that
room was filled with people

02:00:58.650 --> 02:01:01.440
that are very much of a
structure, the military.

02:01:01.440 --> 02:01:02.638
Right?

02:01:02.638 --> 02:01:05.490
So it's no longer considered
left wing, right wing,

02:01:05.490 --> 02:01:06.960
anti-military, pro-military.

02:01:06.960 --> 02:01:09.090
Here this isn't just
about one group of people

02:01:09.090 --> 02:01:10.950
who's exploring
psychedelics as a treatment

02:01:10.950 --> 02:01:13.977
for trauma and PTSD and other things.

02:01:13.977 --> 02:01:15.900
And of course you also have other domains

02:01:15.900 --> 02:01:16.950
of society looking at this.

02:01:16.950 --> 02:01:18.900
And in fact, there were, but
it was really interesting

02:01:18.900 --> 02:01:23.820
because there were both far
left and far right politicians

02:01:23.820 --> 02:01:26.700
at that event up on stage together,

02:01:26.700 --> 02:01:30.300
talking about, in kind of
lighter terms, heart medicine,

02:01:30.300 --> 02:01:32.460
but also talking about
neurobiology and talking.

02:01:32.460 --> 02:01:35.400
It was just fascinating
from the perspective

02:01:35.400 --> 02:01:36.930
of somebody who's trying
to learn about this stuff,

02:01:36.930 --> 02:01:40.320
that psychedelic therapies no longer sit

02:01:40.320 --> 02:01:43.683
within the anti-establishment realm.

02:01:43.683 --> 02:01:47.670
It's independent of all that,

02:01:47.670 --> 02:01:49.650
certainly when people in
the military are adopting it

02:01:49.650 --> 02:01:51.150
as a potential treatment.

02:01:51.150 --> 02:01:52.680
Again, still under exploration,

02:01:52.680 --> 02:01:55.620
but also under exploration
at universities like Stanford

02:01:55.620 --> 02:01:59.010
and Johns Hopkins and UCSF
and University College London

02:01:59.010 --> 02:02:00.630
and on and on.

02:02:00.630 --> 02:02:03.183
Along the lines of tree
barks and toad skins,

02:02:04.110 --> 02:02:06.480
tell me about ayahuasca.

02:02:06.480 --> 02:02:09.630
And as a plant, you know, it's intriguing.

02:02:09.630 --> 02:02:14.630
And is it pro-serotonergic
drug like psilocybin?

02:02:15.750 --> 02:02:19.800
And is it useful for the
same sorts of conditions

02:02:19.800 --> 02:02:21.900
that we've talked about thus far?

02:02:21.900 --> 02:02:24.570
And if you could perhaps
tell me a little bit also

02:02:24.570 --> 02:02:27.000
about the Brazilian prisoner study.

02:02:27.000 --> 02:02:27.900
- Yeah, yeah.

02:02:27.900 --> 02:02:28.740
Definitely.

02:02:28.740 --> 02:02:30.540
Ayahuasca is another psychedelic.

02:02:30.540 --> 02:02:35.540
It's used as a sacrament
in Brazil and in Peru

02:02:36.360 --> 02:02:38.160
and Ecuador, in Columbia.

02:02:38.160 --> 02:02:41.432
So a lot of the South American countries.

02:02:41.432 --> 02:02:46.432
And what they do is they
combine two plants together,

02:02:47.190 --> 02:02:50.970
where one plant of the
two plant combination

02:02:50.970 --> 02:02:53.220
would effectively do nothing,

02:02:53.220 --> 02:02:56.670
but the two plant combination
together is capable

02:02:56.670 --> 02:03:01.620
of producing this very
profound psychedelic effect.

02:03:01.620 --> 02:03:06.620
And what's really kind of
curious is that there are,

02:03:07.200 --> 02:03:08.434
as I understand it,

02:03:08.434 --> 02:03:11.703
10 to 20,000 plant species in the Amazon.

02:03:13.110 --> 02:03:15.754
And somehow, somebody-

02:03:15.754 --> 02:03:16.980
- Someone tried 'em all.

02:03:16.980 --> 02:03:19.440
- Combined these two plants together

02:03:19.440 --> 02:03:21.690
in certain proportionality

02:03:21.690 --> 02:03:24.630
and cooked this for five, 10 hours

02:03:24.630 --> 02:03:27.870
to the point where you cook
out the dimethyltryptamine

02:03:27.870 --> 02:03:28.950
out of one of the plants

02:03:28.950 --> 02:03:32.880
and cook out the reversible
monoamine oxidase inhibitor

02:03:32.880 --> 02:03:35.490
out of the other plant in such a way

02:03:35.490 --> 02:03:40.140
that the reversible monoamine
oxidase inhibitor prevents

02:03:40.140 --> 02:03:43.410
the GI breakdown of the dimethyltryptamine

02:03:43.410 --> 02:03:45.270
in such a way that it's then allowed

02:03:45.270 --> 02:03:49.320
to cross the blood-brain
barrier and get into the brain.

02:03:49.320 --> 02:03:50.760
And if you didn't add

02:03:50.760 --> 02:03:54.570
the reversible monoamine
oxidase inhibitor plant derived

02:03:54.570 --> 02:03:56.490
into this combination,

02:03:56.490 --> 02:03:58.470
then it would never cross the brain.

02:03:58.470 --> 02:04:01.710
If you put people on a standard,

02:04:01.710 --> 02:04:04.500
psychiatry prescribed
monoamine oxidase inhibitor

02:04:04.500 --> 02:04:05.943
that wasn't reversible,

02:04:07.080 --> 02:04:09.990
you'd throw them into
serotonin syndrome, right?

02:04:09.990 --> 02:04:13.350
So this kind of like sweet spot

02:04:13.350 --> 02:04:16.820
that somehow ayahuasca
practitioners have found

02:04:16.820 --> 02:04:21.420
of being able to get DMT into
the brain from an oral source

02:04:21.420 --> 02:04:24.840
with this combination of a
monoamine oxidase inhibitor

02:04:24.840 --> 02:04:25.743
is curious.

02:04:27.060 --> 02:04:29.790
And so that substance has been explored

02:04:29.790 --> 02:04:31.530
as an antidepressant agent,

02:04:31.530 --> 02:04:33.840
and some studies have looked at that.

02:04:33.840 --> 02:04:35.910
It also seems to be very safe.

02:04:35.910 --> 02:04:40.140
There's a psychiatrist down at UCLA Harbor

02:04:40.140 --> 02:04:41.820
who's done a lot of work with this,

02:04:41.820 --> 02:04:44.610
where he's looked at children even

02:04:44.610 --> 02:04:48.270
that have been exposed to kind
of small doses of ayahuasca

02:04:48.270 --> 02:04:52.110
as kind of a sacrament
within Amazonian tribes

02:04:52.110 --> 02:04:53.730
and found no neurocognitive effects,

02:04:53.730 --> 02:04:56.130
no neurocognitive effects in adults.

02:04:56.130 --> 02:04:59.253
And so it appears to be safe.

02:05:00.120 --> 02:05:05.010
It's kind of part and brought
into various religions,

02:05:05.010 --> 02:05:08.460
including kind of merged with
Catholicism in South America,

02:05:08.460 --> 02:05:10.230
which is kind of very interesting.

02:05:10.230 --> 02:05:15.230
And so, you know, in some
sects of Catholicism in Brazil,

02:05:17.280 --> 02:05:21.750
it's used as a sacrament
during religious ceremonies.

02:05:21.750 --> 02:05:25.320
And so it became interesting
to Brazilian researchers

02:05:25.320 --> 02:05:28.440
as to whether or not they
could affect recidivism rates

02:05:28.440 --> 02:05:31.670
for prisoners in Brazilian prisons, right?

02:05:31.670 --> 02:05:35.880
So they gave half of
the prisoners, you know,

02:05:35.880 --> 02:05:37.680
some sort of inert substance

02:05:37.680 --> 02:05:41.940
and half of the prisoners
an ayahuasca session.

02:05:41.940 --> 02:05:45.810
And the recidivism rate or
the return to prison rate

02:05:45.810 --> 02:05:48.060
in the ayahuasca exposed individuals was

02:05:48.060 --> 02:05:51.934
statistically significantly
lower than the recidivism rate

02:05:51.934 --> 02:05:56.070
in the control group,
suggesting that, you know,

02:05:56.070 --> 02:05:59.250
whatever is going on there
seems to have an effect

02:05:59.250 --> 02:06:02.400
on whatever drives criminal behavior,

02:06:02.400 --> 02:06:04.350
whatever criminal behavior
that happened to be.

02:06:04.350 --> 02:06:06.060
And I don't have the details

02:06:06.060 --> 02:06:07.773
on the exact nature of the crime.

02:06:08.700 --> 02:06:10.650
You know, I am also in no way saying

02:06:10.650 --> 02:06:12.360
that we should just be giving psychedelics

02:06:12.360 --> 02:06:15.000
to folks in prison and all of that.

02:06:15.000 --> 02:06:18.750
I think that that is a
very edgy thing to do

02:06:18.750 --> 02:06:21.330
and probably not something
that anybody should try,

02:06:21.330 --> 02:06:25.440
but it does kind of bring
up this curious question

02:06:25.440 --> 02:06:28.500
of what is it about that
that would drive people

02:06:28.500 --> 02:06:31.470
to change those behaviors

02:06:31.470 --> 02:06:35.007
and why do people make
those behavioral decisions?

02:06:35.007 --> 02:06:36.780
And a lot of times

02:06:36.780 --> 02:06:39.690
if you look at prisons
in the United States,

02:06:39.690 --> 02:06:41.040
you know, people say this,

02:06:41.040 --> 02:06:42.720
what's the biggest mental health facility

02:06:42.720 --> 02:06:44.010
in the United States?

02:06:44.010 --> 02:06:44.853
It's a prison.

02:06:45.840 --> 02:06:49.694
- Yeah, there's a lot to
unpack there for sure.

02:06:49.694 --> 02:06:54.363
You know, the homeless
issue, the prison issue.

02:06:55.380 --> 02:06:58.200
It does lead to something
that I heard recently,

02:06:58.200 --> 02:07:01.173
which is related to all
this, which is cannabis.

02:07:02.081 --> 02:07:04.890
You know, we hear a lot
nowadays about people will say,

02:07:04.890 --> 02:07:06.120
well, it's safer than alcohol.

02:07:06.120 --> 02:07:08.070
And we did an episode on alcohol that,

02:07:08.070 --> 02:07:10.410
at least by my read of the literature,

02:07:10.410 --> 02:07:13.410
indeed alcohol does seem to be quite bad

02:07:13.410 --> 02:07:14.820
for our health beyond...

02:07:14.820 --> 02:07:17.550
I think it's pretty clear
that not drinking is better

02:07:17.550 --> 02:07:19.710
for your health than drinking at all.

02:07:19.710 --> 02:07:21.447
And here, I'm not trying
to tell people what to do,

02:07:21.447 --> 02:07:22.710
but those are what the data say.

02:07:22.710 --> 02:07:24.480
And forget the studies on red wine.

02:07:24.480 --> 02:07:25.710
You'd have to drink so much red wine

02:07:25.710 --> 02:07:26.910
to get enough resveratrol.

02:07:26.910 --> 02:07:29.850
It's not even clear resveratrol
does anything useful anyway,

02:07:29.850 --> 02:07:31.110
et cetera, et cetera.

02:07:31.110 --> 02:07:33.420
Nonetheless, cannabis is now available

02:07:33.420 --> 02:07:35.790
in a lot of very high potency forms.

02:07:35.790 --> 02:07:40.320
People are vaping cannabis,
people are smoking cannabis.

02:07:40.320 --> 02:07:42.570
I certainly am not saying
that cannabis is bad

02:07:42.570 --> 02:07:45.600
for people necessarily,
although I think children,

02:07:45.600 --> 02:07:46.800
I would hope

02:07:46.800 --> 02:07:48.990
that their brain development
would be completed first,

02:07:48.990 --> 02:07:50.250
you know, get to age 25.

02:07:50.250 --> 02:07:52.890
I know that sounds late
for a lot of people,

02:07:52.890 --> 02:07:57.890
but the THC obviously taps
into some endogenous systems

02:07:59.423 --> 02:08:03.480
and the cannabinoid
systems and is powerful.

02:08:03.480 --> 02:08:08.480
And I've seen this report
that was in Lancet Psychiatry

02:08:09.360 --> 02:08:12.810
this last year that said that
early use of potent cannabis,

02:08:12.810 --> 02:08:16.170
meaning age 14 to 20 or so,

02:08:16.170 --> 02:08:18.690
can potentially lead to an exacerbation

02:08:18.690 --> 02:08:20.640
of psychosis later in life.

02:08:20.640 --> 02:08:22.860
And I actually put this
out on social media

02:08:22.860 --> 02:08:24.691
and it sort of exploded.

02:08:24.691 --> 02:08:26.517
I didn't expect it to.

02:08:26.517 --> 02:08:28.290
And people were saying,
well, that's not causal.

02:08:28.290 --> 02:08:31.410
And obviously it's not causal
because people say, well,

02:08:31.410 --> 02:08:33.960
maybe people with psychotic tendencies

02:08:33.960 --> 02:08:35.190
are seeking out cannabis.

02:08:35.190 --> 02:08:36.630
Although that's sort of a weak argument

02:08:36.630 --> 02:08:41.630
in the sense that there's
at least a 4X increase

02:08:42.270 --> 02:08:45.600
in these psychotic episodes
for people later in life.

02:08:45.600 --> 02:08:46.800
But what are your thoughts about cannabis?

02:08:46.800 --> 02:08:47.850
Because I do want to acknowledge

02:08:47.850 --> 02:08:50.490
that it does have medical
benefits for certain things,

02:08:50.490 --> 02:08:52.650
pain, chemotherapy.

02:08:52.650 --> 02:08:55.140
So by no means trying to knock on cannabis

02:08:55.140 --> 02:08:57.450
and its appropriate medicinal use.

02:08:57.450 --> 02:09:01.140
But what should we think about cannabis

02:09:01.140 --> 02:09:02.730
in terms of this finding

02:09:02.730 --> 02:09:06.510
that it can exacerbate a
psychosis in certain individuals?

02:09:06.510 --> 02:09:08.551
- Yeah, so I think, you know,

02:09:08.551 --> 02:09:10.890
there's a couple of things, right?

02:09:10.890 --> 02:09:14.661
So cannabis is multiple
cannibinoids, right?

02:09:14.661 --> 02:09:18.600
- Right, THC, CBD, CBN,
sativas, and, you know, indicas,

02:09:18.600 --> 02:09:21.240
it gets, yeah, there's
a lot there to unpack.

02:09:21.240 --> 02:09:22.737
- Yeah, there's a lot.

02:09:22.737 --> 02:09:27.091
But there are two main kind
of chemicals you think about

02:09:27.091 --> 02:09:30.720
and kind of how things are
essentially bred, right?

02:09:30.720 --> 02:09:33.750
And so, you know,
there's a lot of cannabis

02:09:33.750 --> 02:09:38.506
that's really bred to be
very high, very potent THC.

02:09:38.506 --> 02:09:41.700
And there's cannabis where
the THC's bred completely out.

02:09:41.700 --> 02:09:45.990
So there's stories, you
know, from Colorado, right?

02:09:45.990 --> 02:09:49.980
This strain of cannabis that's THC free,

02:09:49.980 --> 02:09:53.460
there's no THC at all, and it's all CBD,

02:09:53.460 --> 02:09:56.580
and it's called Charlotte's Web.

02:09:56.580 --> 02:09:59.940
And a bunch of kids' parents, one kid,

02:09:59.940 --> 02:10:02.070
and then kind of a string
of parents after that moved

02:10:02.070 --> 02:10:05.460
to Colorado when cannabis was legalized,

02:10:05.460 --> 02:10:09.510
because CBD is antiepileptic,

02:10:09.510 --> 02:10:13.590
so CBD is also antipsychotic.

02:10:13.590 --> 02:10:15.033
And so there have been a number of studies

02:10:15.033 --> 02:10:18.390
that if you give CBD at high doses,

02:10:18.390 --> 02:10:23.390
it's antipsychotic in established
schizophrenic patients.

02:10:24.570 --> 02:10:25.403
The issue is

02:10:25.403 --> 02:10:29.490
that we've bred CBD out of
marijuana selectively over time.

02:10:29.490 --> 02:10:30.323
We've gotten very good

02:10:30.323 --> 02:10:32.340
at figuring out how to do that, right?

02:10:32.340 --> 02:10:37.340
Conversely, THC is pro psychotic
and pro epileptic, right?

02:10:40.290 --> 02:10:43.650
And so when you talk about
does cannabis cause psychosis

02:10:43.650 --> 02:10:46.350
or does cannabis treat psychosis,

02:10:46.350 --> 02:10:48.240
it appears to be more related

02:10:48.240 --> 02:10:52.350
to the proportions of CBD to THC

02:10:52.350 --> 02:10:55.020
than it does to the kind
of idea of cannabis.

02:10:55.020 --> 02:10:56.163
So for me,

02:10:57.195 --> 02:10:59.880
and I have no stock in
this or anything like that,

02:10:59.880 --> 02:11:01.920
but there's a company
called GW Pharmaceuticals,

02:11:01.920 --> 02:11:03.420
and I haven't looked into them in a while,

02:11:03.420 --> 02:11:07.410
but they have a lot of clinical trials

02:11:07.410 --> 02:11:09.300
for something called Dravet Syndrome,

02:11:09.300 --> 02:11:12.060
which is a seizure disorder
where kids seize a whole lot,

02:11:12.060 --> 02:11:15.270
Lennox-Gastaut syndrome,
which is a seizure disorder,

02:11:15.270 --> 02:11:16.890
kids are seizing 300 times a day.

02:11:16.890 --> 02:11:19.680
Both of these are like
kids are seizing so much,

02:11:19.680 --> 02:11:20.880
they're basically in a seizure

02:11:20.880 --> 02:11:23.970
or in the postictal phase constantly.

02:11:23.970 --> 02:11:25.740
And they've failed everything.

02:11:25.740 --> 02:11:28.710
They've failed barbiturates,
they've failed bromides,

02:11:28.710 --> 02:11:32.760
which we just don't use
anymore except in these cases

02:11:32.760 --> 02:11:34.140
because of the side effects.

02:11:34.140 --> 02:11:35.845
And they'll give kids CBD.

02:11:35.845 --> 02:11:37.500
And I think CBD is a pretty safe drug

02:11:37.500 --> 02:11:39.150
compared to bromide, right?

02:11:39.150 --> 02:11:44.150
And so this idea that CBD
in a kid is actually safe,

02:11:45.270 --> 02:11:48.687
it's a cannabinoid, but it's
CBD and it's safe, right?

02:11:48.687 --> 02:11:52.440
And so that to me is totally fine.

02:11:52.440 --> 02:11:55.890
Also giving CBD as an adjunctive treatment

02:11:55.890 --> 02:11:57.210
for schizophrenia.

02:11:57.210 --> 02:11:59.010
There have been some positive trials

02:11:59.010 --> 02:11:59.910
and negative trials in that,

02:11:59.910 --> 02:12:01.830
but there seems to be no
negative side effects.

02:12:01.830 --> 02:12:04.920
It seems to reduce some of
the metabolic syndrome issues

02:12:04.920 --> 02:12:08.130
in folks with schizophrenia
who are having side effects

02:12:08.130 --> 02:12:09.933
from the primary antipsychotic.

02:12:10.800 --> 02:12:14.040
The converse is, there's
clearly cases where people

02:12:14.040 --> 02:12:18.930
that are taking very high
doses of THC become psychotic,

02:12:18.930 --> 02:12:21.960
they get put into the
psychiatric unit, nothing happens

02:12:21.960 --> 02:12:25.770
other than they kind of get
the THC out of their system,

02:12:25.770 --> 02:12:28.800
and then they resolve
their psychosis, right?

02:12:28.800 --> 02:12:32.957
And so that, and you know,

02:12:32.957 --> 02:12:34.410
a handful of people who have had seizures

02:12:34.410 --> 02:12:37.110
related to high doses of THC and syncope

02:12:37.110 --> 02:12:38.430
and all sorts of things.

02:12:38.430 --> 02:12:41.820
And so this idea that THC,

02:12:41.820 --> 02:12:45.090
high doses of THC can be pro psychotic,

02:12:45.090 --> 02:12:46.950
is also not taking a shot at people

02:12:46.950 --> 02:12:49.890
that think that cannabis
overall is a good thing.

02:12:49.890 --> 02:12:52.050
It's just, it just is what it is.

02:12:52.050 --> 02:12:54.540
And the kind of pure, I
think if you zoom back

02:12:54.540 --> 02:12:56.910
and you say you're a true naturalist,

02:12:56.910 --> 02:12:59.430
you're thinking about natural
medicines in the world,

02:12:59.430 --> 02:13:00.960
you should think, well,

02:13:00.960 --> 02:13:04.230
probably marijuana was
balanced THC CBD at some point,

02:13:04.230 --> 02:13:06.870
and then we humans messed with it, right?

02:13:06.870 --> 02:13:11.870
And that most likely, that was
probably okay at some level,

02:13:13.110 --> 02:13:16.140
and then we pushed it one way or another.

02:13:16.140 --> 02:13:19.410
And what I mean by okay
is in a 45 year old,

02:13:19.410 --> 02:13:21.063
it's okay, kind of thing.

02:13:22.230 --> 02:13:26.370
Now, what I think is
going on with the kids,

02:13:26.370 --> 02:13:27.420
with the teenagers,

02:13:27.420 --> 02:13:29.520
is you've got prefrontal
maturation, right?

02:13:29.520 --> 02:13:31.110
And then you're exposing them

02:13:31.110 --> 02:13:35.340
to a whole lot of high THC load.

02:13:35.340 --> 02:13:40.340
And while it's unclear
if it's cause or effect,

02:13:40.560 --> 02:13:42.120
it's certainly in the picture.

02:13:42.120 --> 02:13:43.650
And if I were a parent,

02:13:43.650 --> 02:13:46.800
I wouldn't want my 16 year
old smoking marijuana.

02:13:46.800 --> 02:13:51.393
If I were a parent and my 30
year old, otherwise healthy,

02:13:52.290 --> 02:13:57.290
totally fine, you know, whatever,
banker, lawyer kid decided

02:13:58.020 --> 02:13:59.880
to try marijuana for the first time,

02:13:59.880 --> 02:14:01.860
I wouldn't scold them about it, right?

02:14:01.860 --> 02:14:03.870
So I think it's this kind
of a different thing, right?

02:14:03.870 --> 02:14:07.350
I would never want my up to 25 year old,

02:14:07.350 --> 02:14:09.780
just like you're saying,
before prefrontal maturation,

02:14:09.780 --> 02:14:13.320
I would never want my
kid to be exposed at all.

02:14:13.320 --> 02:14:17.970
But it looks like, except
in susceptible individuals

02:14:17.970 --> 02:14:19.920
that are susceptible to
drug-induced psychosis,

02:14:19.920 --> 02:14:24.920
it looks like, you know,
it's a relatively safe thing

02:14:25.020 --> 02:14:27.033
past prefrontal maturation.

02:14:27.900 --> 02:14:31.260
You know, again, I'm not going
to comment of cause and effect,

02:14:31.260 --> 02:14:36.000
but I would say that, you
know, if you're a parent,

02:14:36.000 --> 02:14:37.686
it doesn't make much sense, right?

02:14:37.686 --> 02:14:40.710
You never know what's ultimately
going to hurt your kid.

02:14:40.710 --> 02:14:42.570
I mean, we were talking
about this earlier,

02:14:42.570 --> 02:14:43.956
my wife's pregnant now.

02:14:43.956 --> 02:14:46.200
She kind of avoids everything, right?

02:14:46.200 --> 02:14:47.430
Rightfully so, right?

02:14:47.430 --> 02:14:50.070
This idea that we just,
we want to be careful

02:14:50.070 --> 02:14:51.780
when our children's brains are developing.

02:14:51.780 --> 02:14:53.520
And I think that's really
what you were saying

02:14:53.520 --> 02:14:55.560
and I think actually important.

02:14:55.560 --> 02:14:57.630
The bigger question that you asked,

02:14:57.630 --> 02:15:01.230
which is relative risks of
drugs, is an interesting one.

02:15:01.230 --> 02:15:04.080
So David Nutt published in, I
think it was in "The Lancet,"

02:15:04.080 --> 02:15:06.270
I'll have to look it up,
but I think in "The Lancet,"

02:15:06.270 --> 02:15:09.510
an article about relative drug risks

02:15:09.510 --> 02:15:11.190
for the person and for society.

02:15:11.190 --> 02:15:12.630
And this was like,

02:15:12.630 --> 02:15:17.630
he was on the UK's like
British Drug Policy Group,

02:15:20.520 --> 02:15:22.560
where essentially what he showed was

02:15:22.560 --> 02:15:26.910
if you look at societal
risk plus personal risk,

02:15:26.910 --> 02:15:28.560
and you combine those two,

02:15:28.560 --> 02:15:32.460
you know what drug is the most
dangerous drug in the world?

02:15:32.460 --> 02:15:33.600
- I'm going to guess it's alcohol.

02:15:33.600 --> 02:15:34.920
- It's alcohol,

02:15:34.920 --> 02:15:38.247
right behind heroin and
cocaine and da da da da da,

02:15:38.247 --> 02:15:39.270
and somewhere in the middle is marijuana.

02:15:39.270 --> 02:15:40.705
And right on the tail end,

02:15:40.705 --> 02:15:45.705
on the exact other end
of this, psilocybin.

02:15:47.100 --> 02:15:50.090
- Is caffeine, usually
doesn't make the list.

02:15:50.090 --> 02:15:51.690
- It may have been on the list.

02:15:51.690 --> 02:15:54.000
If it was, it was probably
pretty close to psilocybin,

02:15:54.000 --> 02:15:56.040
but somewhere in the middle was ketamine,

02:15:56.040 --> 02:15:58.860
somewhere in the middle was amphetamine,

02:15:58.860 --> 02:15:59.940
somewhere in the, you know,

02:15:59.940 --> 02:16:03.723
a little closer to psilocybin,
I think was MDMA, you know.

02:16:03.723 --> 02:16:08.723
But it's this combined
personal kind of world risk

02:16:09.270 --> 02:16:10.103
of these things.

02:16:10.103 --> 02:16:12.600
And so alcohol makes it,

02:16:12.600 --> 02:16:15.180
because there's a huge
amount of personal risk

02:16:15.180 --> 02:16:17.640
and there's a huge amount
of societal risk, right?

02:16:17.640 --> 02:16:20.850
Drunk drivers kill X amount
of people in the world.

02:16:20.850 --> 02:16:23.372
- Fight, sexual assault, all that.

02:16:23.372 --> 02:16:24.387
- All that, yeah.

02:16:24.387 --> 02:16:27.090
And then all the cancer
and all that stuff.

02:16:27.090 --> 02:16:29.790
And so it beats out cocaine,
it beats out heroin,

02:16:29.790 --> 02:16:31.917
it beats out all of these things.

02:16:31.917 --> 02:16:36.917
And yet, we don't, as a
culture, for whatever reason,

02:16:37.440 --> 02:16:41.430
we don't as a culture see it as a drug.

02:16:41.430 --> 02:16:44.640
And that's the part that
really baffles me, you know?

02:16:44.640 --> 02:16:45.570
- I mean, they serve it.

02:16:45.570 --> 02:16:48.270
I mean, this is no knock
on Stanford at all.

02:16:48.270 --> 02:16:49.576
Of course, I wouldn't do that.

02:16:49.576 --> 02:16:50.970
This is at every institution I've been to,

02:16:50.970 --> 02:16:54.556
they serve alcohol at the
graduate student events.

02:16:54.556 --> 02:16:55.526
- [Nolan] That's right.

02:16:55.526 --> 02:16:57.330
- You know, they serve alcohol.

02:16:57.330 --> 02:16:58.620
They do a happy hour.

02:16:58.620 --> 02:16:59.850
I've never been a drinker.

02:16:59.850 --> 02:17:01.296
I can take it or leave it.

02:17:01.296 --> 02:17:02.129
- [Nolan] Yeah, same.

02:17:02.129 --> 02:17:04.890
- And I realize that some people,
they really enjoy alcohol.

02:17:04.890 --> 02:17:06.900
You know, my former partner, I mean,

02:17:06.900 --> 02:17:10.050
she just was in that 10% or so of people

02:17:10.050 --> 02:17:12.510
who have a glass of wine
and just feel great.

02:17:12.510 --> 02:17:14.100
And the second one, feel great.

02:17:14.100 --> 02:17:17.040
I just want to take a nap
after I have a bit of alcohol,

02:17:17.040 --> 02:17:18.090
so it never does much for me.

02:17:18.090 --> 02:17:19.500
I always feel poisoned.

02:17:19.500 --> 02:17:20.760
I feel lucky in that sense.

02:17:20.760 --> 02:17:25.620
But it's unbelievable
that it is so prevalent

02:17:25.620 --> 02:17:28.020
and it's just, it's
baked into the medical,

02:17:28.020 --> 02:17:29.490
even medical institutions,

02:17:29.490 --> 02:17:30.840
they'll pop a bottle of champagne

02:17:30.840 --> 02:17:32.956
to celebrate the opening of a hospital.

02:17:32.956 --> 02:17:34.065
- [Nolan] That's right, that's right.

02:17:34.065 --> 02:17:35.370
- You know, that's pretty crazy.

02:17:35.370 --> 02:17:36.630
- Yeah, no, you're absolutely right.

02:17:36.630 --> 02:17:39.030
You know, I think what's going to happen,

02:17:39.030 --> 02:17:40.800
but this is me, you know,

02:17:40.800 --> 02:17:42.240
looking at the crystal ball a little bit,

02:17:42.240 --> 02:17:44.010
but I think what's going to happen is

02:17:44.010 --> 02:17:45.450
what happened with doctors and smoking.

02:17:45.450 --> 02:17:47.327
So if you look at the
'50s and '60s, right?

02:17:47.327 --> 02:17:50.100
There are all these pictures
of doctors smoking cigarettes,

02:17:50.100 --> 02:17:52.050
you know, with patients or, you know,

02:17:52.050 --> 02:17:54.390
psychiatrists doing psychotherapy
and smoking a cigarette

02:17:54.390 --> 02:17:55.800
with the patient sitting on the couch,

02:17:55.800 --> 02:17:58.890
you know, surgeons smoking a
cigarette in between cases,

02:17:58.890 --> 02:18:00.540
there are all these
pictures of that, right?

02:18:00.540 --> 02:18:02.730
And now all of a sudden,
smoking's totally banned.

02:18:02.730 --> 02:18:05.970
I think it's totally banned
from most of Stanford campus.

02:18:05.970 --> 02:18:09.150
My suspicion is, as
you're suggesting, right?

02:18:09.150 --> 02:18:10.530
You know, this is everywhere

02:18:10.530 --> 02:18:11.970
and it's all kind of ubiquitous.

02:18:11.970 --> 02:18:14.613
At some critical point,
some tipping point,

02:18:15.480 --> 02:18:19.290
everybody's going to realize
that, just like with smoking,

02:18:19.290 --> 02:18:21.900
we've got to rid hospital systems

02:18:21.900 --> 02:18:24.690
and universities of alcohol.

02:18:24.690 --> 02:18:27.870
And at some point in 50 years,

02:18:27.870 --> 02:18:31.440
it's my view that we'll look
it back at these scenarios

02:18:31.440 --> 02:18:34.860
that you're talking about
and be like, you know what,

02:18:34.860 --> 02:18:37.143
we were foolish about this.

02:18:38.175 --> 02:18:40.956
I can't believe that
we gave people alcohol

02:18:40.956 --> 02:18:43.560
when they graduated
from whatever, you know?

02:18:43.560 --> 02:18:45.810
And I think we'll have
a different take on it,

02:18:45.810 --> 02:18:47.995
but it's going to take a longer time.

02:18:47.995 --> 02:18:51.350
I think people did a really
good job tying smoking

02:18:51.350 --> 02:18:54.960
to lung cancer and it's like
a very simplistic story.

02:18:54.960 --> 02:18:57.900
Smoking, lung cancer, you know?

02:18:57.900 --> 02:18:59.820
Now, as you know,

02:18:59.820 --> 02:19:02.760
alcohol increases the risk of
a lot of different cancers.

02:19:02.760 --> 02:19:04.500
Not so clear which one.

02:19:04.500 --> 02:19:06.240
I mean, there's like, you
know, the kind of oral,

02:19:06.240 --> 02:19:08.130
like the throat, tongue
cancer, that's one of-

02:19:08.130 --> 02:19:08.963
- Breast cancer.

02:19:08.963 --> 02:19:10.080
- Yeah, breast cancer, you know?

02:19:10.080 --> 02:19:14.280
And so it's kind of just,
it's a harder story to tell,

02:19:14.280 --> 02:19:15.113
you know?

02:19:15.113 --> 02:19:17.400
And I think that's why, and everybody,

02:19:17.400 --> 02:19:18.510
you know, and then there's this whole,

02:19:18.510 --> 02:19:19.957
it's, you know, my mom says this.

02:19:19.957 --> 02:19:22.110
It's like I drank my glass of wine

02:19:22.110 --> 02:19:25.020
because my doctor told
me it was heart healthy.

02:19:25.020 --> 02:19:27.000
And we were talking
about this, and I try to,

02:19:27.000 --> 02:19:30.420
no, no, no, but Dr. So-and-so
said it's heart healthy.

02:19:30.420 --> 02:19:31.890
And so it ends up being this thing

02:19:31.890 --> 02:19:34.800
where like she's drinking
alcohol because she thinks

02:19:34.800 --> 02:19:37.685
that it's good for her heart.

02:19:37.685 --> 02:19:39.510
And, you know, and it's hard.

02:19:39.510 --> 02:19:40.980
I've had those conversations with her.

02:19:40.980 --> 02:19:42.870
It's hard to untie that.

02:19:42.870 --> 02:19:44.520
And I think that, yeah,

02:19:44.520 --> 02:19:48.360
at some point we're going to
hit some threshold moment.

02:19:48.360 --> 02:19:50.520
And it'll be interesting if
we really look at the data

02:19:50.520 --> 02:19:52.920
and we really look at
what's safe and not safe

02:19:52.920 --> 02:19:55.020
purely from this analysis,

02:19:55.020 --> 02:19:58.380
it kind of points to the right direction.

02:19:58.380 --> 02:19:59.213
- It's really interesting.

02:19:59.213 --> 02:20:01.440
And also say nothing of poor judgment

02:20:01.440 --> 02:20:02.940
under the influence of alcohol.

02:20:02.940 --> 02:20:04.740
I mean, I would venture

02:20:04.740 --> 02:20:08.520
that if we were to remove
alcohol from university campuses,

02:20:08.520 --> 02:20:10.170
watch, the students are
going to lobby against me

02:20:10.170 --> 02:20:11.003
if I say this.

02:20:11.003 --> 02:20:14.160
But if you were to remove
alcohol from campuses,

02:20:14.160 --> 02:20:17.220
I mean, just think about
what I suspect would be

02:20:17.220 --> 02:20:19.623
the improvement in good decision making.

02:20:20.497 --> 02:20:23.460
And that would occur.

02:20:23.460 --> 02:20:27.540
Or, you know, I've got stories
from graduate school and...

02:20:27.540 --> 02:20:30.360
It was very different,
you know, 10 years ago.

02:20:30.360 --> 02:20:32.430
There was a lot more alcohol consumption.

02:20:32.430 --> 02:20:33.450
Again, that was never my thing,

02:20:33.450 --> 02:20:36.243
but I know people who
make really bad decisions.

02:20:37.500 --> 02:20:40.080
In any case, there's a whole
landscape there emerging.

02:20:40.080 --> 02:20:42.600
I think you got your finger
right on the pulse of it.

02:20:42.600 --> 02:20:44.970
I want to touch on
something slightly different

02:20:44.970 --> 02:20:45.930
than what we've been talking about,

02:20:45.930 --> 02:20:47.670
but definitely related to depression.

02:20:47.670 --> 02:20:48.503
And this, again,

02:20:48.503 --> 02:20:51.000
is one of these intriguing
but perplexing things,

02:20:51.000 --> 02:20:55.800
which is that sleep deprivation
can improve symptoms

02:20:55.800 --> 02:20:57.000
of depression.

02:20:57.000 --> 02:21:00.120
And yet I'm personally
very familiar with the fact

02:21:00.120 --> 02:21:02.430
that if I don't sleep well for one night

02:21:02.430 --> 02:21:04.230
or don't sleep at all, in fact,

02:21:04.230 --> 02:21:06.690
I do have an ability to function
pretty well the next day.

02:21:06.690 --> 02:21:08.520
I'll do this non-sleep deep rest practice

02:21:08.520 --> 02:21:10.750
that I blab a lot about on
the Huberman Lab Podcast,

02:21:10.750 --> 02:21:12.750
which for me is tremendously restorative,

02:21:12.750 --> 02:21:15.060
but I like a good night's sleep.

02:21:15.060 --> 02:21:18.180
I think everybody understands now,

02:21:18.180 --> 02:21:20.070
thanks to the great work of Matthew Walker

02:21:20.070 --> 02:21:22.020
and others that have really
gotten out into the world

02:21:22.020 --> 02:21:24.900
saying, look, the
foundation of mental health,

02:21:24.900 --> 02:21:27.900
physical health, and high
performance, if that's your thing,

02:21:27.900 --> 02:21:29.010
being a functional human being,

02:21:29.010 --> 02:21:32.670
is to try and get enough
quality deep sleep

02:21:32.670 --> 02:21:35.614
at least 80% of the nights
of your life, if you can.

02:21:35.614 --> 02:21:37.320
That's something to focus on.

02:21:37.320 --> 02:21:38.670
Just like good nutrition,

02:21:38.670 --> 02:21:42.150
just like exercise and
social connection, et cetera.

02:21:42.150 --> 02:21:45.300
So sleep deprivation,
we know, in particular,

02:21:45.300 --> 02:21:48.390
I think rapid eye movement
components of sleep deprivation

02:21:48.390 --> 02:21:51.060
can improve the symptoms of depression.

02:21:51.060 --> 02:21:55.950
And yet being sleep deprived
can also really disregulate

02:21:55.950 --> 02:21:57.570
our control of the autonomic system.

02:21:57.570 --> 02:21:59.730
I notice on night two or
night three of poor sleep,

02:21:59.730 --> 02:22:02.610
if I'm going through a stressful
phase and that's happening,

02:22:02.610 --> 02:22:05.430
all of a sudden my heart
rate is chronically elevated,

02:22:05.430 --> 02:22:07.500
my thought patterns
become really disrupted.

02:22:07.500 --> 02:22:11.280
I can't then exercise, my
decision making is thrown off,

02:22:11.280 --> 02:22:12.720
my emotionality is more labile.

02:22:12.720 --> 02:22:15.030
The hinge, as we were
referring to it earlier,

02:22:15.030 --> 02:22:18.210
feels less in control, under my control.

02:22:18.210 --> 02:22:21.390
And maybe I wonder sometimes
if I enter that state

02:22:21.390 --> 02:22:22.290
that you refer to earlier,

02:22:22.290 --> 02:22:24.510
where the dorsolateral
prefrontal cortex is

02:22:24.510 --> 02:22:26.010
no longer leading the cingulate,

02:22:26.010 --> 02:22:27.720
but the cingulate is now in charge.

02:22:27.720 --> 02:22:30.240
The players are in charge of the coach.

02:22:30.240 --> 02:22:31.740
Not a good situation.

02:22:31.740 --> 02:22:34.500
So I know you've done some
work on sleep deprivation

02:22:34.500 --> 02:22:37.050
and light and effects.

02:22:37.050 --> 02:22:38.160
Please tell us about that

02:22:38.160 --> 02:22:40.950
and please tell us about
this triple therapy.

02:22:40.950 --> 02:22:41.783
Is that?
- Yeah, yeah.

02:22:41.783 --> 02:22:44.220
So friend of mine, Greg Salem,

02:22:44.220 --> 02:22:46.863
another one of the professors at Stanford,

02:22:47.880 --> 02:22:49.170
was very interested in sleep.

02:22:49.170 --> 02:22:52.020
He did a bunch of training in sleep

02:22:52.020 --> 02:22:53.280
before he went to medical school

02:22:53.280 --> 02:22:57.810
and got very interested in this
idea that, as you're saying,

02:22:57.810 --> 02:23:01.740
if you sleep deprive somebody one night

02:23:01.740 --> 02:23:04.440
in just kind of an isolated single night,

02:23:04.440 --> 02:23:06.240
at the end of that sleep deprivation,

02:23:06.240 --> 02:23:08.010
they will have an antidepressant effect,

02:23:08.010 --> 02:23:10.470
but as soon as they fall
asleep, they lose it.

02:23:10.470 --> 02:23:13.050
So if it's a depressed individual,

02:23:13.050 --> 02:23:16.200
you can get them to be
less depressed acutely.

02:23:16.200 --> 02:23:19.050
Soon as they fall asleep, they
wake up eight hours later,

02:23:19.050 --> 02:23:22.440
then they come back into the
same level of depression.

02:23:22.440 --> 02:23:24.480
And so the idea is that you needed

02:23:24.480 --> 02:23:26.580
to do some sort of circadian reset.

02:23:26.580 --> 02:23:29.460
And that part of what depression is,

02:23:29.460 --> 02:23:32.160
is that it's a dysregulated
circadian system.

02:23:32.160 --> 02:23:33.960
And so mentors of mine say,

02:23:33.960 --> 02:23:35.820
if you can just get the sleep better,

02:23:35.820 --> 02:23:37.337
that's half the battle of
dealing with depression.

02:23:37.337 --> 02:23:41.100
'Cause so many people have
insomnia around depression

02:23:41.100 --> 02:23:43.413
and have a whole host
of types of insomnia.

02:23:44.280 --> 02:23:45.600
Having a hard time falling asleep,

02:23:45.600 --> 02:23:47.700
waking up in the middle of the
night, and waking up earlier,

02:23:47.700 --> 02:23:50.190
all symptoms of depression.

02:23:50.190 --> 02:23:55.170
And so what this does is it
sleep deprives the individual

02:23:55.170 --> 02:23:57.090
and then there's a certain calculation

02:23:57.090 --> 02:23:59.460
of shifting their phase

02:23:59.460 --> 02:24:02.700
and simultaneously exposing
them to bright lights.

02:24:02.700 --> 02:24:05.100
So that's the triple, the phase shift,

02:24:05.100 --> 02:24:07.260
the sleep deprivation,
and the bright light,

02:24:07.260 --> 02:24:10.650
to try to get their circadian rhythm.

02:24:10.650 --> 02:24:14.100
Essentially the theory is reintrained.

02:24:14.100 --> 02:24:18.030
And so, you know, in the
trials that we've done

02:24:18.030 --> 02:24:22.572
and other trials prior to
ours and after, you know,

02:24:22.572 --> 02:24:23.405
it looked like there was

02:24:23.405 --> 02:24:26.340
a pretty profound antidepressant effect

02:24:26.340 --> 02:24:30.450
from this triple therapy
that seemed to be durable,

02:24:30.450 --> 02:24:33.450
meaning durability is this term we use

02:24:33.450 --> 02:24:36.300
to say that not only can you
get kind of point relief,

02:24:36.300 --> 02:24:39.450
but that the relief ends
up, you know, lasting.

02:24:39.450 --> 02:24:41.670
What's important to
know about this is like,

02:24:41.670 --> 02:24:44.310
you shouldn't do this at home for sure.

02:24:44.310 --> 02:24:45.870
You would need to do
this with a professional,

02:24:45.870 --> 02:24:48.570
'cause it's complicated,
it's not just one thing.

02:24:48.570 --> 02:24:51.240
And sleep deprivation,

02:24:51.240 --> 02:24:54.900
while it seems to be
antidepressant, it's pro anxiety.

02:24:54.900 --> 02:24:56.970
So if you take a highly anxious
person that's not depressed

02:24:56.970 --> 02:24:59.910
and you sleep deprive them,
they get profoundly anxious.

02:24:59.910 --> 02:25:01.410
And so that's the other thing

02:25:01.410 --> 02:25:02.493
that you have to really realize is

02:25:02.493 --> 02:25:04.890
that this is like everything else

02:25:04.890 --> 02:25:06.060
that I've talked about today,

02:25:06.060 --> 02:25:09.570
all things that you have to
do under medical supervision,

02:25:09.570 --> 02:25:11.337
but curious, right?

02:25:11.337 --> 02:25:14.400
And I think, you know, the
question that always comes up is

02:25:14.400 --> 02:25:16.230
why isn't this used more?

02:25:16.230 --> 02:25:17.760
And I think the reason is

02:25:17.760 --> 02:25:21.480
that there's not really a
mechanism for, you know,

02:25:21.480 --> 02:25:23.940
ultimately in medicine, as sad as it is,

02:25:23.940 --> 02:25:26.070
you have to have a code to do a thing.

02:25:26.070 --> 02:25:29.070
There has to be a code
associated with a treatment

02:25:29.070 --> 02:25:32.040
and it's hard to figure out
how to make a code for this.

02:25:32.040 --> 02:25:33.690
And so I think that's part of it.

02:25:33.690 --> 02:25:36.570
And so if there's a way,

02:25:36.570 --> 02:25:39.600
and somebody's got to kind
of take that baton on that,

02:25:39.600 --> 02:25:41.883
but if there's a way to
make a code for this,

02:25:42.840 --> 02:25:45.960
you know, I think you could
actually turn it into something

02:25:45.960 --> 02:25:48.420
that was more widely utilized.

02:25:48.420 --> 02:25:51.360
And, you know, probably dream up ways

02:25:51.360 --> 02:25:55.260
of how to integrate AI, passive sensing,

02:25:55.260 --> 02:25:57.660
all that stuff to really make that work.

02:25:57.660 --> 02:26:00.300
But I think that would be the idea,

02:26:00.300 --> 02:26:02.490
that would be the trajectory
I'd see, so, yeah.

02:26:02.490 --> 02:26:07.080
- Yeah, having a billable to
insurance code is fundamental.

02:26:07.080 --> 02:26:09.330
And a lot of listeners to this podcast,

02:26:09.330 --> 02:26:11.610
I think, have a background
in engineering science.

02:26:11.610 --> 02:26:14.010
And we will put a link to that manuscript

02:26:14.010 --> 02:26:15.210
that talks about the triple therapy,

02:26:15.210 --> 02:26:16.290
because here we're talking

02:26:16.290 --> 02:26:17.670
about one night sleep deprivation,

02:26:17.670 --> 02:26:20.340
some timed light exposure to the eyes,

02:26:20.340 --> 02:26:23.010
and then shifting in the
circadian clock being central

02:26:23.010 --> 02:26:26.490
to the themes of the
podcast that come up often.

02:26:26.490 --> 02:26:28.380
I think for the typical person,

02:26:28.380 --> 02:26:32.760
can we say that trying to get
a regular light dark cycle

02:26:32.760 --> 02:26:34.200
at sleep rhythm would be beneficial

02:26:34.200 --> 02:26:36.030
for overall mood regulation?

02:26:36.030 --> 02:26:40.263
- Yeah, I think for the
typical person, you know,

02:26:40.263 --> 02:26:42.720
really kind of reregulating your sleep

02:26:42.720 --> 02:26:45.060
and trying to get, you
know, a good night's sleep

02:26:45.060 --> 02:26:47.520
in which you fall asleep, stay asleep,

02:26:47.520 --> 02:26:51.210
wake up at a set time every
morning is going to be

02:26:51.210 --> 02:26:52.323
pretty crucial.

02:26:53.370 --> 02:26:55.140
You know, in mild depression,

02:26:55.140 --> 02:26:57.500
I think that one has a
lot of control over that.

02:26:57.500 --> 02:26:58.590
As we were talking about earlier,

02:26:58.590 --> 02:27:00.690
I think when you hit some
threshold in depression

02:27:00.690 --> 02:27:02.520
where things become
kind of semi-volitional

02:27:02.520 --> 02:27:06.120
and it's harder to kind of
will yourself into that.

02:27:06.120 --> 02:27:09.060
There are therapies like, you know,

02:27:09.060 --> 02:27:11.160
there's a CBT for insomnia, for instance,

02:27:11.160 --> 02:27:12.840
where you can do cognitive
behavioral therapy

02:27:12.840 --> 02:27:14.460
to help with insomnia.

02:27:14.460 --> 02:27:17.580
Sometimes people, and I'm no sleep expert.

02:27:17.580 --> 02:27:20.074
Kind of pass this to Greg
to fully talk about this,

02:27:20.074 --> 02:27:23.400
but some of what goes on

02:27:23.400 --> 02:27:27.090
that people with kind of
milder insomnia experience is

02:27:27.090 --> 02:27:30.467
like blue light out of their
computer and things like that,

02:27:30.467 --> 02:27:33.690
so you can use like
blue light blockers to,

02:27:33.690 --> 02:27:36.240
it tricks your brain, as
you know better than me,

02:27:36.240 --> 02:27:38.910
it tricks your brain to think
that it's still light outside.

02:27:38.910 --> 02:27:41.550
And so people will still have insomnia

02:27:41.550 --> 02:27:44.520
because their brain still
thinks that it's light outside.

02:27:44.520 --> 02:27:47.217
And then people will, you know,

02:27:47.217 --> 02:27:49.803
the kind of strict CBT for sleep.

02:27:51.420 --> 02:27:54.840
You know, therapists will
say there are only two things

02:27:54.840 --> 02:27:56.520
that you should do in your bed.

02:27:56.520 --> 02:27:59.670
And if you're under a
certain age and whatnot,

02:27:59.670 --> 02:28:01.800
it's really one thing that
you should do in your bed,

02:28:01.800 --> 02:28:05.517
which is to sleep and be
with your partner, right?

02:28:05.517 --> 02:28:09.990
And so those are kind of the two things

02:28:09.990 --> 02:28:11.100
that you should do in a bedroom.

02:28:11.100 --> 02:28:12.450
And that's really the only things

02:28:12.450 --> 02:28:13.380
that you should do in a bedroom

02:28:13.380 --> 02:28:14.940
if you're having sleep problems.

02:28:14.940 --> 02:28:15.927
You shouldn't watch TV in a bedroom,

02:28:15.927 --> 02:28:17.823
you shouldn't eat in a
bedroom, shouldn't hang out.

02:28:17.823 --> 02:28:19.290
- Keep the phone out of the bedroom.

02:28:19.290 --> 02:28:21.090
- [Nolan] Keep the phone
out of the bedroom, yeah.

02:28:21.090 --> 02:28:22.500
- Yeah.

02:28:22.500 --> 02:28:24.352
We should get Greg Salem on the podcast.

02:28:24.352 --> 02:28:25.290
I'll just mention

02:28:25.290 --> 02:28:26.610
for people that want to
regulate their sleep,

02:28:26.610 --> 02:28:29.670
we have a sleep toolkit that's available

02:28:29.670 --> 02:28:31.890
as a downloadable PDF at hubermanlab.com.

02:28:31.890 --> 02:28:33.420
Just go to the menu
and a lot of the things

02:28:33.420 --> 02:28:35.550
in that toolkit are based on work

02:28:35.550 --> 02:28:37.110
from Stanford Sleep Laboratories,

02:28:37.110 --> 02:28:39.750
including Jamie Zeitz and others' lab,

02:28:39.750 --> 02:28:41.703
not aimed at depression specifically.

02:28:44.280 --> 02:28:48.180
Listen, Nolan, Dr. Williams,

02:28:48.180 --> 02:28:50.850
this has been an amazing voyage

02:28:50.850 --> 02:28:53.670
through the circuitry
of autonomic control.

02:28:53.670 --> 02:28:55.849
This landscape of the
prefrontal cortex is,

02:28:55.849 --> 02:29:00.690
I find incredibly fascinating
and I just want to start off

02:29:00.690 --> 02:29:02.383
by saying please do come back again

02:29:02.383 --> 02:29:06.900
and teach us more about
that and your TMS work.

02:29:06.900 --> 02:29:08.790
Before we wrap, however,

02:29:08.790 --> 02:29:10.470
I do want to give you the opportunity

02:29:10.470 --> 02:29:12.250
to talk about the SAINT study.

02:29:12.250 --> 02:29:13.083
- [Nolan] Yeah, definitely.

02:29:13.083 --> 02:29:14.340
- Is it SAINT or SAINTS plural?

02:29:14.340 --> 02:29:15.816
- Yeah, it's SAINT.

02:29:15.816 --> 02:29:19.800
So SAINT, or we're calling it SNT now.

02:29:19.800 --> 02:29:21.450
SAINT has, you know...

02:29:21.450 --> 02:29:24.965
The intent was not to kind
of connect it to religion,

02:29:24.965 --> 02:29:28.350
but we may have accidentally done so.

02:29:28.350 --> 02:29:31.077
And so we abbreviated it to SNT

02:29:31.077 --> 02:29:34.500
for the subsequent trials,
which was initially

02:29:34.500 --> 02:29:37.800
Stanford Accelerated Intelligent
Neuromodulation Therapy,

02:29:37.800 --> 02:29:40.020
or now what we're calling
Stanford Neuromodulation Therapy.

02:29:40.020 --> 02:29:43.710
But the idea there, which is a cool idea,

02:29:43.710 --> 02:29:48.710
is that TMS is a device
that delivers a treatment,

02:29:52.140 --> 02:29:54.387
and the treatment is the protocol.

02:29:54.387 --> 02:29:59.220
And the protocol is the
stimulation parameter set

02:29:59.220 --> 02:30:03.240
in a specific brain region
for a specific condition.

02:30:03.240 --> 02:30:05.550
And so what's cool about neuromodulation,

02:30:05.550 --> 02:30:08.220
whether it be transcranial
magnetic stimulation

02:30:08.220 --> 02:30:09.990
or transcranial direct current stimulation

02:30:09.990 --> 02:30:11.130
or deep brain stimulation,

02:30:11.130 --> 02:30:15.210
like what Casey Halpern talked
about on another podcast,

02:30:15.210 --> 02:30:19.140
is this idea that in all of those cases,

02:30:19.140 --> 02:30:23.220
the device itself is a
physical layer conduit

02:30:23.220 --> 02:30:26.880
of a stimulation protocol
that's therapeutic

02:30:26.880 --> 02:30:29.400
for a given condition
in a given brain region.

02:30:29.400 --> 02:30:31.050
And so in the case of depression,

02:30:31.050 --> 02:30:33.450
which we know the most about for with TMS,

02:30:33.450 --> 02:30:36.720
we've been doing TMS
studies for depression for,

02:30:36.720 --> 02:30:39.180
you know, since 1995, right?

02:30:39.180 --> 02:30:43.350
And the clearance in 2000, 2008, 2009.

02:30:43.350 --> 02:30:46.440
And in that timeframe,

02:30:46.440 --> 02:30:50.370
we were able to go from really
knowing very little at all

02:30:50.370 --> 02:30:51.900
about how to do something like this

02:30:51.900 --> 02:30:53.817
to getting an FDA clearance.

02:30:53.817 --> 02:30:55.590
And the way that it went down was

02:30:55.590 --> 02:31:00.590
that there were two groups
studying different components

02:31:00.840 --> 02:31:01.673
at NIH.

02:31:01.673 --> 02:31:05.520
The first group was
studying mood neuroanatomy

02:31:05.520 --> 02:31:06.810
on functional imaging.

02:31:06.810 --> 02:31:08.190
That was kind of the first generation

02:31:08.190 --> 02:31:11.460
of functional imaging
back then, so PET scans,

02:31:11.460 --> 02:31:16.350
which are kind of metabolic
scans, and then SPECT scans.

02:31:16.350 --> 02:31:19.830
And the idea there was looking
at activity and metabolism

02:31:19.830 --> 02:31:21.030
and prefrontal cortex.

02:31:21.030 --> 02:31:24.033
And what they found in these
kind of more crude scans is

02:31:24.033 --> 02:31:27.257
that just general
hypoactivity, hypometabolism.

02:31:28.380 --> 02:31:29.760
The other group right upstairs

02:31:29.760 --> 02:31:32.310
at the National Institute
for Neurological Diseases

02:31:32.310 --> 02:31:36.810
and Stroke, NINDS, they
were looking at using TMS,

02:31:36.810 --> 02:31:38.160
which had been around for 10 years,

02:31:38.160 --> 02:31:41.850
and repetitively
stimulating in motor cortex.

02:31:41.850 --> 02:31:43.560
And what they found was, gosh,

02:31:43.560 --> 02:31:48.560
we can get a readout in thumb
muscle movement amplitude

02:31:49.200 --> 02:31:51.510
that's really reproducible across people.

02:31:51.510 --> 02:31:54.780
It's like, you know,
universally reproducible.

02:31:54.780 --> 02:31:57.480
And if we do certain
stimulation approaches,

02:31:57.480 --> 02:31:59.100
they are biologically active

02:31:59.100 --> 02:32:02.730
to either increase excitability,
IE the thumb motion,

02:32:02.730 --> 02:32:05.130
and a set intensity goes up,

02:32:05.130 --> 02:32:06.810
the amount of amplitude goes up,

02:32:06.810 --> 02:32:08.670
or inhibitory or depotentiating,

02:32:08.670 --> 02:32:12.755
it goes down with other
biological stimulation approaches.

02:32:12.755 --> 02:32:14.520
And then a third outcome,
which is important,

02:32:14.520 --> 02:32:16.140
that it's inert, it doesn't do either.

02:32:16.140 --> 02:32:19.200
So you can have stimulation
approaches that do one,

02:32:19.200 --> 02:32:21.570
you know, increase
activity, decrease activity,

02:32:21.570 --> 02:32:24.300
or are inert.

02:32:24.300 --> 02:32:26.010
And so what they found was, oh,

02:32:26.010 --> 02:32:28.020
we can excite certain brain regions.

02:32:28.020 --> 02:32:29.940
Then my mentor, Mark George said,

02:32:29.940 --> 02:32:32.437
had this kind of aha moment where he said,

02:32:32.437 --> 02:32:36.240
"Wow, there's underactivity
in prefrontal cortex

02:32:36.240 --> 02:32:39.633
in depression, and we can
increase activity using this thing

02:32:39.633 --> 02:32:42.030
that we know we can increase
activity in motor cortex.

02:32:42.030 --> 02:32:43.200
We just need to put it

02:32:43.200 --> 02:32:45.840
in the left dorsolateral
prefrontal cortex."

02:32:45.840 --> 02:32:48.720
And then they combined the two

02:32:48.720 --> 02:32:51.180
and started stimulating once a day

02:32:51.180 --> 02:32:53.580
in this kind of very abbreviated fashion.

02:32:53.580 --> 02:32:55.410
And lo and behold,

02:32:55.410 --> 02:32:58.590
some of those depression patients
resolved their depression.

02:32:58.590 --> 02:33:00.360
And back then, and still today,

02:33:00.360 --> 02:33:02.610
you can go and as a
psychiatric patient stay

02:33:02.610 --> 02:33:04.680
at the National Institute of Mental Health

02:33:04.680 --> 02:33:07.320
and go through clinical
trials to try to get treated.

02:33:07.320 --> 02:33:09.570
And there were patients
who'd been there for months

02:33:09.570 --> 02:33:11.400
and they were able to be discharged.

02:33:11.400 --> 02:33:13.140
Because their mood was better, yeah.

02:33:13.140 --> 02:33:15.570
And so just very crude approach

02:33:15.570 --> 02:33:18.687
where they were using ruler
measurements where DLPFC was,

02:33:18.687 --> 02:33:20.520
and they were stimulating with devices

02:33:20.520 --> 02:33:23.910
that you needed to physically
dunk the coil in an ice bath.

02:33:23.910 --> 02:33:25.980
And with that, they still were able to,

02:33:25.980 --> 02:33:28.034
the kind of genius of
this, Mark and others,

02:33:28.034 --> 02:33:29.575
they would still be able

02:33:29.575 --> 02:33:34.575
to create a purely engineered
stimulation approach.

02:33:34.980 --> 02:33:36.990
What's cool about that is

02:33:36.990 --> 02:33:40.200
that they kind of found two things, right?

02:33:40.200 --> 02:33:42.300
They found this one stimulation protocol

02:33:42.300 --> 02:33:44.460
that does have some antidepressant effect.

02:33:44.460 --> 02:33:46.230
It's limited, it doesn't treat everybody,

02:33:46.230 --> 02:33:48.180
it does have some antidepressant effect.

02:33:48.180 --> 02:33:52.350
And this bigger concept that
a neuromodulation device is

02:33:52.350 --> 02:33:56.610
kind of like a pharmaceutical
company for you, right?

02:33:56.610 --> 02:33:58.500
That in a given individual,

02:33:58.500 --> 02:34:02.010
a TMS device or whatever
neuromodulation device is able

02:34:02.010 --> 02:34:06.240
to generate, you can create
a stimulation approach

02:34:06.240 --> 02:34:09.477
that is specific to a given condition

02:34:09.477 --> 02:34:11.880
and specific to an individual.

02:34:11.880 --> 02:34:16.020
And so the physical layer
is just how you exert that,

02:34:16.020 --> 02:34:18.750
similarly to how we make
pharmaceutical drugs

02:34:18.750 --> 02:34:20.280
in a pharmaceutical company.

02:34:20.280 --> 02:34:22.860
But the actual therapy
itself is what you do,

02:34:22.860 --> 02:34:24.270
where you do it.

02:34:24.270 --> 02:34:26.580
And so what we learned from, you know,

02:34:26.580 --> 02:34:29.130
another 20, 30 years of this is

02:34:29.130 --> 02:34:33.300
that you can modify the
stimulation protocol in such a way

02:34:33.300 --> 02:34:35.640
where you can create a whole new treatment

02:34:35.640 --> 02:34:37.920
and put it through the same TMS device

02:34:37.920 --> 02:34:40.830
or, thank god, an evolved version of it

02:34:40.830 --> 02:34:42.450
where you don't have
to dunk it in ice baths

02:34:42.450 --> 02:34:44.670
and they can actually really handle

02:34:44.670 --> 02:34:47.550
much more aggressive
stimulation approaches.

02:34:47.550 --> 02:34:49.830
And so in 2005,

02:34:49.830 --> 02:34:52.620
a group published in "Neuron"
a paper demonstrating

02:34:52.620 --> 02:34:57.570
that if you stimulate with
the hippocampal rhythms

02:34:57.570 --> 02:34:59.460
through a TMS coil,

02:34:59.460 --> 02:35:03.690
you can excite the brain
with memory rhythms

02:35:03.690 --> 02:35:05.820
and it'll last an hour.

02:35:05.820 --> 02:35:07.590
So you can change cortical excitability

02:35:07.590 --> 02:35:09.210
in the thumb twitch for an hour,

02:35:09.210 --> 02:35:12.370
sending three minutes of excitatory

02:35:12.370 --> 02:35:15.150
or 40 seconds in the case
of inhibitory stimulation

02:35:15.150 --> 02:35:17.160
that mimics hippocampal rhythms.

02:35:17.160 --> 02:35:20.970
So much more efficient than
the original TMS approaches.

02:35:20.970 --> 02:35:23.910
And so, you know, after
that group tried to do it

02:35:23.910 --> 02:35:26.040
in this kind of six week schedule,

02:35:26.040 --> 02:35:29.130
and after that, you know, and
while they were doing that,

02:35:29.130 --> 02:35:30.930
we decided, gosh, you know,

02:35:30.930 --> 02:35:32.670
this problem I talked about
at the beginning of the show

02:35:32.670 --> 02:35:36.990
where you have this problem
that we don't have a treatment

02:35:36.990 --> 02:35:37.823
for people who are

02:35:37.823 --> 02:35:42.030
in these high acuity psychiatric
emergency states, right?

02:35:42.030 --> 02:35:44.970
This idea that we are going
to engineer a treatment

02:35:44.970 --> 02:35:49.110
where we can reorganize the
stimulation approach in time

02:35:49.110 --> 02:35:52.530
to be much more efficient
by utilizing something

02:35:52.530 --> 02:35:54.000
called space learning theory.

02:35:54.000 --> 02:35:56.070
And so you probably know about
the space learning theory.

02:35:56.070 --> 02:35:57.993
So the idea for the viewers is,

02:35:59.070 --> 02:36:00.540
it's a simple psychological thing,

02:36:00.540 --> 02:36:02.490
but we've also seen it
in hippocampal slice

02:36:02.490 --> 02:36:07.490
sort of physiology too, where
if I'm cramming for a test,

02:36:09.000 --> 02:36:11.580
what I do is I write out 60 note cards

02:36:11.580 --> 02:36:13.170
and I read each one for a minute

02:36:13.170 --> 02:36:14.667
until I get to the first
note card and again,

02:36:14.667 --> 02:36:16.890
and that's about an hour later, right?

02:36:16.890 --> 02:36:19.145
And we just intuitively do this.

02:36:19.145 --> 02:36:22.620
We all, you know, automatically do that.

02:36:22.620 --> 02:36:26.520
And we intuit that because we
know that what doesn't work is

02:36:26.520 --> 02:36:27.780
writing out one note card

02:36:27.780 --> 02:36:29.430
and looking at it over and over again.

02:36:29.430 --> 02:36:30.600
Nobody ever does that, right?

02:36:30.600 --> 02:36:33.717
You know, we've all been in
graduate school, medical school,

02:36:33.717 --> 02:36:36.060
and we have these big
stacks of note cards.

02:36:36.060 --> 02:36:37.290
That's space learning theory.

02:36:37.290 --> 02:36:39.630
It's this idea that you need
to see it about every hour

02:36:39.630 --> 02:36:42.420
to an hour and a half and
that optimizes learning.

02:36:42.420 --> 02:36:45.810
If you take the same stimulation approach

02:36:45.810 --> 02:36:46.643
that I'm talking about,

02:36:46.643 --> 02:36:47.820
this data burst stimulation approach,

02:36:47.820 --> 02:36:50.400
and you take a hippocampal
slice of a mouse

02:36:50.400 --> 02:36:55.350
and you stimulate, you
enlarge some dendritic spines

02:36:55.350 --> 02:36:57.030
and you prime some.

02:36:57.030 --> 02:36:59.370
And then if you stimulate
right after that,

02:36:59.370 --> 02:37:00.330
you don't get any change.

02:37:00.330 --> 02:37:01.770
It's called in mass stimulation.

02:37:01.770 --> 02:37:04.860
But if you wait about an
hour to an hour and a half,

02:37:04.860 --> 02:37:08.370
you get more dendritic spines
enlarged and more primed.

02:37:08.370 --> 02:37:10.320
Which, by the way, also
is what ketamine does,

02:37:10.320 --> 02:37:13.830
it causes a dendritic spine enlargement.

02:37:13.830 --> 02:37:17.400
And so, you know, what we
found was is that the old way

02:37:17.400 --> 02:37:20.280
of doing TMS, this idea of
just doing it once a day,

02:37:20.280 --> 02:37:23.040
every day, five days a week for six weeks,

02:37:23.040 --> 02:37:24.990
didn't utilize the space learning theory.

02:37:24.990 --> 02:37:28.320
It's like studying for a month or two,

02:37:28.320 --> 02:37:29.760
just a little bit once a day.

02:37:29.760 --> 02:37:31.260
Like you remember some of that stuff,

02:37:31.260 --> 02:37:33.180
but it's like not as potent as that week

02:37:33.180 --> 02:37:35.100
where you're kind of cramming, right?

02:37:35.100 --> 02:37:36.210
And what we realized is

02:37:36.210 --> 02:37:39.470
that if we could reorganize
the stimulation in time

02:37:39.470 --> 02:37:41.280
so that we took the whole six week course,

02:37:41.280 --> 02:37:43.680
we actually figured out
a way to do it in a day.

02:37:44.610 --> 02:37:46.140
And then what we also figured out is

02:37:46.140 --> 02:37:48.240
that people were underdosing TMS

02:37:48.240 --> 02:37:50.478
because if you just keep
going after six weeks

02:37:50.478 --> 02:37:52.740
out to month three, four, five,

02:37:52.740 --> 02:37:54.000
more and more people got better.

02:37:54.000 --> 02:37:55.800
So we figured out it's not just one day,

02:37:55.800 --> 02:37:57.715
we're going to give five
times the normal dose.

02:37:57.715 --> 02:38:00.270
We're going to have 7 1/2
months' worth in five days

02:38:00.270 --> 02:38:02.430
using space learning theory.

02:38:02.430 --> 02:38:03.630
- So every hour?

02:38:03.630 --> 02:38:05.160
- [Nolan] Every hour for 10 hours.

02:38:05.160 --> 02:38:05.993
- For five days.

02:38:05.993 --> 02:38:06.826
- For five days.

02:38:06.826 --> 02:38:07.860
So it's a 50 hour block.

02:38:07.860 --> 02:38:10.680
It's 90 minutes of actual stimulation,

02:38:10.680 --> 02:38:13.740
but spread out through the day
in the same way of learning.

02:38:13.740 --> 02:38:16.110
Which is perfect for an inpatient
psychiatric unit, right?

02:38:16.110 --> 02:38:17.340
- [Andrew] Five days is manageable.

02:38:17.340 --> 02:38:18.900
- Yeah, you can get stimulation.

02:38:18.900 --> 02:38:21.333
Nobody's ever dropped out by the schedule.

02:38:22.457 --> 02:38:25.500
You know, folks that want
to do this, want to do it.

02:38:25.500 --> 02:38:26.460
So they'll do their nine minutes,

02:38:26.460 --> 02:38:28.440
they'll go get breakfast,
they'll do their nine minutes,

02:38:28.440 --> 02:38:31.110
they'll go see their
therapist or whatever it is.

02:38:31.110 --> 02:38:34.680
And so what we found
with this reorganization

02:38:34.680 --> 02:38:38.010
and time of the stimulation dose,

02:38:38.010 --> 02:38:39.540
and then the third component is

02:38:39.540 --> 02:38:42.000
we do resting state
functional connectivity scans

02:38:42.000 --> 02:38:42.833
on everybody.

02:38:42.833 --> 02:38:45.240
And we have ways now in
the last five to 10 years

02:38:45.240 --> 02:38:50.240
of picking out that specific
subgenual DLPFC subcircuit

02:38:50.310 --> 02:38:52.770
that I was talking about
earlier, that cingulate DLPFC,

02:38:52.770 --> 02:38:54.240
we can pick that out in every single one.

02:38:54.240 --> 02:38:55.200
If you want to come to the lab,

02:38:55.200 --> 02:38:57.900
we can find your DLPFC subgenual.

02:38:57.900 --> 02:38:59.370
It's even more robust than non-

02:38:59.370 --> 02:39:00.930
- Maybe we could stimulate
too, just while we're in there.

02:39:00.930 --> 02:39:01.763
- Yeah, if you want to,

02:39:01.763 --> 02:39:03.723
we can move around your hypnotizability.

02:39:04.830 --> 02:39:09.830
And we can find that spot in each person.

02:39:09.990 --> 02:39:11.730
Instead of finding the
same spot on the skull,

02:39:11.730 --> 02:39:14.280
we find the same spot on the
brain, and we can stimulate.

02:39:14.280 --> 02:39:15.540
And we do that every hour on the hour.

02:39:15.540 --> 02:39:20.460
And what we've found is that folks will,

02:39:20.460 --> 02:39:24.360
within one to five days, you
know, in more cases than not,

02:39:24.360 --> 02:39:26.460
and depending upon if you're
looking at this open label

02:39:26.460 --> 02:39:30.420
or in trials, somewhere
between 60 and 90% of the time,

02:39:30.420 --> 02:39:32.610
they will go into full on remission

02:39:32.610 --> 02:39:36.270
in the sense they're totally
normal from a mood standpoint

02:39:36.270 --> 02:39:37.413
at the end of this.

02:39:38.550 --> 02:39:41.010
And like I said, with variable durability.

02:39:41.010 --> 02:39:42.450
So that's the part we
have to figure out now

02:39:42.450 --> 02:39:44.280
about dosing and how to keep people well.

02:39:44.280 --> 02:39:45.300
But for some people, you know,

02:39:45.300 --> 02:39:47.010
we've had four years of remission,

02:39:47.010 --> 02:39:48.690
you know, year of remission.

02:39:48.690 --> 02:39:51.660
And it's really that cramming of the test.

02:39:51.660 --> 02:39:55.470
It's really that idea that
you're laying in that information

02:39:55.470 --> 02:39:57.450
to the exact right spot.

02:39:57.450 --> 02:40:00.240
And the signal is a simple
signal, but it's a profound one,

02:40:00.240 --> 02:40:05.240
which is turn on, stay
on, remember to stay on.

02:40:05.880 --> 02:40:09.000
You know, that idea that you're
sending this memory signal

02:40:09.000 --> 02:40:10.800
into the brain and you're
doing it in such a way

02:40:10.800 --> 02:40:12.660
that you're telling the system,

02:40:12.660 --> 02:40:16.407
you're kind of taking it out
of your own hippocampus' hand

02:40:16.407 --> 02:40:18.060
and you're sending the same signal

02:40:18.060 --> 02:40:20.340
the hippocampus normally signals out.

02:40:20.340 --> 02:40:24.150
Now you're sending that signal
into the prefrontal cortex

02:40:24.150 --> 02:40:28.950
and kind of utilizing the
brain's own communication style

02:40:28.950 --> 02:40:31.380
to get it to get out of the state.

02:40:31.380 --> 02:40:35.430
And what's very cool
about this is that people,

02:40:35.430 --> 02:40:37.860
when they kind of exit out of that,

02:40:37.860 --> 02:40:42.860
they end up saying they don't
have any side effects from it.

02:40:43.710 --> 02:40:46.080
And they feel back to normal.

02:40:46.080 --> 02:40:47.280
Like some people, you know,

02:40:47.280 --> 02:40:48.783
not everybody, but there's
a subsection of people

02:40:48.783 --> 02:40:52.710
that with SSRIs where they'll
say, I kind of feel numb,

02:40:52.710 --> 02:40:55.650
or I have GI side effects,
or I can't, you know,

02:40:55.650 --> 02:40:57.510
I don't have the sexual
interest that I used to have

02:40:57.510 --> 02:40:58.950
and that sort of thing.

02:40:58.950 --> 02:41:00.960
You know, not anything against SSRIs,

02:41:00.960 --> 02:41:02.700
as I said earlier, life saving, you know,

02:41:02.700 --> 02:41:05.220
for a subsection of people,
these things really work.

02:41:05.220 --> 02:41:07.290
But with this, what you see is

02:41:07.290 --> 02:41:09.930
that people don't talk
about any of that stuff.

02:41:09.930 --> 02:41:11.700
And I think it's likely

02:41:11.700 --> 02:41:14.850
because you're tapping
into that core circuitry

02:41:14.850 --> 02:41:18.090
and you're reversing it and
you're doing it with a magnet

02:41:18.090 --> 02:41:21.360
that, because it's a very
profound electromagnet,

02:41:21.360 --> 02:41:24.000
it's the same field
strength as an MRI scanner,

02:41:24.000 --> 02:41:26.910
it's able to induce a current in the brain

02:41:26.910 --> 02:41:29.640
in this focal targeted way

02:41:29.640 --> 02:41:31.140
without getting into
the rest of the brain,

02:41:31.140 --> 02:41:32.910
without getting into the
rest of the body at all.

02:41:32.910 --> 02:41:36.060
And just really kind of
acting only on that circuitry

02:41:36.060 --> 02:41:37.500
that's involved.

